msgctxt "0x77/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x77/1"
msgid ""
"\"I bid you welcome to your new province.  I may call upon you from time "
"to time, to provide both vital resources and Denarii for our beloved "
"Senate.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1387
msgctxt "0x79/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1387
msgctxt "0x79/1"
msgid ""
"\"The Senate worries that you are not pulling your weight.  We have "
"decided to increase your annual tribute as indicated below.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1386
msgctxt "0x7a/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1386
msgctxt "0x7a/1"
msgid ""
"\"Your poor performance offends us.  We expect a higher tribute to make "
"up for our annoyance.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1382
msgctxt "0x7b/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1382
msgctxt "0x7b/1"
msgid ""
"\"The Senate is cautiously optimistic about your performance, so we have "
"decided to reduce your tribute accordingly.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1381
msgctxt "0x7c/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1381
msgctxt "0x7c/1"
msgid ""
"\"We are extremely pleased with your continuing loyalty to the state. We "
"hope your new reduced tribute pleases you.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7d/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7d/1"
msgid ""
"\"Normally, bribes are worth more than the pleb who delivers them.  You "
"insult me with such paltry sums!\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7e/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7e/1"
msgid ""
"\"Thanks for the kind gift.  I shall be sure to use it to purchase a "
"grape at our next festival.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7f/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x7f/1"
msgid "\"Your gift has been received.  Thank you.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x80/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x80/1"
msgid "\"Your thoughtfulness is much appreciated.  Long life.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x81/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x81/1"
msgid ""
"\"I so enjoy these correspondences.  My gratitude for your favors to the "
"Empire.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x82/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x82/1"
msgid ""
"\"Your generosity touches me.  Rome needs more leaders like yourself.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x83/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x83/1"
msgid ""
"\"Gifts such as these are more than I could hope for.  The Empire thanks "
"you!\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x84/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x84/1"
msgid "\"Never have I been so flattered!  The Empire salutes you!\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x85/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x86/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1355
msgctxt "0x87/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1355
msgctxt "0x87/1"
msgid "\"The Empire has need of the bounties your land may provide.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1356
msgctxt "0x88/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1356
msgctxt "0x88/1"
msgid ""
"\"Perhaps my messenger was killed en route -- where are the supplies I "
"requested?\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1357
msgctxt "0x89/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1357
msgctxt "0x89/1"
msgid ""
"\"Enough of your excuses -- For the good of the Empire, I demand you "
"provide us with the supplies we requested!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1359
msgctxt "0x8a/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1359
msgctxt "0x8a/1"
msgid ""
"\"Your Insolence outrages me -- send me my goods now or I'll have your "
"head!\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x8b/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1385
msgctxt "0x8c/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1385
msgctxt "0x8c/1"
msgid ""
"\"Seeing how wisely you have managed your province, surely you can "
"afford a greater tribute to the Senate.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1384
msgctxt "0x8d/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1384
msgctxt "0x8d/1"
msgid ""
"\"Your temples overflow with gold.  Surely it would be more safely kept "
"with the Senate at Rome?\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1380
msgctxt "0x8e/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1380
msgctxt "0x8e/1"
msgid ""
"\"Your city is low on funds.  The Senate will help you through these "
"difficult times by lowering your tribute.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1379
msgctxt "0x8f/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1379
msgctxt "0x8f/1"
msgid ""
"\"The Senate has grudgingly decreed a lowering of your tribute.  Please "
"manage your accounts better in the future.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1411
msgctxt "0x90/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1411
msgctxt "0x90/1"
msgid ""
"\"For this year's Imperial tax, I have decided to be lenient on you.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1406
msgctxt "0x91/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1406
msgctxt "0x91/1"
msgid ""
"\"I hope this year's Imperial tax is to your liking.  It certainly is to "
"mine!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1401
msgctxt "0x92/0"
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: src/formulae.c:1401
msgctxt "0x92/1"
msgid ""
"\"This year's Imperial tax befits a Governor as successful -- and "
"wealthy -- as yourself.\""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x92/2"
msgid ""
msgstr ""

msgctxt "0x92/3"
msgid ""
msgstr ""

#. help page 1: title
msgctxt "help/1/0"
msgid "On-Line Help"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1/1"
msgid ""
"Select a category from the list below to view its on-line help "
"Listings. You may also access on-line help at any time as you play by "
"right-clicking on any item in the game, or by using the Query function.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#59City Level#\n"
"#56Province Level#\n"
"#4The Forum#\n"
"#5Combat#\n"
"#92Icons#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 2: title
msgctxt "help/2/0"
msgid "General Strategies"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/2/1"
msgid ""
"Start your city close to the river; it will be cheaper to supply with "
"water, and to defend with walls (as a river without bridges cannot be "
"crossed by enemies).\n"
"\n"
"Try to arrange housing blocks and the structures that they need so that "
"multiple blocks can take advantage of the same structures.\n"
"\n"
"Don't expand too quickly; you have a limited treasury at the start of "
"the city, and you should concentrate on spending it to build housing "
"and population that can generate MORE Denarii.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#36Next Page#\n"
"#120Item Strategies#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 3: title
msgctxt "help/3/0"
msgid "On-Line History"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/3/1"
msgid ""
"Select a category from the list below to view its on-line history.\n"
"\n"
"#1200Ave Imperator! The Creation of the Roman Empire#\n"
"#1201 Introduction#\n"
"#1800 Chapter I: Rome Before the Empire#\n"
"#1240 Chapter II: Julius Caesar#\n"
"#1260 Chapter III: Heir to Caesar #\n"
"#1290 Chapter IV: The Julio-Claudian Emperors #\n"
"#1325 Chapter V: The Flavian Emperors#\n"
"#1345 Chapter VI: The Antonine Emperors #\n"
"#1370 Chapter VII: The Long Decline#\n"
"#1390 Conclusion#\n"
"#1400 Bibliography#\n"
"\n"
"#110 Appendix: Item Histories#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 4: title
msgctxt "help/4/0"
msgid "Help for the Forum"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/4/1"
msgid ""
"The Forum serves as your main government headquarters. In the Forum, "
"you may do the following things:\n"
"- Monitor your Empire, Peace, Prosperity, and Culture ratings.\n"
"- Communicate with the Emperor.\n"
"- Check your industrial performance.\n"
"- Set taxes.\n"
"- Manage your Plebeian work force.\n"
"- Manage your Legion.\n"
"- Keep an eye on many other aspects of city management. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 5: title
msgctxt "help/5/0"
msgid "Help for Combat"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/5/1"
msgid ""
"In order to do battle, you must first have built at least one fort on "
"the Province Level. You must also have formed a Legion through your "
"Centurion in the Forum.\n"
"\n"
"#52Your Legion#\n"
"#51Battle#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 6: title
msgctxt "help/6/0"
msgid "Forum (continued)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/6/1"
msgid ""
"To enter the Forum from either the City or Province Level, click on the "
"Go To Forum button on the Control Panel. Select any of the following "
"items and advisors in the Forum by clicking directly on them to "
"consult, receive advice and information, or issue orders.\n"
"#7The Oracle#\n"
"#9The Scribe#\n"
"#10The Merchant#\n"
"#11The Centurion#\n"
"#12The Advisor from Rome#\n"
"#15The Plebeian Tribune#\n"
"#16The Treasurer#\n"
"#17The Personal Advisor#\n"
"#18The Empire Map#\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#4Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 7: title
msgctxt "help/7/0"
msgid "The Oracle"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/7/1"
msgid ""
"The columns in the Oracle show you your current status in the game's "
"four ratings: Empire, Peace, Prosperity, and Culture. In order to "
"receive a promotion in a full game, you will need to achieve a minimum "
"rating in each category and a minimum combined average rating. Click "
"directly on each category name for information and advice.\n"
"\n"
"The Empire Rating, in a full game, measures how well you have extended "
"your influence on the Province Level. It considers things like your "
"provincial road network, the size of the towns in your Province, and "
"your trade and commerce. In a City Building Only game, this rating does "
"not apply.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #20Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 8: title
msgctxt "help/8/0"
msgid "The Forum (continued)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/8/1"
msgid ""
"The Prosperity Rating is an evaluation of your financial management. A "
"large population and a thriving economy will bring in a healthy level "
"of industrial and population taxes, which will boost the Prosperity "
"Rating. Do what you can to stay out of debt, spend wisely, and stay "
"profitable.\n"
"\n"
"The Culture Rating measures your city's cultural richness and "
"diversity. An abundance of Schools, Libraries, and Hospitals will boost "
"this rating, as will Gardens, Plazas, and Religious and Entertainment "
"buildings. A large population will also help your Culture rating.\n"
"\n"
"#7Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 9: title
msgctxt "help/9/0"
msgid "The Scribe"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/9/1"
msgid ""
"Consult the Scribe if you would like to take a look at his collection "
"of graphs illustrating City Population, City Funds, Population Tax "
"Income, and Industrial Tax Income over the past several years. Click on "
"the right and left arrows to adjust the time span shown in the graphs.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 10: title
msgctxt "help/10/0"
msgid "The Merchant"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/10/1"
msgid ""
"Select the Merchant to access a list of several available industrial "
"commodities and the status of each in your Province. The first column "
"shows the number of units of each item produced in your Province.\n"
"\n"
"Note that the bottom four listings refer to commodities available "
"outside of your province, beyond each of the four sides of the Province "
"map. A ship icon in the first column indicates that this item is "
"available via overseas trade, and requires a Port. A caravan icon "
"indicates that the item is available via overland trade route, and "
"requires a Trading Post. Building the appropriate structure on the "
"appropriate side of the Province map will bring these commodities into "
"your Province.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #19Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 11: title
msgctxt "help/11/0"
msgid "The Centurion"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/11/1"
msgid ""
"Your Centurion is the highest-ranking general of your armed forces, or "
"Legion. Your Legion is composed of four different varieties of "
"fighters, and is split up evenly into Cohorts equal to the number of "
"Forts you have built on the Province Level.\n"
"\n"
"Heavy Infantry are your best-trained and best-armed professional "
"soldiers. Since they are career fighters, you will increase their ranks "
"in your Legion by raising monthly wages. When you raise wages, more "
"Heavy Infantrymen will join up, but they will not be available "
"immediately. They require the longest training period before they are "
"battle-ready.\n"
"\n"
"Light Infantry are your lesser soldiers. These are not professional "
"soldiers, but rather they are ordinary\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #21Next Page# \n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 12: title
msgctxt "help/12/0"
msgid "Advisor from Rome"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/12/1"
msgid ""
"This is the Imperial Representative to your city. He serves as a link "
"from you to Rome and the Emperor. He will keep you abreast of just how "
"the Emperor feels about you and your performance. Click on the Imperial "
"Advisor to see your current Annual Tribute. The Annual Tribute is the "
"amount of Denarii that you must send to the Emperor each year. It will "
"rise if his confidence in you falls, and will fall if  Imperial favor "
"rises. If you are careless with your Denarii, you may not have enough "
"to pay the Annual Tribute. Should this happen, the Emperor will not be "
"pleased.\n"
"\n"
"The Imperial Advisor will also let you know what, if anything, the "
"Emperor is currently requesting from you. Rome will request goods from "
"you occasionally as needs arise.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #24Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 13: title
msgctxt "help/13/0"
msgid "Forum Screen Tips"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/13/1"
msgid ""
"Check your ratings with the Oracle REGULARLY.  If your career is "
"important to you, remember that fulfilling the ratings is more "
"important than just making money or expanding the size of the city "
"(although one ofter results from the other).  If you don't check the "
"ratings regularly -- particularly in early provinces -- you may find "
"yourself expanding your city in ways that only improve the ratings you "
"have already fulfilled.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #32Next Page#\n"
"#4Help#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 14: title
msgctxt "help/14/0"
msgid "Battle Tips"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/14/1"
msgid ""
"Set your troops' formations and positions at the start of a battle, to "
"gain a tactical advantage over the enemy.\n"
"\n"
"Use the right formation for the right task.  Change formations when "
"attempting to flank your enemy.  Use the tortoise formation to approach "
"missile-firing troops.\n"
"\n"
"Keep your men in their formations until you are sure you have the upper "
"hand.  When in pursuit formation, your men are less effective in combat "
"and more vulnerable to counterattack.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#5Help#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 15: title
msgctxt "help/15/0"
msgid "The Plebeian Tribune"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/15/1"
msgid ""
"The Plebs are the working underclass of your city. It is their lot to "
"tend to the city's infrastructure. Plebs do not live in your housing, "
"and are not considered part of your population. You may adjust the size "
"of your Pleb work force by adjusting the amount of Denarii you allocate "
"to Pleb Welfare.\n"
"\n"
"Click on the Plebeian Tribune to access the Pleb Management panel. Use "
"the up and Down arrows to set your Pleb Welfare allocation, paying "
"close attention to the Clerk's estimate of how many Plebs the "
"adjustment will yield. Keep in mind, though, that this is always a "
"short-term estimate. More Plebs may come in later months. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #28Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 16: title
msgctxt "help/16/0"
msgid "The Treasurer"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/16/1"
msgid ""
"The Treasurer's Report lists your city's income (marked with a \"+\") and "
"expenditures (marked with a \"-\") for the current year and estimates the "
"figures for the coming year. Income comes in the form of Population and "
"Industrial taxes. Expenditures include Construction and Operating Costs "
"(Army wages and Pleb Welfare expenses). The Annual Tribute is paid "
"automatically each year to the Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"Use the up and down arrows to set population and industrial taxes. "
"Taxes are expressed as a percentage of citizen and business income.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #30Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 17: title
msgctxt "help/17/0"
msgid "Personal Advisor"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/17/1"
msgid ""
"Your Personal Advisor allows you to set your own monthly salary. It may "
"be wise to give yourself a significant salary, and not just out of "
"greed. If you find your city desperate for funds, you can always grant "
"a donation out of your own pocket if you have the Denarii. To do so, "
"just select Donate to the City, set your amount, and click on Make this "
"Donation. In addition, any savings you accumulate over the years will "
"stay with you if you are promoted to another province. It is nice to be "
"able to ride into a new city with a large chest of Denarii.\n"
"\n"
"Your personal savings may also be used for \"gifts\" to the Emperor. It "
"will also be taxed at varying rates -- if you have lots of personal "
"wealth, the Emperor may try to cut in by imposing a large tax rate!\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 18: title
msgctxt "help/18/0"
msgid "Empire Map"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/18/1"
msgid ""
"Select the map at the back of the room in the Forum for a view of the "
"Roman Empire. In addition to the provinces that you govern, the Empire "
"will expand on its own through the efforts of the Emperor and other "
"Roman agents such as the General, Pompous Maximus. +\n"
"\n"
"Pompous Maximus marches into new provinces with his army, and seizes "
"them for the Roman Empire. The provinces he conquers will not be "
"available to you. Each time you receive a promotion, you must choose "
"from among the provinces along the Empire's borders that have not yet "
"been taken by Pompous Maximus.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#6Previous Page# / #31Next Page# \n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 19: title
msgctxt "help/19/0"
msgid "The Merchant (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/19/1"
msgid ""
"The second column shows how many units of each commodity you have in "
"storage. If you have a lot of stored goods, you might consider opening "
"another business--if your population will support it --to process and "
"sell the excess. You will want to keep some goods in storage--the "
"Emperor will request things from time to time, and if you fail to "
"deliver he will be quite angry.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#10Previous Page# / #33Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 20: title
msgctxt "help/20/0"
msgid "The Oracle (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/20/1"
msgid ""
"The Peace Rating gauges the safety and security of your population. You "
"must have adequate Cohorts to stop any invasions on the Province Level "
"before they reach your city. On the City Level, you must have adequate "
"security in the form of Barracks, Praefectures, Walls, and Towers. "
"Also, the larger your population, the higher your Peace Rating can "
"climb.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#7Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips# "
msgstr ""

#. help page 21: title
msgctxt "help/21/0"
msgid "The Centurion (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/21/1"
msgid ""
"residents who have been conscripted into the Legion. You may raise more "
"Light Infantrymen by raising your city's conscription percentage. Be "
"careful not to raise conscription too high, though. Too much of it can "
"provoke unrest among your populace. Light Infantry also require a "
"training period. The Centurion panel will tell you how many have "
"completed training and are ready to fight. \n"
"\n"
"Slingers are Plebs who have been assigned to Army duty through the "
"Plebeian Tribune and trained quickly to hurl rocks with a sling. You "
"may increase or decrease their ranks through the Plebeian Tribune, but "
"they require a brief training period. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#11Previous Page# / #22Next Page# \n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 22: title
msgctxt "help/22/0"
msgid "The Centurion (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/22/1"
msgid ""
"Auxiliaries are mercenaries from your Province who will fight for "
"money. They may be of a variety of fighting types. including cavalry, "
"archers, etc. The number, type, and quality of auxiliary soldiers vary "
"from province to province, as will their wages. If you can afford them, "
"they require no training. As soon as you hire them, they are ready to "
"fight for you. You have a limited number of auxiliaries per province, "
"so use them, wisely. \n"
"\n"
"Select the Centurion to access the Legion Management Panel. Here you "
"may set monthly wages and conscription rates, and hire or release "
"Auxiliaries.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#21Previous Page# / #23Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 23: title
msgctxt "help/23/0"
msgid "The Centurion (page 4)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/23/1"
msgid ""
"On the right side of the panel is a list of each type of soldier you "
"have ready and in training, and a description of your troops' morale "
"and battle-readiness. Once troops become trained, they will be added to "
"existing Cohorts that have been ordered to Return to Fort, or have "
"moved over their fort, within the past few months. \n"
"\n"
"The right and left arrows next to the \"Legion Has\" listing allows you "
"to view each Cohort individually. Each Cohort display will show the "
"number and type of soldiers the Cohort has, the morale of its troops, "
"its combat readiness, and its recruitment rate (Minor, Major, or "
"Normal).\n"
"\n"
"While the Normal setting is activated, the Cohort will receive an equal "
"number of new recruits in \n"
"#22Previous Page# / #153Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 24: title
msgctxt "help/24/0"
msgid "Advisor from Rome (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/24/1"
msgid ""
"To try to butter up the Emperor, you will always have the option to "
"send him an \"extra tribute\" (the cynical might call it a bribe). Just "
"click on the button, set the amount you'd like to send, and then select "
"\"Send This Gift.\" A gift will come out of your personal savings. Of "
"course, you can never be sure if such a gesture will please or anger "
"the Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"Your relationship with the Emperor may vary throughout your game, and "
"will manifest itself in several different forms:\n"
"\n"
"Imperial Favor:  This rating is a general measure of your relationship "
"with the Emperor. It considers such factors as how well and how quickly "
"you fulfill his requests, and how much money you send in the form of  "
"\"gifts.\"\n"
"\n"
"#12Previous Page# / #25Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 25: title
msgctxt "help/25/0"
msgid "Advisor from Rome (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/25/1"
msgid ""
"Annual Tribute: The Annual Tribute is a fee that you pay to the Emperor "
"each year, just for the privilege of being Roman. The Emperor "
"determines the amount of the Tribute based on the profitability of your "
"city, the total amount in your treasury (the more you have, the higher "
"the tribute), and Imperial Favor. If the Emperor likes you, he will "
"lower your Tribute. If you annoy him, he will surely raise it.\n"
"\n"
"Requests: The Emperor may occasionally request raw materials from you. "
"When this happens, the best thing you can do is send him what he asks "
"for -- any way you can. This may mean building a port several months "
"before you had planned to in order to get the Emperor some sand. If you "
"fail to give the Emperor what he requests, he may banish you (and end "
"your game!).\n"
"\n"
"#24Previous Page# / #26Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 26: title
msgctxt "help/26/0"
msgid "Advisor from Rome (page 4)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/26/1"
msgid ""
"If you know that it will be a while before you can grant a request, a "
"generous gift may delay the emperor's wrath. But you will still need to "
"fulfill the request, the sooner the better! The lower your Imperial "
"Favor is, the less patient the Emperor will be while waiting for a "
"request.\n"
"\n"
"\"Gifts\":  Gifts come out of your personal savings. They work in a "
"strange way: on the one hand, a gift (or bribe) can boost your Imperial "
"Favor (if it is high enough); but on the other hand, once you begin "
"sending gifts the Emperor will expect you to continue. He might be "
"perturbed if you stop!\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#25Previous Page# / #27Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 27: title
msgctxt "help/27/0"
msgid "Advisor from Rome (page 5)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/27/1"
msgid ""
"If your gift is not high enough, the Emperor will be insulted, and the "
"gesture will have a negative effect. How do you know how much to send? "
"You really donÆt, but you can anticipate his reaction. In evaluating "
"your gift, the Emperor will compare it to the amount of your Annual "
"Tribute, your Annual Salary, and any gifts you may have sent in the "
"past.\n"
"\n"
"Imperial Tax: As if the Emperor were not enough of a thorn in your "
"side, he also has the power to tax your personal savings. The tax rate "
"he sets depends on your Imperial Favor, and on the size of your "
"personal savings.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#26Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 28: title
msgctxt "help/28/0"
msgid "Plebeian Tribune (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/28/1"
msgid ""
"Then assign your labor force using the up and down arrows next to each "
"task. The column on the right side of the panel shows the estimated "
"number of Plebs needed for each assignment. Click directly on the word "
"\"Need\" to set the recommended amount. You should consider throwing a "
"few extra workers in any problem areas, or to cover future expansion "
"projects. If any Pleb force drops below its recommended level, you will "
"receive a warning message on your menu bar.\n"
"\n"
"Note: When you raise the number of Plebs assigned to a task by more "
"than the number of available Plebs, Plebs will be taken from the ranks "
"of the next listed task. When that one is exhausted, Plebs will be "
"taken from the next one down, and so on, before beginning at the top of "
"the list. \n"
"\n"
"#15Previous Page# / #29Next Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 29: title
msgctxt "help/29/0"
msgid "Plebeian Tribune (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/29/1"
msgid ""
"Keep a close watch on your labor force as the game progresses. If you "
"mismanage your work force, roads may deteriorate, fountains may break "
"down, and fires may break out. As you build structures on the Province "
"and City Levels, consider the ways in which your changes will affect "
"the work force. Plan ahead: a new road extension will require more road "
"workers, new farms or mines will call for more provincial workers, and "
"so forth. \n"
"\n"
"Plebs assigned to Army Duty are trained as Slingers. They provide a "
"fast and cheap way to bolster your Legion.\n"
"\n"
"Note:  If your Pleb Welfare allocation is too low, your work force may "
"dwindle to dangerous levels, forcing cuts in city services!\n"
"\n"
"#28Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 30: title
msgctxt "help/30/0"
msgid "The Treasurer (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/30/1"
msgid ""
"The Average figure next to each listing shows the average tax bill for "
"the people and businesses of the city. Citizen income is based on the "
"number and quality of houses in your city -- higher quality housing "
"generates more income. Business income is represented by the jar icons "
"shown next to each business in your city.\n"
"\n"
"High population taxes can bring in lots of Denarii, but if the tax rate "
"climbs too high, your city may be slow to develop, and people may pack "
"up and leave. Similarly, excessively high industrial taxes may "
"discourage businesses from growing, and ultimately reduce your tax "
"income.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#16Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 31: title
msgctxt "help/31/0"
msgid "Empire Map (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/31/1"
msgid ""
"On the Empire Map, gray areas indicate provinces that are already under "
"Roman rule. The remaining areas are waiting to be conquered. Your "
"current Province, your past Province(s), and any Provinces captured by "
"Pompous Maximus, will be indicated on the Empire Map.\n"
"\n"
"Click on the provinces near Rome to see a brief profile detailing "
"each's military strength, attitude toward Rome, neighbors, and "
"industrial potential. All these factors should be considered when you "
"earn a promotion and must choose a new province to govern.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#18Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 32: title
msgctxt "help/32/0"
msgid "Forum Tips (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/32/1"
msgid ""
"Add extra plebs to tasks that cover areas of the city you expect to "
"expand; for example, increase the plebs assigned to water before you "
"add to your water network.\n"
"\n"
"Check the treasurer to see if you expect to turning a profit each year. "
" If you're losing money and trying to stay out of the red, try lowering "
"your operating costs: decrease your salary, don't pay for extra plebs, "
"or reduce the size of your legion.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#13Previous Page#\n"
"#4Help#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 33: title
msgctxt "help/33/0"
msgid "Merchant (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/33/1"
msgid ""
"The final column shows the number of factories, or businesses, in your "
"city that are currently working on the commodity, and the percentage of "
"the production capacity that is being met. If you have factories with "
"low percentages, they may not be receiving enough raw materials from "
"provincial sources. Consider building new sources or stepping up trade "
"to maximize efficiency. Ideally, you will want to keep storage fairly "
"low, and supply percentages at 100 percent.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#19Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 34: title
msgctxt "help/34/0"
msgid "Battle (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/34/1"
msgid ""
"You will give orders to units of soldiers within your Cohort. At the "
"start of the battle, each unit will be separated slightly from the "
"others on the battlefield, and so will be easy to distinguish. To issue "
"orders, you must first select a unit or units to command. To select a "
"unit, just click on the top half of the unit(s). To select more than "
"one unit at a time, click and hold your mouse button down, and drag it "
"over the units you would like to select. Selected units will be "
"highlighted. To deselect units, just click on any empty space on the "
"battlefield. To select all of your troops easily, you may use the \"All\" "
"button on the control panel.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#5Previous Page# / #35Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 35: title
msgctxt "help/35/0"
msgid "Battle (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/35/1"
msgid ""
"A window in the lower right corner of the screen will display "
"information about the selected unit(s): types of soldiers, numbers, and "
"morale. Morale is expressed as number between 0 (low) and 100 (high). "
"When unit's morale hits 0, it is routed, and will not fight.\n"
"\n"
"(Note:  You can also select enemy units to view their stats in this "
"box, but you cannot select both your units and the enemy's at the same "
"time.)\n"
"\n"
"Any positions or formations you set before the action starts will "
"happen instantly, but you may only move your troops around on your side "
"of the battlefield. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#34Previous Page# / #48Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 36: title
msgctxt "help/36/0"
msgid "Strategies (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/36/1"
msgid ""
"Don't try to always cram buildings into the smallest space possible.  "
"Leave room for the larger forums, more expensive forms of "
"entertainment, and other structures that you can build later in the "
"game.  In the meantine, you can fill these spaces with gardens to "
"increase land values. \n"
"\n"
"Strive for an even balance of successful housing and income.  You can "
"get a fair amount of money from either of them, but high ratings "
"require you to have a good mix of both.\n"
"\n"
"Invaders and Raiders always come at a province from its neighboring "
"regions, which can be either provinces or distant lands outside the "
"Empire.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#2Previous Page# / #37Next Page#\n"
"#120Item Strategies#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 37: title
msgctxt "help/37/0"
msgid "Strategies (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/37/1"
msgid ""
"If you know of a province that is likely to suffer from raids and "
"invasions from many sides, try conquering its neighbors BEFORE taking "
"it on.\n"
"\n"
"Try not to send \"gifts\" to the Emperor unless you are in an emergency "
"situation (such as needing to delay his requests for goods, because you "
"don't have them yet), or unless you have plenty of savings.  Once you "
"start bribing the Emperor, he will expect you to keep it up.\n"
"\n"
"When you're doing well, be sure to increase your salary.  That way, "
"you'll be able to use the profits of one province to help fund the "
"beginnings of another one.  When playing at the harder difficulty "
"levels, this will be a key to your success.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#36Previous Page#\n"
"#120Item Strategies#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 38: title
msgctxt "help/38/0"
msgid "Roads (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/38/1"
msgid ""
"While many buildings need roads, some do not. Bath Houses, "
"Entertainment structures, Temples, and Schools are designed to be "
"accessed on foot, and therefore do not need road access. \n"
"\n"
"Roads do not evolve, they remain the same as long as they are "
"adequately maintained. If you find that sections of your roads are "
"disappearing spontaneously, check to make sure you have an adequate "
"number of Plebs assigned to road duty. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#310Previous Page# / #119Next Page#\n"
"#311Tips# / #312History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 39: title
msgctxt "help/39/0"
msgid "Forum (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/39/1"
msgid ""
"A forum also has a square radius in which tax coverage is high "
"regardless of the clerks. If you do not have adequate forum coverage, "
"taxes may go uncollected, and your city will suffer.\n"
"\n"
"There are three different classes of forums: Aventine, Janiculan, and "
"Palatine. The more advanced classes occupy larger map areas and provide "
"tax coverage for more territory, but they will be available only after "
"your city advances a bit and your engineers develop their skills. \n"
"\n"
"Any class of forum will grow visibly fancier if its land value rises, "
"but it will always remain the same size.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#280Previous Page# \n"
"#281Tips# / #282History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 40: title
msgctxt "help/40/0"
msgid "Reservoirs (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/40/1"
msgid ""
"Houses built outside the water supply radius (even if they are within "
"the pipe access radius) will not have water, and will have limited "
"development.\n"
"\n"
"In the latter instance, you can make the houses happy with the "
"construction of a Fountain.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#355Previous Page#\n"
"#356Tips# / #357History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 41: title
msgctxt "help/41/0"
msgid "Businesses (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/41/1"
msgid ""
"The output of a business is represented by small jars in the \"loading "
"area\" of the business building: the more jars, the more successful the "
"business. The maximum output of a business is seven jars.\n"
"\n"
"When you play a full game, you must tend to industry on the Province "
"Level to feed your businesses. When you play City Building Only, you "
"will automatically receive raw materials in various quantities from "
"your Province.  When you select the Industry icon on the City Level, "
"businesses that are currently not supplied from the province are listed "
"in red.\n"
"\n"
"Business pay Industrial Taxes, which are an important source of "
"municipal income.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#625Previous Page#\n"
"#626Tips# / #657History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 42: title
msgctxt "help/42/0"
msgid "Worship (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/42/1"
msgid ""
"Religious buildings develop based on land values, and on the size of "
"the city's population. Placing them is a good way boost land values, "
"and to raise your city's Culture rating. Religious buildings serve a "
"surrounding radius, and do not need road access.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#235Previous Page# \n"
"#236Tips# / #237History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 43: title
msgctxt "help/43/0"
msgid "Capital City (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/43/1"
msgid ""
"You must also consider proctecting your city on the Province Level by "
"building Forts and Walls, and by ordering your Cohorts. By all means, "
"stop invading enemies before they can reach your city!\n"
"\n"
"Managing your realm on both the Province and City Level will be a "
"challenge. But success is the key to advancing your Imperial career, "
"and may ultimately be a matter of life and death -- yours, that is.\n"
"\n"
"#760Previous Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 44: title
msgctxt "help/44/0"
msgid "Housing Tips (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/44/1"
msgid ""
"To make housing grow, make sure that you not only provide the "
"structures it needs, but also raise its land value to a high enough "
"level.  If a house needs a higher land value to grow, and you have "
"provided for all of its other needs, try building Gardens, Plazas or "
"Temples near it. Sometimes, you might need to \"sacrifice\" some houses "
"in a block so that you can get a useful structure close to the rest of "
"the housing.\n"
"\n"
"Finally, make sure you QUERY your housing regularly (by right-clicking "
"on it) to see what it needs.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#146Previous Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #147History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 45: title
msgctxt "help/45/0"
msgid "Tips for Roads (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/45/1"
msgid ""
"If you want to better control where your walkers can and cannot go, try "
"separating your road network into different, disconnected sections. To "
"keep structures that limit land value away from your housing, try "
"building most of them along a single, straight-line \"spur\" road that "
"connects to the roads surrounding the houses.\n"
"\n"
"Don't build bridges across the river unless you intend to surround your "
"city with walls on BOTH sides of the river.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#311Previous Page#\n"
"#310Help# / #312History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 46: title
msgctxt "help/46/0"
msgid "Color Overlays (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/46/1"
msgid ""
"A color key will appear in place of the Overview Map, and the colors "
"will appear in the Main Map to display the information. Solid black "
"areas on overlays indicate a complete lack of the selected feature or "
"service. Map squares that contain housing will outlined in gray, and "
"other structures (aside from roads and plazas) will be outlined in "
"black. \n"
"\n"
"Some of the Color Overlay Maps will leave relevant structures visible "
"on the Main Map. The Security overlay, for example, will leave Barracks "
"and Praefectures visible.\n"
"\n"
"#1025Previous Page#\n"
"#47Next Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 47: title
msgctxt "help/47/0"
msgid "Color Overlays (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/47/1"
msgid ""
"The Color Overlays are a good way to keep your finger on the pulse of "
"your city. Check them often -- they just may alert you to an impending "
"crisis in time to take preventive action. Remember to use the Legend "
"Square to place the Color Overlay on the Main Map. You may also use the "
"Zoom controls to set your field of view.\n"
"\n"
"You can access the following color-coded map reports from the city "
"level: Geography (clears all reports), Water and Supply, Health, Tax "
"Coverage, Education, Entertainment, Security, Markets, and Land Value.\n"
"\n"
"#46Previous Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 48: title
msgctxt "help/48/0"
msgid "Battle (page 4)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/48/1"
msgid ""
"Formations: You may order your units into a number of formations. You "
"may want to change troop formations as the two armies move around each "
"other. After ordering a formation, you MUST select a location for that "
"formation by clicking on the battlefield.\n"
"A Line is a row in which each soldier faces the enemy at the start of "
"the battle. The Line is an attack formation, i.e. troops in a line "
"formation will have an attack bonus in battle.\n"
"A Column is a row of soldiers aligned toward the enemy forces at the "
"start of the battle. The Column is an attack formation, i.e. troops in "
"a Column formation will have an attack bonus in battle.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#35Previous Page# / #49Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 49: title
msgctxt "help/49/0"
msgid "Battle (page 5)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/49/1"
msgid ""
"A Tortoise is an exclusively Roman formation in which all the troops on "
"the outside of a rectangle use their shields to form a protective wall "
"for the whole unit. The Tortoise is a defensive formation, i.e. troops "
"in this formation will have a defend bonus in battle.\n"
"If you issue the Pursue command, the selected unit(s) will break "
"formation and attack the nearest enemies.\n"
"Move:  To issue a movement command, select a unit or units, and then "
"click on the Move button. Your mouse pointer will now control a marker "
"showing the units' destination. Place the destination indicator on the "
"battlefield where you would like the selected troops to go, and click "
"to set the destination. You will not be able to set a destination on "
"any spot that is already occupied by troops.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#48Previous Page# / #50Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 50: title
msgctxt "help/50/0"
msgid "Battle (page 6) "
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/50/1"
msgid ""
"Aim:  If you have missile troops (Slingers, Archers, etc.), use the Aim "
"command to order them to fire. Just select the missile unit(s), select "
"the Aim button, and then click on the battlefield where you would like "
"the forces to fire. When the action resumes, you will see your missiles "
"flying through the air, and (hopefully) striking their targets. All "
"missile-bearing troops have limited firing ranges.\n"
"\n"
"The other buttons available in the Battle Screen are as follows: \n"
"\n"
"Turbo:  The Turbo button switches the speed of the action between two "
"modes. The quick mode speeds up the action, but diminishes the quality "
"of the battle animations. Use it to speed through a battle if you're in "
"a hurry.\n"
"\n"
"#49Previous Page# / #58Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 51: title
msgctxt "help/51/0"
msgid "Battle"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/51/1"
msgid ""
"The Battle Screen consists of a battlefield where the two armies face "
"each other, and a control panel. If you are unsure of which troops are "
"yours, just click on the soldiers' heads with your mouse. Map squares "
"beneath your units will highlight to indicate that they are selected, "
"while the squares beneath enemy units will not highlight. You may use "
"the Zoom buttons on the control panel to zoom in and out for a better "
"view of the battle field (there are two magnification levels), and you "
"may rotate your view using the rotate arrows.\n"
"\n"
"The window on the left side of the screen charts the numbers and morale "
"of both armies. The Overview Map shows an overall view of the "
"battlefield, with different colored dots to represent your troops and "
"the enemy's. \n"
"\n"
"#5Previous Page# / #34Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 52: title
msgctxt "help/52/0"
msgid "Your Legion"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/52/1"
msgid ""
"The term \"Legion\" describes your entire force of fighting men. You  may "
"manage your Legion in the Forum through your Centurion.\n"
"\n"
"Your Legion is composed of four types of soldiers: Heavy Infantry, "
"Light Infantry, Slingers, and Auxiliaries.\n"
"\n"
"The Legion is divided into fighting battalions called Cohorts. You will "
"have as many Cohorts as you have Forts in your Province -- each time "
"you build a fort, a new Cohort will come into existence. Your Legion "
"forces will be divided between its Cohorts based on each Cohorts "
"recruitment rate (Normal, Minor, or Major), which you set through the "
"Centurion in the Forum.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#5Previous Page# / #53Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 53: title
msgctxt "help/53/0"
msgid "Your Legion (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/53/1"
msgid ""
"Each Cohort will be further divided into Centuries. In Caesar II, a "
"Century is a unit of 60 soldiers of the same type. (In Roman times, the "
"number varied from 60 to more than 100. The word \"century\" means 100 in "
"Latin, even though a century often had fewer soldiers than that.) Each "
"Century will be represented by an icon in its Cohort's information "
"panel. An incomplete icon represents a Century with fewer than 60 "
"soldiers.\n"
"Each Cohort will have both a Morale rating and a Battle Readiness "
"status. \n"
"Morale refers to the spirit and eagerness of the soldiers to fight. It "
"is expressed in battles as a number between 0 (low morale) and 100 "
"(high morale). If a Century's morale is 0, it will refuse to fight.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#52Previous Page# / #54Next Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 54: title
msgctxt "help/54/0"
msgid "Your Legion (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/54/1"
msgid ""
"Battle Readiness refers to a minimum number of soldiers (100) required "
"before a Cohort is strong enough to act as an independent fighting "
"unit. In other words, a Cohort will only take the initiative to attack "
"an enemy without your orders if it has at least 100 soldiers. If a "
"Cohort has fewer than 100 soldiers, it can still fight and win (with "
"your orders), even though it is not considered Battle Ready.   \n"
"\n"
"To issue orders to a Cohort on the Province Level, use the Order Cohort "
"button.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#53Previous Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 55: title
msgctxt "help/55/0"
msgid "The Province Level"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/55/1"
msgid ""
"Select a topic below to view its description.\n"
"\n"
"#775Barbarians#\n"
"#805Border Towns#\n"
"#685Grassland#\n"
"#746Industrial Support#\n"
"#1108Industry#\n"
"#745Meadows#\n"
"#715Mountains#\n"
"#1113Order Cohort#\n"
"#1000Ports#\n"
"#835Roads#\n"
"#730Rocky Areas#\n"
"#700Sea#\n"
"#820Sealanes#\n"
"#1100Security#\n"
"#985Trading Post#\n"
"#670Woodlands#"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/56/0"
msgid "@55"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/56/1"
msgid "@55"
msgstr ""

#. help page 57: title
msgctxt "help/57/0"
msgid "General Concepts"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/57/1"
msgid ""
"You will benefit from an understanding of the following general "
"concepts in Caesar II.\n"
"\n"
"#95Money#\n"
"#99Population#\n"
"#105Land Values#\n"
"#108Growth Rates#\n"
"#135Unrest#\n"
"#136Employment#\n"
"#137Illness#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 58: title
msgctxt "help/58/0"
msgid "Battle (page 7)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/58/1"
msgid ""
"Select All: This button allows you to select all of your forces with a "
"single mouse click.\n"
"Retreat:  Select retreat to withdraw your forces from the battle. They "
"will disperse and regroup at their fort. This will be a serious blow to "
"morale, but may allow your troops to fight again another day. \n"
"Surrender: If you surrender, your troops will be entirely at the mercy "
"of the enemy. \n"
"\n"
"Auto:  Select this button if you would like to give up control of the "
"battle, and have its results calculated automatically for the remaining "
"forces.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#50Previous Page#\n"
"#14Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 59: title
msgctxt "help/59/0"
msgid "The City Level"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/59/1"
msgid ""
"Click on the listings below for a brief description of each item.\n"
"\n"
"#1092Amenities#\n"
"#1048Clear#\n"
"#57Concepts#\n"
"#1088Education#\n"
"#1080Entertainment#\n"
"#1060Forums#\n"
"#144Housing#\n"
"#1072Industry#\n"
"#115Rivers#\n"
"#309Roads#\n"
"#1076Sanitation#\n"
"#1068Security#\n"
"#1064Water Structures#\n"
"#1084Worship#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 60: title
msgctxt "help/60/0"
msgid "Welcome!"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/60/1"
msgid ""
"This tutorial will take you through the first steps of building a "
"thriving Roman city.  It will introduce game features one at a time, "
"and give you a chance to try each one out.\n"
"You can pause and unpause the sample game at any time by pressing the "
"\"P\" key.  To exit the sample game and continue with the tutorial, click "
"on the Exit button.\n"
"A mouse is required to play CAESAR II. When you are told to \"click on,\" "
"\"press\" or \"select\" something, use the left mouse button.\n"
"\n"
"-     Press this to see the last page you read.\n"
"\n"
"-     Press this to skip these introductory pages, and start building "
"right away.\n"
"\n"
"-     Press this to see the next page in the tutorial.\n"
"\n"
"-     Press this to exit the tutorial.\n"
"\n"
"Now press the button to move to the next page."
msgstr ""

#. help page 61: title
msgctxt "help/61/0"
msgid "The Object of CAESAR"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/61/1"
msgid ""
"YOU are a low-ranking Roman, charged with bringing Peace, Prosperity, "
"Culture and Empire to an untamed world.  The Empire is made up of "
"PROVINCES, which are ruled by Governors.  You are the Governor of one "
"of these provinces.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Each province contains a Capital City, which you must build to "
"greatness.  Each province also contains towns and resources that you "
"connect to your city, as well as enemies to defend against.  However, "
"you can choose to play a simplified version of the game, where you only "
"have to worry about what the City needs.  This tutorial uses the basic, "
"CITY-ONLY game."
msgstr ""

#. help page 62: title
msgctxt "help/62/0"
msgid "The City Screen"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/62/1"
msgid ""
"You view your Capital through the City Screen (shown here smaller than "
"normal size).  The left part of this screen is the MAIN MAP VIEW, which "
"shows part of the area where you will build your city -- a grassy plain "
"with a river running through it. At the right is the control column, "
"which has two main parts: an OVERVIEW MAP which shows an overhead view "
"of your entire city, and banks of ICONS that let you perform different "
"actions."
msgstr ""

#. help page 63: title
msgctxt "help/63/0"
msgid "Changing the View"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/63/1"
msgid ""
"You can do several things to change your view of the city.\n"
"\n"
"If you point to any part of the Overview Map with your mouse and press "
"the LEFT mouse button, the Main Map View will change to center on it.\n"
"\n"
"If you push the mouse pointer to any of the edges of the screen, the "
"Main Map View will SCROLL in the direction of the mouse pointer.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"These buttons ROTATE the Main Map View in either direction.  These do "
"NOT affect the Overview Map.\n"
"\n"
"These buttons ZOOM the Main Map View to one of three levels: far, "
"medium and close.  After you press Zoom In, click on the part of the "
"city you want to zoom in on (zooming out is automatic).  You can make "
"changes to your city at any zoom level."
msgstr ""

#. help page 64: title
msgctxt "help/64/0"
msgid "The Basics (Part I)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/64/1"
msgid ""
"We'll get building right away, but first you need to know some basics.\n"
"\n"
"FIRST, building a city costs money, which is counted in Roman coins "
"called DENARII.  The top right corner of the screen shows you how many "
"Denarii you have at all times, so watch it while building to make sure "
"you don't run out!\n"
"\n"
"Also, the cost for any buildings or actions you select is shown in the "
"small display between the banks of icons.\n"
"\n"
"You will spend more Denarii to maintain your city, and you will MAKE "
"Denarii by building successful HOUSES and BUSINESSES -- but we'll "
"explain that later."
msgstr ""

#. help page 65: title
msgctxt "help/65/0"
msgid "The Basics (Part II)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/65/1"
msgid ""
"SECOND, some buildings you put in your city will CHANGE after you build "
"them.  Some will develop into better (and sometimes larger) versions of "
"themselves, like a small tent growing into a great Palace.  Other will "
"become busier, fancier, or more productive.  In each case, the "
"structure grows because it is given things it needs to improve.  The "
"flipside is that when buildings do not have what they need, they may "
"shrink, and even disappear entirely!"
msgstr ""

#. help page 66: title
msgctxt "help/66/0"
msgid "Your Friend, the Query Panel"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/66/1"
msgid ""
"QUERY lets you find out how different structures in the city are doing. "
" Click on the Query button, then select any button or building for "
"information and on-line help. None of the regular game commands work in "
"Query Mode. To exit this mode, click again on the Query icon.\n"
"\n"
"(You can also query any button or building AT ANY TIME by pointing to "
"it and pressing the RIGHT mouse button.)\n"
"\n"
"When you query a building, the Query panel has several modes; for now, "
"leave it in GENERAL mode (indicated by the bottom-left icon).  This "
"mode tells you whether the building is growing or shrinking, and the "
"most important issue (if any) affecting its growth.  You will learn "
"about those issues in time.  For now, just remember that any time you "
"want any information about something in your city, USE THE QUERY PANEL."
msgstr ""

#. help page 67: title
msgctxt "help/67/0"
msgid "Enough Talk -- Let's Build!"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/67/1"
msgid ""
"Without further ado, you will start building houses.  When you select "
"the next page of this tutorial, you will be taken to the city view.  "
"There you can select these two icons (in addition to the "
"already-mentioned View and Query icons):\n"
"\n"
"HOUSING lets you place tents.  With the proper surroundings, these will "
"grow and change into fancier types of dwelling.  Without them, they may "
"wither and disappear.\n"
"\n"
"CLEAR AREA lets you remove houses or other structures from the City.  "
"Most of them turn to rubble when cleared, which you can then remove by "
"clearing it again.  Rubble is also cleared for you when you build on "
"it.  You have to pay to clear areas.\n"
"\n"
"To use either icon on a single square of the map, just select the "
"appropriate icon, point to the square and click."
msgstr ""

#. help page 68: title
msgctxt "help/68/0"
msgid "Enough Talk -- Let's Build! (cont..)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/68/1"
msgid ""
"Both of these commands let you build (or destroy) on several squares at "
"once.  Just click on one square and HOLD the mouse button down, and "
"then DRAG the mouse to another square -- a rectangle of squares between "
"those two points will be filled with tents, or cleared, as requested.  "
"For other structures, this will let you build a road, wall, or aqueduct "
"between the two points.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Once you select an icon, you can use it as much as you like, until you "
"select another one.\n"
"\n"
"Now turn the page and start experimenting with these icons.  When "
"you're ready for more, wait for the timer to count down to zero, or "
"just press this Button."
msgstr ""

#. help page 69: title
msgctxt "help/69/0"
msgid "Okay, So What's Going On?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/69/1"
msgid ""
"When you built houses, you saw that they DIDN'T actually grow much "
"larger; they stayed as small tents.  Some of them may have even "
"disappeared soon after you built them.  Since growing houses into "
"beautiful mansions is one of your main jobs as Governor, you'll "
"probably want to know why.  The important thing to remember about "
"houses is that there are only two things that stop them from growing.  "
"Either --\n"
"\n"
"A) the LAND VALUE in their area is too low,\n"
"\n"
"  -- OR --\n"
"\n"
"B) they are missing something that they NEED.\n"
"\n"
"(If you QUERIED your houses, you may have found some of this out already.)"
msgstr ""

#. help page 70: title
msgctxt "help/70/0"
msgid "What is Land Value?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/70/1"
msgid ""
"LAND VALUE is a number between 0 and 64 that is given to every square "
"on the city map.  Everything you place on the map affects the value of "
"the land surrounding it.  For example, if you build a Coliseum in the "
"city, it boosts nearby land values because entertainment is good for "
"your city.  It's also something that houses like to be near, and that's "
"important because LAND VALUE DETERMINES HOW LARGE A HOUSE CAN GROW.  "
"The higher the land value is in a square, the larger and fancier a "
"house built there will become.  Some other buildings (like temples and "
"fountains) also improve in high land value, but houses are affected the "
"most (and are most important).\n"
"\n"
"Most things you build add to land values (why build something that "
"hurts your city?); items that result from disaster -- fire, rubble, "
"disease -- actively LOWER land values.  A few structures -- mainly "
"those related to industry and security -- will LIMIT land values in the "
"areas around them.  This is because, just as you won't find mansions "
"built right next to factories, your city won't produce palaces right "
"next to these necessary, but less-than-dazzling buildings.\n"
"\n"
"You can learn the land value of a square by QUERYING it."
msgstr ""

#. help page 71: title
msgctxt "help/71/0"
msgid "Making Things Clear -- Color Overlays"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/71/1"
msgid ""
"To get a better idea of how land values -- and other issues affecting "
"your city -- work, you can use the Color Overlay maps.  These are "
"color-coded reports that appear on top of the Overview Map, and can be "
"displayed on the Main Map View by pressing the Map Legend button.  This "
"button also explains what the colors in an Overlay mean.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Call up a Color Overlay by clicking on this window, and selecting a "
"report from the menu that appears.  There are Color Overlay reports for "
"almost every issue affecting the city.\n"
"\n"
"Now let's take a look at the second thing that limits a house's growth "
"-- when it NEEDS something."
msgstr ""

#. help page 72: title
msgctxt "help/72/0"
msgid "Water, the first Housing Need"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/72/1"
msgid ""
"Houses need many things to reach their full potential; the purpose of "
"most of the structures you build in the city is to provide for those "
"needs.  The first, most important need these people have is for a "
"source of WATER.  Fortunately, you have many ways to give them water -- "
"and TWO types of water to give.\n"
"\n"
"The most important type of water is WATER SUPPLY, which is what people "
"can actually use.  The second type is PIPE ACCESS, which gives water to "
"OTHER BUILDINGS.\n"
"\n"
"WELLS are a primitive Water Supply.  Building one will let houses grow "
"only SO FAR; they will need a better Water Supply to expand further.\n"
"\n"
"RIVERS are also a primitive water supply, but naturally you can't build "
"them.\n"
"\n"
"When built next to a River, RESERVOIRS provide a better Water Supply "
"for people in a small area, but more importantly they provide PIPE "
"ACCESS to buildings like --"
msgstr ""

#. help page 73: title
msgctxt "help/73/0"
msgid "Water (cont..)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/73/1"
msgid ""
"FOUNTAINS, which turn Pipe Access into Water Supply for a good-sized "
"area, and AQUEDUCTS that connect two reservoirs together, so that you "
"can build a reservoir that provides water far from a river.  You can "
"connect only five reservoirs together in this way, and each one will "
"give less supply and access than the last.\n"
"\n"
"This picture demonstrates how the different water structures work.  The "
"blue, red and purple areas show which squares have water supply, pipe "
"access, or both, respectively.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Now try to give water to your houses.  When one of these structures is "
"properly set up to provide water, you will see the water.  You can also "
"check on an area's water status by Querying it, or by calling up the "
"WATER Color Overlay.  All water structures are available through the "
"Water icon."
msgstr ""

#. help page 74: title
msgctxt "help/74/0"
msgid "Forum and Roads"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/74/1"
msgid ""
"To grow further, your houses will want access to other areas of the "
"city and the services they provide.  For this, they need access to "
"ROADS.  Houses don't need to be directly on a road in order to use "
"them, but they shouldn't be too far away from one.  You can join two "
"points with a road by clicking-and-dragging the mouse as you do for "
"Aqueducts.\n"
"\n"
"The first thing houses will use roads for is to get to a FORUM.  People "
"like Forums because they are good gathering places; YOU will like "
"forums because they collect POPULATION TAX from your houses.  "
"Population Tax is one of your two sources of income, so you'll want to "
"give forum access to all your houses.  Now you can only build one type "
"of forum, the Aventine; but when your city has grown large enough, "
"you'll be able to build larger and more effective ones.\n"
"\n"
"When you build a forum, make sure it's right next to a road.  You'll "
"see why soon after you build one; as long as you have some houses in "
"your city, that's when the forum will produce its first WALKER."
msgstr ""

#. help page 75: title
msgctxt "help/75/0"
msgid "Forum and Roads (cont..)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/75/1"
msgid ""
"Walkers are little people that forge links between buildings in your "
"city; in this case the walker is called a Forum Clerk.  You actually "
"see walkers wander around your city, providing a link (in this case, "
"Forum Access) to every area of the map they pass by.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"However, walkers can only walk so far before they are replaced with a "
"new one, and they make random turns at each crossing they reach.  So "
"you must build a network of roads large enough for walkers to get "
"around quickly and easily, but not so large that they get confused and "
"wander aimlessly.\n"
"\n"
"If you display the Cover Overlay called \"Tax Coverage\" (because forum "
"access equals tax coverage), you can see what areas are linked to "
"forums.  Note that areas around forums ALWAYS have forum access.  "
"Return to your city now to give your houses road access, and place a "
"forum somewhere on those roads."
msgstr ""

#. help page 76: title
msgctxt "help/76/0"
msgid "Internal Security"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/76/1"
msgid ""
"There are two types of security that houses need.  INTERNAL Security "
"protects houses from threats within the city.  EXTERNAL Security "
"protects against threats from outside the city.  The Security icon "
"builds these structures:\n"
"\n"
"PRAEFECTURES produce walkers called \"Vigiles\" who wander around the "
"city watching for fires, rioters or invaders. If they find one, they "
"try to get rid of it.\n"
"\n"
"BARRACKS produce walkers called \"soldiers,\" who keep a look out for "
"rioters or enemy barbarians attacking the city, and will attack and try "
"to defeat any they find.  They are much better at this combat than "
"vigiles.\n"
"\n"
"Like any structure that produces walkers, both buildings need to be "
"built NEXT TO ROADS.  Any areas that either a Vigile or Soldier "
"regularly walks past will receive Internal Security."
msgstr ""

#. help page 77: title
msgctxt "help/77/0"
msgid "External Security"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/77/1"
msgid ""
"For external security, you can put a WALL around your city and add "
"TOWERS to strengthen it.  A Wall will help protect the city from "
"barbarian invaders, though barbarians will eventually be able to knock "
"down any wall.  Towers provide one more line of defense, as they will "
"produce Soldiers when an enemy is near.  RIVERS also act as walls, but "
"not if you build Roads over them.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Your citizens want to feel secure, but as mentioned earlier, housing "
"located too near to any security structure will be LIMITED in its "
"growth.\n"
"\n"
"Return to your sample city and add a praefecture or two, plus a "
"barracks.  Make sure they're not too close to your houses, but that "
"they have road access to them.  Add a wall around your city if you like "
"(if you want to spend the money).  Use the SECURITY Color Overlay to "
"see which areas have been secured. "
msgstr ""

#. help page 78: title
msgctxt "help/78/0"
msgid "The Forum Screen"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/78/1"
msgid ""
"Aside from the Forums you build in your city for your people to use, "
"you have a Forum of your own that YOU can use.  It contains advisors "
"and areas that help you control your city's services and see how well "
"the city is doing.\n"
"\n"
"To reach your Forum, click on the Go to Forum Icon.\n"
"\n"
"You click on the people and objects here to access them.  (You can also "
"use the menu at the bottom of the screen.)  There are nine areas in the "
"Forum, seven of which are used in this tutorial.  We'll discuss three "
"of them now -- you can discover the rest."
msgstr ""

#. help page 79: title
msgctxt "help/79/0"
msgid "Maintenance and the Plebeian Tribune"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/79/1"
msgid ""
"The Plebeian Tribune directs the work force of Plebs that are assigned "
"to specific jobs in your city -- fire prevention, roads, water and "
"walls.  (A set amount are assigned to construction; if you don't "
"provide enough welfare for even them, you won't be able to build!)  You "
"can use the arrows to move the plebs between jobs, and to change the "
"amount spent on their welfare (which changes how many plebs you have).\n"
"\n"
"As a city grows, the numbers you need for each job will grow.  If you "
"end up with too few plebs for a job, a warning will appear on the Menu "
"Bar, giving you some time to go the Plebeian Tribune to fix things.  If "
"you don't, disaster may strike!\n"
"\n"
"Since new plebs take time to appear, you should plan any welfare "
"changes ahead of time.  When expanding your city, keep extra plebs on "
"hand for each job."
msgstr ""

#. help page 80: title
msgctxt "help/80/0"
msgid "Taxes and The Treasurer"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/80/1"
msgid ""
"The Treasurer keeps tracks of your finances, and lets you set the tax "
"rates for your city.  You collect two types of taxes:\n"
"\n"
"POPULATION TAX, which comes from houses, and\n"
"\n"
"INDUSTRIAL TAX, which comes from businesses.\n"
"\n"
"You can set a different rate for each of these taxes.  If you raise a "
"tax rate, you will get more money, but you risk angering your "
"population and decreasing productivity."
msgstr ""

#. help page 81: title
msgctxt "help/81/0"
msgid "Ratings and the Oracle"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/81/1"
msgid ""
"The Oracle screen shows your performance as COLUMNS that rise as you do "
"better.  The two columns on the right -- PROSPERITY AND CULTURE -- are "
"the only ones measured in a city-building-only game.  In a full game "
"(which includes the Province Level and optional Battles), all four "
"ratings (the previous two, plus PEACE and EMPIRE) are used to determine "
"when you deserve to get a PROMOTION to a tougher, more challenging city.\n"
"\n"
"When you want to learn how your city can improve, click on the BOXES "
"below each column for advice.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"Now go to the forum, and check out these three areas.  Make sure to:\n"
"\n"
"+ Increase your spending on pleb welfare, and assign plebs where they "
"are needed (you will be doing a lot of building, so add extra ones to "
"Fire Prevention!)\n"
"\n"
"+ Check out your cash balances, and adjust your tax rates if you want "
"(but be prepared for the consequences!)"
msgstr ""

#. help page 82: title
msgctxt "help/82/0"
msgid "What Else Can I Build?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/82/1"
msgid ""
"Houses have many other needs that can be fulfilled by other buildings "
"-- some are very important for every citizen, while others are only "
"required for the elite who live in villas and palaces.  All of these "
"buildings increase the land values around them.  The following buttons "
"let you build these structures:\n"
"\n"
"SANITATION lets you build Baths and Hospitals.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"The WORSHIP button lets you build three sizes of religious structures.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"The ENTERTAINMENT button lets you build Theaters, Arenas and Circuses."
msgstr ""

#. help page 83: title
msgctxt "help/83/0"
msgid "What Else...? (cont..)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/83/1"
msgid ""
"The EDUCATION button lets you build the Grammaticus, Rhetor and "
"Library. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"The AMENITIES button lets you build Gardens and Plazas.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"The INDUSTRY button lets you build Markets, and eight kinds of Business.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"You can learn about each of these buildings by referring to the On-Line "
"Help or the Game Manual.  Now try them out for a while, and then come "
"back here for some final tips."
msgstr ""

#. help page 84: title
msgctxt "help/84/0"
msgid "End of Tutorial"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/84/1"
msgid ""
"In conclusion, follow these basic guidelines when expanding your city:\n"
"\n"
"- Increase your profitability by building INDUSTRY.\n"
"\n"
"- QUERY your houses regularly, and try your best to give them what they "
"want.  Keep a particular eye on the GROWTH RATES listed there.\n"
"\n"
"- Change your tax and conscription rates carefully, or else risk "
"angering your population and industries.\n"
"\n"
"- Maintain a cash reserve, so you are always prepared to deal with an "
"emergency.\n"
"\n"
"- Finally, remember that the city level is only PART of the Caesar II "
"experience.  Try starting a new game with the Campaign option ON.\n"
"\n"
"Hail, Caesar!"
msgstr ""

