#. help page 105: title
msgctxt "help/105/0"
msgid "Land Values"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/105/1"
msgid ""
"Land Values determine the extent to which buildings in your city "
"(housing, in particular) will develop or decline. Each map square will "
"have a value ranging from 0 to 64. There are many ever-changing "
"influences which will overlap and work with or against each other to "
"raise or limit the land value of any given square. In other words, it "
"may not always be easy to figure out exactly why any given squareÆs "
"value is higher or lower than another's.\n"
"There are, however, some basic rules to keep in mind when trying to "
"manage land values. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#57Previous Page# / #106Next Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 106: title
msgctxt "help/106/0"
msgid "Land Values (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/106/1"
msgid ""
"Every structure that you place down will have a radius within which it "
"boosts land values. However, some types of structures will also have "
"the effect of capping, or limiting the value of the land around it. "
"Structures that limit land values are as follows: Barracks, "
"Praefectures, Markets, Businesses, Walls, Gates, and Towers. While "
"these structures are necessary and positive for your city, they just "
"donÆt make good neighbors, so land values near them will always be "
"limited.\n"
"The only influences that will actively reduce land values are rubble, "
"fire, and plague. You would be wise to eliminate these as soon as "
"possible. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#105Previous Page# / #107Next Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 107: title
msgctxt "help/107/0"
msgid "Land Values (page 3)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/107/1"
msgid ""
"Other structures -- Bath Houses, Forums, Temples, Entertainment "
"centers, Hospitals, Schools & Libraries, and Amenities -- will have "
"only positive effects on land value. These structures provide valuable "
"services and are a pleasure to live near.\n"
"In addition to the radius of influence that structures exert, some "
"structures also produce walkers, which patrol the streets and provide "
"benefits to any areas they walk past. Praefectures, for example, send "
"out Vigiles which pass a security bonus to any building within a range "
"of three squares that they walk by. Markets send out Market Traders, "
"and Barracks and Towers send out Soldiers. Walkers have a limited "
"lifespan. Keep this in mind as you design your road system. Areas that "
"receive much road traffic will have the highest potential land values.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#106Previous Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 108: title
msgctxt "help/108/0"
msgid "Growth Rates"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/108/1"
msgid ""
"You city will have both an Industrial Growth Rate and a Population "
"Growth Rate, which indicate the trends for development in the city. To "
"check your growth rates, just right click on any structure on the City "
"Level . You should strive to keep both rates as positive as you can. \n"
"Industrial Growth Rate is determined primarily by your Industrial Tax "
"rate. The higher your Industrial taxes, the lower your Industrial "
"Growth Rate will drop. In addition, any sharp increase in Industrial "
"taxes will cause lingering damage your Industrial Growth Rate.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#57Previous Page# / #109Next Page#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 109: title
msgctxt "help/109/0"
msgid "Growth Rates (page 2)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/109/1"
msgid ""
"Population Growth Rate is based on your Population Tax Rate, your level "
"of employment, and on the inherent temperament of the people in your "
"Province. If your taxes are low, employment is high, and if your people "
"are happy with your regime, your Population Growth Rate will be high, "
"and you will benefit.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#108Previous Page#"
msgstr ""

