msgctxt "help/144/0"
msgid "Housing"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/144/1"
msgid ""
"This button allows you to place housing in your city. Housing will "
"begin as huts, and will develop into more advanced buildings depending "
"on the Land Value of its location. \n"
"\n"
"#145Help with Housing#\n"
"#146Tips for Housing#\n"
"#147History of Housing#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 145: title
msgctxt "help/145/0"
msgid "Help with Housing"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/145/1"
msgid ""
"People will only move into your city and build nice houses if you "
"provide pleasant areas for them to live.  To place a section of "
"housing, select the Housing button on the control panel and click on "
"the map where you would like to build yoiur houses. All housing will "
"begin as Huts, which will develop and grow only if they get what they "
"need: water, forum access, baths, hospitals, entertainment, and so on. "
"If you fail to provide the basic necessities for your housing, "
"residents will pack up and leave, and your houses will vanish. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#144Previous Page# / #118Next Page#\n"
"#146Tips# / #147History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 146: title
msgctxt "help/146/0"
msgid "Tips for Housing"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/146/1"
msgid ""
"There are many different approaches to building successful areas of "
"housing. The one that suits you best will depend on how developed your "
"city is, and what your goals for the new housing are. For instance, if "
"you want to build your industry base, then all your housing needs is "
"some water, road access to your businesses and a forum, and perhaps a "
"few extras to raise land value. If you want to build blocks of the best "
"housing possible, surround them with a road and place all of their "
"needs around them.  More importantly, try to arrange these high-value "
"blocks so that multiple blocks can share the benefits of the same "
"entertainment, education, and so on.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#144Previous Page# / #44Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #147History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 147: title
msgctxt "help/147/0"
msgid "Roman Housing"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/147/1"
msgid ""
"At the height of the Empire, the city of Rome sheltered about one "
"million people, a truly staggering population for a city of ancient "
"times.  Less than two out of every ten people in the city could afford "
"to live in a private house, or Domus.  The rest of the population -- "
"more than 800,000 souls -- lived in apartment buildings called Insulae, "
"a Roman invention of necessity.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#148Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 148: title
msgctxt "help/148/0"
msgid "Roman Housing (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/148/1"
msgid ""
"The Domus was a pleasant dwelling, built as a hollow rectangle one or "
"two stories high, with the living quarters surrounding an inner garden. "
" It could house an extended family of a dozen or more members, plus "
"several dozen more slaves, servants, and close friends.  The wealthiest "
"Romans even had private running water piped into their homes.  \n"
"\n"
"The residents of the Insulae were less fortunate.  These \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#147Previous Page# / #149Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 149: title
msgctxt "help/149/0"
msgid "Roman Housing (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/149/1"
msgid ""
"buildings were usually at least three stories tall, and some grew as "
"high as seven stories. Each floor held a separate apartment, or "
"cenacula, and each cenacula housed five or six people. The first story "
"of the building was made of stone, but the upper stories used "
"progressively more and more wood, to make them lighter and easier to "
"construct. This also made them flimsier, more prone to collapse, less "
"resistant to the weather, and a major fire hazard. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#148Previous Page# / #150 Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 150: title
msgctxt "help/150/0"
msgid "Roman Housing (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/150/1"
msgid ""
"The lower apartments were therefore the most desirable and expensive; "
"they were often leased to shopkeepers and artisans as retail space.  "
"The top floor was the most miserable place in the insula to live -- hot "
"in the summer, cold in the winter, and a death trap in the case of "
"collapse or fire.  \n"
"\n"
"So far, archaeological excavations have turned up evidence of Insulae "
"only at Rome and Ostia, Rome's port city.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#149Previous Page# / #151 Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 151: title
msgctxt "help/151/0"
msgid "Roman Housing (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/151/1"
msgid ""
"Provincial towns might have had a few Insulae, but since space wasn't "
"at nearly the premium it was in Rome, most citizens managed to have a "
"Domus, even if only a small one.  \n"
"\n"
"The wealthiest Romans could afford the luxury of a country house, "
"called a Villa Urbana.  The Villa was a scaled-up and more luxuriously "
"appointed version of the Domus.  A rich Roman would often try to spend "
"as much time at his\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#150Previous Page# / #152Next Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 152: title
msgctxt "help/152/0"
msgid "Roman Housing (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/152/1"
msgid ""
"Villa as possible, away from the noise, smell, and heat of city life.  \n"
"\n"
" The Emperor, of course, didn't have to settle for a Domus. He, his "
"family, and his huge staff all lived in the Imperial Palace on top of "
"the Palatine Hill.  The Palatine, believed by Romans to be the site "
"where Romulus founded their city, was reserved for the exclusive use of "
"the Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#151Previous Page#\n"
"#145Help# / #146Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 153: title
msgctxt "help/153/0"
msgid "The Centurion (page 5)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/153/1"
msgid ""
"proportion to the other Cohorts in the Legion. Under the Minor setting, "
"the Cohort will receive a slightly lower proportion of new recruits, "
"and under the Major setting, the Cohort will receive a slightly larger "
"proportion of new soldiers.\n"
"\n"
"A Cohort will perform better if its Morale is high. Morale will rise "
"after a Cohort is victorious in battle and will fall if it loses a "
"battle.\n"
"\n"
"If you Demobilize a Cohort, the Cohort will stop receiving new recruits "
"from the training pool.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#23Previous Page#\n"
"#13Tips#"
msgstr ""

