#. help page 235: title
msgctxt "help/235/0"
msgid "Worship"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/235/1"
msgid ""
"In addition to providing places of worship, religious buildings provide "
"secure storage for your treasury's gold. If your city begins to "
"accumulate wealth but has no religious buildings in which to secure it, "
"robberies are likely to occur. More wealth creates a need for more "
"religious buildings. Religious buildings come in three sizes: Shrine, "
"Temple, and Basilica, which increase in size, cost, and capacity "
"respectively. Since these buildings store valuables, be sure to build "
"them in places with high security levels (i.e. with road connections to "
"Barracks and Praefectures) or they may fall victim to crime.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1084Previous Page# / #42Next Page#\n"
"#236Tips# / #237History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 236: title
msgctxt "help/236/0"
msgid "Tips for Worship"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/236/1"
msgid ""
"Building religious structures near barracks and praefectures is the "
"best way to stop robberies, but they will have little effect on housing "
"there.\n"
"\n"
"Remember that temples are a good way to increase land values for "
"housing when they already have all of their needs fulfilled.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#235Help# / #237History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 237: title
msgctxt "help/237/0"
msgid "Roman Worship"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/237/1"
msgid ""
"The Temple of Vesta \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#238Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 238: title
msgctxt "help/238/0"
msgid "Roman Worship"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/238/1"
msgid ""
"The Romans were a superstitious people who spent a lot of time, effort, "
"and wealth trying to please the gods and influence them to act in the "
"desired manner. One common method for earning a god's favor was to "
"swear a vow to build that god a new temple, provided the god granted a "
"certain favor, such as victory in battle or in a political campaign.  "
"If the god came through, the Roman would be obliged to provide the "
"temple, as luxurious as he could afford.  Thanks to this, Rome was a "
"city with temples on almost every street corner.\n"
"\n"
"The Romans built temples to any number of things.  The established gods "
"had their worship places, of \n"
"#237Previous Page#/#239Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 239: title
msgctxt "help/239/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/239/1"
msgid ""
"course, but there were also temples dedicated to foreign gods the "
"Romans adopted, to heroic legendary figures like Romulus and Aeneas, to "
"deified Emperors, to the city of Rome itself as a supernatural entity, "
"and even to nebulous ideals: one lovely and famous temple, the Ara "
"Pacis Augustae, or Altar ofAugustan Peace, is dedicated to the worship "
"of peace itself.\n"
"\n"
" Early Roman temples were small, round, colonnaded structures.  By "
"Imperial times, a more Greek look had taken over and temples became "
"rectangular, as well as much larger and more luxurious.  Three temples "
"of particular magnificence dominated Imperial Rome: the temple of Juno "
"Moneta, the temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, and the Pantheon.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#238Previous Page#/#240Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 240: title
msgctxt "help/240/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/240/1"
msgid ""
"The temple of the goddess Juno Moneta -- \"Juno Admonisher\" -- was "
"located on the north crest of the Capitoline hill. In addition to being "
"a center of worship to the dominant female goddess of the Romans, it "
"served as an Imperial mint and reserve.  (The name \"Moneta\" came down "
"to the present day as the word \"money.\")  On the south crest of the "
"same hill was the huge temple for Juno's husband (and brother) Jupiter "
"Optimus Maximus, first erected in 509 BC.  This temple burned down "
"three times in Rome's history and was rebuilt in larger and more "
"luxurious form each time.  Its final incarnation, created by the "
"Emperor Domitian, was entirely of white marble, with gilded roof tiles "
"and doors covered in solid gold plate.  Inside the temple could be "
"found piles of treasure heaped there as offerings by Roman generals who "
"had been victorious in war. \n"
"#239Previous Page#/#241Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 241: title
msgctxt "help/241/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/241/1"
msgid ""
"The most exceptional temple in Rome was the remarkable Pantheon, the "
"\"Temple of All Gods.\" It was first erected in 27 BC by the Roman "
"general Agrippa, but Emperor Hadrian so completely redesigned and "
"rebuilt it in 118 AD that archaeologists have been unable to decipher "
"Agrippa's original building plan.  The Pantheon's most stunning feature "
"is its huge dome with its perfectly round central skylight, made of "
"identical castings of the finest concrete.  The dome, 114 feet in "
"diameter, was the largest dome ever constructed until the twentieth "
"century. Thanks to its dedication to \"all gods\", it was able to make "
"the transition from pagan temple to church with the arrival of "
"Christianity, and it survives intact today, one of the most "
"awe-inspiring remnants of Roman architecture still in existence. \n"
"\n"
"#240Previous Page#/ #242 Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 242: title
msgctxt "help/242/0"
msgid "Roman Worship"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/242/1"
msgid ""
"The Pantheon: Inside view of the dome and ouside view of main entrance.\n"
"\n"
"#241 Previous Page#"
msgstr ""

