#. help page 280: title
msgctxt "help/280/0"
msgid "Help with Forums"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/280/1"
msgid ""
"Forums are your city's centers of government. Their primary role is to "
"collect industrial taxes from your businesses and population taxes from "
"your citizens. They also act as gathering places for the masses, so "
"people will want to live near them. An absence of forum access will "
"limit a neighborhood's growth.\n"
"\n"
"Your forums must be adequately linked via roads to all houses and "
"businesses in the city. Forums send out Forum Clerks to visit housing "
"areas and bring in municipal cash.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#39Next Page#\n"
"#281Tips# / #282History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 281: title
msgctxt "help/281/0"
msgid "Tips for Forums"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/281/1"
msgid ""
"As a city grows you'll be able to build larger, more effective forums.  "
"You might want to leave room around the small forums you build, so that "
"you can improve them later.\n"
"\n"
"If your tax coverage seems to vary in part of your city, try adding a "
"forum to the area, or changing the road network there to include more "
"or fewer crossings. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#280Help# / #282History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 282: title
msgctxt "help/282/0"
msgid "The Roman Forum"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/282/1"
msgid ""
"The Forum of Augustus\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#283Next Page#\n"
"#280Help#/#281Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 283: title
msgctxt "help/283/0"
msgid "The Roman Forum"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/283/1"
msgid ""
"During the time of Rome's Etruscan kings (prior to 509 BC), the word "
"\"forum\" simply referred to the little marshy valley area between the "
"Capitoline and Palatine hills where the city dwellers would go to trade "
"goods, settle disputes, and gossip.  As time progressed, the valley was "
"drained and buildings dedicated to the political, judicial, and "
"administrative tasks of the settlement sprouted on the valley floor.  "
"The whole area was landscaped and remade into a place fit to become the "
"heart of the greatest city of the ancient world.  \n"
"\n"
" The forum was the social and political center of Rome.  Temples, "
"fountains, baths, and public \n"
"#282Previous Page#/#284Next Page#\n"
"#280Help#/#281Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 284: title
msgctxt "help/284/0"
msgid "The Roman Forum (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/284/1"
msgid ""
"entertainment facilities surrounded it on every side.  Public debate on "
"important issues took place there, as did important trials.  The Roman "
"equivalent of the daily newspaper, the Acta Diurna, was posted on the "
"wall every day to be read or copied down by the eager citizens.  \n"
"\n"
" This original meeting place, the Forum Romanum Magnum, was sufficient "
"for the city's needs until  Imperial times.  When Julius Caesar claimed "
"dictatorship, one of his first acts was to build an entirely new forum, "
"the Forum of Caesar, in the same area, probably as a strong symbol of "
"the new political order.  The cost of building this forum was enormous, "
"since land within the original valley area was extremely scarce and "
"therefore expensive.  Later emperors copied Caesar's grandiose gesture, "
"until by \n"
"#283Previous Page#/#285Next Page#\n"
"#280Help#/#281Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 285: title
msgctxt "help/285/0"
msgid "The Roman Forum (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/285/1"
msgid ""
"the end of the Empire there were six forums in the valley:  the "
"original and Caesar's, plus the Forum of Augustus, the Forum of "
"Vespasian, the forum of Nerva, and the Forum of Trajan. This last "
"forum, built between 112 and 114 AD, was the grandest of the lot, and "
"doubled the area occupied by the previous five forums. As a rule, new "
"forums were built just after times of serious political unrest -- which "
"was undoubtedly no coincidence. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#284Previous Page#\n"
"#280Help#/#281Tips#"
msgstr ""

