#. help page 309: title
msgctxt "help/309/0"
msgid "Roads"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/309/1"
msgid ""
"This button allows you to build a road system in your city. You may "
"place a strip of road by clicking on individual squares, one at a time; "
"or you may click and hold your mouse button down and drag the pointer "
"across an area to place a strip. The displayed construction cost the "
"cost per map square of road. \n"
"\n"
"#310Help with Roads#\n"
"#311Tips for Roads#\n"
"#312History of Roads#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 310: title
msgctxt "help/310/0"
msgid "Help with Roads"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/310/1"
msgid ""
"A good network of roads connecting your buildings is vital to your "
"city. Many buildings need to be connected to other buildings in order "
"for your city to function. Houses must be connected to Forums in order "
"for your tax collectors to do their jobs. Markets must have roads for "
"their traders to travel. Barracks and Towers send out soldiers, and "
"Praefectures produce vigiles who need roads to patrol. And workers need "
"roads between housing and their businesses.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#38Next Page#\n"
"#311Tips# / #312History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 311: title
msgctxt "help/311/0"
msgid "Tips for Roads"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/311/1"
msgid ""
"If you often surround \"blocks\" of housing with a single road, make sure "
"you don't make the blocks too small or too large.  Very small blocks "
"will cause your city's walkers to follow too many twists and turns in "
"their travels, causing their coverage of your housing to be erratic.\n"
"\n"
"On the other hand, very large blocks will make it difficult for walkers "
"to regularly make it all the way around the block, which results in "
"uneven coverage unless you build more structures that produce walkers, "
"and place them on all sides of the city.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#309Previous Page# / #45Next Page#\n"
"#310Help# / #312History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 312: title
msgctxt "help/312/0"
msgid "Roman City Roads"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/312/1"
msgid ""
"The Romans were great road builders.  Their construction methods "
"created highways so sound that they have long outlasted the Empire that "
"created them and are still in use today.  The excellence of Roman road "
"construction can be seen in the precise square grid layout of the "
"streets found in the ruins of many provincial cities.  \n"
"\n"
"Ironically, the streets of Rome itself were notoriously bad -- not for "
"their construction, which was the usual high quality, but for their "
"layout, which was tortuous in the extreme once a citizen ventured off "
"the main routes.  Unlike the planned provincial cities, Rome simply "
"grew from accidental beginnings; her \n"
"#313Next Page#\n"
"#310Help#/#311Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 313: title
msgctxt "help/313/0"
msgid "Roman City Roads (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/313/1"
msgid ""
"road network was laid out in her earliest years, and remained in its "
"original configuration long after that layout was obsolete.  As a "
"result, only the few major roads were straight and wide; the rest were "
"narrow, twisty, and crooked.  Despite this design deficiency, the "
"streets of Rome were generally cleaner and better maintained than any "
"city streets until the nineteenth century; they were sturdily paved "
"with cut rock bricks, and regularly cleaned everywhere except in the "
"poorer sections. People living in these areas had no public latrine "
"facilities and usually threw their refuse out through windows to land "
"in the street -- or on unfortunate passers-by.\n"
"\n"
" Rome had three types of roads: the itinera, a pedestrian road; the "
"actus, wide enough for a single cart; and the main viae, wide enough "
"for two carts to \n"
"#312Previous Page#/#314Next Page#\n"
"#310Help#/#311Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 314: title
msgctxt "help/314/0"
msgid "Roman City Roads (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/314/1"
msgid ""
"pass each other.  There were less than twenty viae in the entire city "
"of Rome.  The traffic situation was so bad by the start of Imperial "
"times that one of Julius Caesar's first decrees forbade wagons and "
"carts from entering the city during daylight.  All shipping and "
"transport had to take place under the cover of darkness.  The "
"congestion was relieved slightly after the great fire of 64 when Nero "
"rebuilt the ruined part of the city with better streets. It was barely "
"a patch on the real problem, however, which was never adequately solved "
"-- merely tolerated. The only advantage to the narrow streets was the "
"shade provided by the tall insulae buildings on either side, which was "
"complete enough that some streets in the city never saw direct "
"sunlight. \n"
"\n"
" The main viae were no more than fifteen or twenty \n"
"#313Previous Page#/#315Next Page#\n"
"#310Help#/#311Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 315: title
msgctxt "help/315/0"
msgid "Roman City Roads (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/315/1"
msgid ""
"feet wide.  The first one built was the Via Appia -- the Appian Way -- "
"constructed by Appius Claudius Caecula in the fourth century BC.  Other "
"main roads were the Via Aurelia and the Via Flaminia, which is still "
"used in modern Rome, rechristened the Via del Corso. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
