#. help page 340: title
msgctxt "help/340/0"
msgid "Aqueducts"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/340/1"
msgid ""
"You may place a length of aqueduct by clicking on a line of map "
"squares, one at a time, or by dragging across a map area while holding "
"your mouse button down. An aqueduct can only be used to send water from "
"one reservoir to another reservoir. In this way, you can send water all "
"over your city. You cannot connect aqueducts to Fountains or Wells.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#341Tips# / #342History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 341: title
msgctxt "help/341/0"
msgid "Tips for Reservoirs and Aqueducts"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/341/1"
msgid ""
"The length of an aqueduct does not affect its performance -- only the "
"number of reservoirs supplied by it does.  However, longer aqueducts "
"require more plebeian maintenance. You can run several aqueducts off of "
"the same reservoir, to create a \"water tree\" that has branches in "
"different parts of the city. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#340Help# / #342History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 342: title
msgctxt "help/342/0"
msgid "Roman Aqueducts"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/342/1"
msgid ""
"The aqueduct system that brought plentiful fresh water to Rome's "
"denizens was an engineering marvel.  The first one, the Aqua Appia, was "
"built in 312 BC by Appius Claudius Caecus, the same man who built the "
"Appian Way, Rome's first major road.  It carried water into the city "
"from a spring ten miles to the east, running underground for most of "
"its length. As it neared the city, it was elevated onto the famous "
"Roman aqueduct arches, so that water could be piped into the wealthy "
"hilltop dwellings.  \n"
"\n"
" At its height, Rome had at least fourteen separate aqueducts, which "
"spanned 316 miles and brought in 60 million cubic feet of fresh water "
"each day.  The \n"
"#343Next Page#\n"
"#340Help#/#341Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 343: title
msgctxt "help/343/0"
msgid "Roman Aqueducts (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/343/1"
msgid ""
"longest city aqueduct was the Aqua Marcia which extended more than 56 "
"miles.  This extensive system wasn't restricted to Rome proper; any "
"major Roman city had a similar arrangement.  The aqueduct system at "
"what would become modern day Lyons was equivalent to Rome's in every "
"way.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
