#. help page 355: title
msgctxt "help/355/0"
msgid "Reservoirs"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/355/1"
msgid ""
"Reservoirs provide the foundation of your city's water system. A "
"reservoir will only function when it is either adjacent to a river or "
"connected to a full reservoir via an aqueduct. You may create a string "
"of up to four reservoirs, but the outer reservoirs in the string will "
"be able to supply water to a smaller range than those closer to the "
"river.\n"
"\n"
"A reservoir creates a water supply in a small radius, and a zone of "
"pipe access in a wider radius. Houses built inside the water supply "
"radius will have water, and they will be happy.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1064Previous Page# / #40Next Page#\n"
"#356Tips# / #357History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 356: title
msgctxt "help/356/0"
msgid "Tips for Reservoirs and Aqueducts"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/356/1"
msgid ""
"The length of an aqueduct does not affect its performance -- only the "
"number of reservoirs supplied by it does.  However, longer aqueducts "
"require more plebeian maintenance. You can run several aqueducts off of "
"the same reservoir, to create a \"water tree\" that has branches in "
"different parts of the city. \n"
"\n"
"#355Help# / #357History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 357: title
msgctxt "help/357/0"
msgid "Roman Reservoirs"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/357/1"
msgid ""
"Until the fourth century BC, Rome's only sources of water were the "
"river Tiber and numerous ground wells.  As the city grew, however, the "
"native waters rapidly became choked with the city's refuse until they "
"became too polluted to drink. Another solution had to be found.  In 312 "
"BC, Appius Claudius Caecus built Rome's first aqueduct, which carried "
"water into Rome from a spring ten miles to the east.  The spring water "
"was collected in a reservoir in the city, to be doled out for public "
"use. \n"
"\n"
" Roman reservoirs, called castellum divisorium, were well-designed and "
"numerous.  One or two stories tall, they featured a large settling tank "
"where silt and \n"
"#358Next Page#\n"
"#355Help#/#356Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 358: title
msgctxt "help/358/0"
msgid "Roman Reservoirs (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/358/1"
msgid ""
"other solid debris were removed from the water, and a complex system of "
"sluices and taps that controlled the outflow of water to public "
"fountains and baths.  Wealthy Roman citizens could arrange to have "
"water piped directly to their houses from these reservoirs, for a "
"substantial fee paid to the city. As of 270 AD, Rome had around 250 of "
"these reservoirs in operation, ensuring a constant supply of clean, "
"fresh water to the city. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#357Previous Page#\n"
"#355Help#/#356Tips#"
msgstr ""

