#. help page 388: title
msgctxt "help/388/0"
msgid "Fountains"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/388/1"
msgid ""
"From ancient times to the present, Rome has been famous for its "
"magnificent fountains.  Numerous songs and stories have been inspired "
"by their constant flow.  In ancient Rome, the fountains served a double "
"purpose  as sources of fresh water for the public and as works of art.  "
"The fountains were linked to reservoirs by a pipe network usually made "
"of ceramic, though the much more expensive lead was infrequently used.  "
"Carved wooden pipes also sometimes made an appearance.   \n"
"\n"
"The oldest fountain in Rome is a natural spring, the Lacus Juturnae in "
"the Forum valley.  New fountains were built as they were needed, or as "
"they were \n"
"#387Previous Page#/#389Next Page#\n"
"#385Help#/#386Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 389: title
msgctxt "help/389/0"
msgid "The Fountains of Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/389/1"
msgid ""
"wanted -- fountains were often used as monuments to famous people or "
"events.  As of 270 AD, Rome had more than 1,200 public fountains.  \n"
"\n"
"One of the most famous fountains in Rome is the Trevi fountain, "
"originally constructed during ancient times but completely remodeled "
"and rebuilt in recent centuries.  This fountain, like many of Rome's "
"fountains, has a legend associated with it that says anyone who throws "
"a coin in the fountain is guaranteed a return to Rome.  Its waters come "
"from the Aqua Vergine, and have been considered the city's best-tasting "
"for centuries.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#388Previous Page#\n"
"#385Help#/#386Tips#"
msgstr ""

