#. help page 400: title
msgctxt "help/400/0"
msgid "Help with Baths"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/400/1"
msgid ""
"Be sure to build Bath Houses in areas that have pipe access, and are "
"near housing. Bath Houses raise the land values of surrounding areas, "
"and will raise your city's Culture rating. Bath Houses will develop and "
"improve in appearance as their land values go up.\n"
"\n"
"A Bath House provides sanitation to certain radius around it -- a "
"larger, more developed Bath House will serve a wider radius.\n"
"\n"
"Bath Houses do not need road access.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#401Tips# / #402History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 401: title
msgctxt "help/401/0"
msgid "Tips for Baths"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/401/1"
msgid ""
"Baths will expand their area of effect as their land value increases.  "
"When this happens, you may find some of your baths now overlap, so you "
"can move or remove some of them. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#400Help# / #402History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 402: title
msgctxt "help/402/0"
msgid "Roman Baths"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/402/1"
msgid ""
"The Baths of Caracalla\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#403Next Page#\n"
"#400Help# / #401Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 403: title
msgctxt "help/403/0"
msgid "Roman Baths"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/403/1"
msgid ""
"Roman citizens, patricians and plebs both, valued personal cleanliness "
"highly.  They achieved this efficiently by the creation and state "
"maintenance of lavish public bath houses where citizens could bathe as "
"many times a day as they liked.  As of 270 AD, Rome had 11 great public "
"baths called thermae and nearly a thousand lesser baths.  Emperors "
"found it useful to create huge and luxurious bath houses for public "
"use; the Baths of Caracalla (also called the Thermae of Antonius) "
"covered twenty seven acres, and those of Diocletian covered thirty two. "
" \n"
"As with many things they did, the Romans made an art out of necessity.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#402Previous Page# / #404Next Page#\n"
"#400Help# / #401Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 404: title
msgctxt "help/404/0"
msgid "Roman Baths (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/404/1"
msgid ""
"The bath wasn't just the place Romans went to get clean -- it was an "
"all-purpose community center. The biggest baths included shops, "
"gymnasiums, stadia, libraries, museums, art galleries, and other "
"facilities within their walls.  The baths were built out of high "
"quality stone or marble, and sumptuously decorated with carved statuary "
"and other art pieces. Slaves were present to tend to the slightest need "
"of the bathing citizens.  \n"
"\n"
"The structure of a Roman bath was fairly standardized; one would "
"generally find the same sorts of facilities from one bath to another, "
"although the layout could be very different.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#403Previous Page# / #405Next Page#\n"
"#400Help# / #401Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 405: title
msgctxt "help/405/0"
msgid "Roman Baths (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/405/1"
msgid ""
"The baths were segregated; either women and men used different baths "
"altogether, or in some cases the bath building itself was divided into "
"two parts, one for each gender. The baths featured several pools of "
"water at different temperatures, and the order in which they were to be "
"used was prescribed by tradition. First, the citizen stopped in the "
"apodyterium (changing room) to hang up his clothes and get a towel.  "
"Next, he went to the frigidarium (cold pool), then the tepidarium (warm "
"pool), then the caldarium (hot pool.)  If he felt so inclined, he could "
"then go on to the sudatorium (steam room.) Caracalla's baths also "
"included a central shower arrangement called a lavatio. Oddly enough, "
"at least one historian claims that the desirable order was the reverse "
"-- from hot pool to cold.  It may be that the desirable order changed "
"with the season of the year. \n"
"#404Previous Page# / #406Next Page#\n"
"#400Help# / #401Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 406: title
msgctxt "help/406/0"
msgid "Roman Baths (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/406/1"
msgid ""
"The warm and hot pools in the bath were heated by another innovation of "
"Roman engineering, the hypocaust.  The floor of the bath was actually "
"suspended several feet above ground on pillars of stone. The space "
"underneath held a charcoal-burning furnace, the praefurnium. (In one "
"house with a private bath, the bath was suspended over the bread oven "
"in an elegant, economical design.)  Air was blown past the furnace, and "
"open spaces under each of the pools determined how much heat would "
"reach the water, from none to as much as possible.  Also running "
"underneath the baths were the tunnels for the slaves, who could thus "
"move back and forth between the various parts of the baths without "
"disturbing the bathers.  The tunnels under the ruins of Caracalla's "
"baths are more than three miles long.\n"
"\n"
"#405Previous Page#\n"
"#400Help# / #401Tips#"
msgstr ""

