#. help page 445: title
msgctxt "help/445/0"
msgid "Help with Walls"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/445/1"
msgid ""
"Walls enclose areas to protect them from Barbarians. You must enclose "
"an area entirely in order to secure it. For purposes of enclosure, "
"rivers are equivalent to walls because Barbarians cannot cross them "
"without bridges.  Watch out for breaks in your walls!\n"
"\n"
"To  strengthen walls, you may build Towers. Towers serve as posts for "
"soldiers so that if invaders enter the city, the soldiers will emerge "
"to counterattack.\n"
"\n"
"To build a gate for a wall, just run a section of road through an "
"existing wall, or vice versa. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#446Tips# / #447History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 446: title
msgctxt "help/446/0"
msgid "Tips for Walls"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/446/1"
msgid ""
"Remember that you can use the natural defenses of a river to keep "
"enemies out of the city; building a city within the gentle curve of a "
"river can save a lot on wall construction costs. The benefits of "
"External Security take a long time to be recognized by your city.  If "
"you don't get External Security after building a wall, wait a while.  "
"If you still don't get External Security, or if disappears after you "
"got it, check your wall for breaks or areas that have collapsed. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#445Help# / #447History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 447: title
msgctxt "help/447/0"
msgid "City Walls of Rome"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/447/1"
msgid ""
"The presence of city walls around a Roman town presented something of a "
"paradox.  One the one hand, a wall around the town meant that the "
"residents could expect greater safety in case of trouble; on the other "
"hand, a wall wasn't necessary unless the province was disorderly or "
"under serious attack already, something that happened only rarely while "
"the Empire was at its height.  To a certain extent, then, the decline "
"of the Empire can be charted  by the walls the Roman citizens required "
"around their homes. \n"
"\n"
" Rome itself was walled three times, not counting the original "
"fortifications built around the Palatine Hill \n"
"#448Next Page#\n"
"#445Help#/#446Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 448: title
msgctxt "help/448/0"
msgid "City Walls of Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/448/1"
msgid ""
"when it was settled with no more than a cluster of huts.  The first "
"wall was built around 390 BC, in response to the Gaul invasion that "
"sacked the city around that time.  This wall has been misnamed the "
"Servian Wall, due to the later Roman belief that it was built around "
"the time of the Etruscan king Servius Tullius, which proved to be "
"incorrect.  The Servian Wall was  five and a half miles long and built "
"across the crests of the seven hills of Rome, enclosing the forum "
"valley.  \n"
"\n"
" After the Gaul invasion, Rome had no need of a wall for another six "
"hundred years.  The building of the Aurelian Wall between 272 and 280 "
"AD was a sign of the slow but sure decline of the Empire; it was "
"constructed in response to the barbarian invasion of 271. The Emperor "
"Aurelian built the wall around the \n"
"#447Previous Page#/#449Next Page#\n"
"#445Help#/#446Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 449: title
msgctxt "help/449/0"
msgid "City Walls of Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/449/1"
msgid ""
"entirety of Rome's seven hills as well as a fair bit of land on the far "
"northern bank of the Tiber.  It was eleven and a half miles in length, "
"enclosed an area of roughly five and a half square miles, and was "
"between twenty-five and thirty feet tall all around.  It featured "
"strenghthening towers with defensive capability every thirty-two yards, "
"and was made of concrete faced with brick.  The construction was so "
"sound that most of the wall is still intact to this day, although some "
"of its longevity can be attributed to the rebuilding of the wall in 402 "
"by the Emperor Honorious, the third wall-builder of Rome.  Honorious "
"added more towers to the wall, to make a total of 381 of the structures "
"around the city.  Unfortunately, by that point Rome's decline was not "
"going to be contained by the building of a big wall.  It was mostly "
"useless in preventing repeated barbarian \n"
"#448Previous Page#/#450Next Page#\n"
"#445Help#/#446Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 450: title
msgctxt "help/450/0"
msgid "City Walls of Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/450/1"
msgid ""
"sackings of the town in the fifth century AD.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
