#. help page 460: title
msgctxt "help/460/0"
msgid "Praefectures"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/460/1"
msgid ""
"Praefectures send out vigiles, or watchmen, who patrol the city in "
"search of rioters and fires. A strong vigile presence will help prevent "
"riots before they start (fires may be prevented by assigning Plebs to "
"fire duty). If a fire or a riot does break out, the vigiles will take "
"care of it automatically as soon as they discover the problem. Like "
"soldiers, vigiles will enhance the security of any area they walk past. "
"Like Barracks, Praefectures are undesirable to live near, so they limit "
"land value. Make sure that there is good road access between your "
"praefectures and your housing.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#461Tips# / #462History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 461: title
msgctxt "help/461/0"
msgid "Tips for Praefectures"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/461/1"
msgid ""
"Prafectures are small.  Building more than one of them along a single "
"road can improve their coverage of an area. If a fire has already "
"broken out, and you did not prepare for it by building praefectures, an "
"expensive solution is to build a group of them near the fire.  Make "
"sure that the praefectures are next to roads -- or else they can't send "
"out vigiles -- and make sure they aren't so close to the fire that they "
"catch fire themselves! \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#460Help# / #462History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 462: title
msgctxt "help/462/0"
msgid "Praefectures"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/462/1"
msgid ""
"The threat of fire was easily the greatest danger to the citizens of "
"Rome. Although the insulae had stone bases, the upper floors were "
"mostly timber. Since all lighting, heating, and cooking came from open "
"flames, fires were common and inevitable. Once ignited, a fire could "
"easily jump the narrow streets and the gaps between insulae, to spread "
"unchecked throughout the city. \n"
"\n"
" Until the reign of the Emperor Augustus, Rome had nothing resembling a "
"fire department. The wealthy and unscrupulous Roman general, Marcus "
"Licinius Crassus, organized a private corps of men trained to fight "
"fires during the last years of the Republic, but \n"
"#463Next Page#\n"
"#460Help#/#461Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 463: title
msgctxt "help/463/0"
msgid "Praefectures"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/463/1"
msgid ""
"they were not precisely public servants. When a fire broke out, Crassus "
"would accompany his men to the scene and make the distraught owner an "
"outrageously low offer for the burning building. If the owner accepted, "
"Crassus' men would extinguish the fire; if not, the building would be "
"allowed to burn to the ground.\n"
"\n"
" Julius Caesar tried to do something about the problem by decreeing "
"that all buildings had to be roofed with non-flammable tiles, and that "
"they had to be built at least three feet apart.  But it fell to his "
"successor, Augustus, to organize an official fire brigade for the city, "
"paid for from state funds. Augustus' workers, known as vigiles, were "
"stationed in buildings called praefectures.  In addition to answering "
"fire alarms, they also served as night watchmen in general, at \n"
"#462Previous Page#/#464Next Page#\n"
"#460Help#/#461Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 464: title
msgctxt "help/464/0"
msgid "Praefectures"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/464/1"
msgid ""
"least for the better neighborhoods. There were seven squads of vigiles, "
"each with 1,000 men. Despite their vital function, the were held in low "
"esteem by many citizens, who called them \"Bucketmen.\"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#463Previous Page#\n"
"#460Help#/#461Tips#"
msgstr ""

