#. help page 490: title
msgctxt "help/490/0"
msgid "Theaters"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/490/1"
msgid ""
"Theaters where plays, operas, and other shows are performed come in two "
"sizes: the basic Theater, and the Odeum. An Odeum will reach a larger "
"area than the basic Theater. Neither variety needs road access.\n"
"\n"
"Each will boost land values and, in a full game, will improve your "
"Culture Rating.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#491Tips# / #492History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 491: title
msgctxt "help/491/0"
msgid "Tips for Entertainment"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/491/1"
msgid ""
"When you have access to improved entertainment, you should consider "
"\"upgrading\" the ones you have already built.  You can easily replace "
"your old ones with odeums and coliseums, as they take up the same "
"space.  On the other hand, the Circus Maximus is larger than the "
"Circus, so you plan to clear extra space for it.\n"
"\n"
"If you can't afford to build the more expensive forms of entertainment, "
"you should still prepare space for them in your city.  For example, if "
"you try to arrange as many city blocks around a clearing that will "
"eventually hold a circus, than you won't need to build so many "
"circuses. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#490Help# / #492History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 492: title
msgctxt "help/492/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/492/1"
msgid ""
"The Maritime Theater at Hadrian's Villa\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#493Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 493: title
msgctxt "help/493/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/493/1"
msgid ""
"Although theaters had long been wildly popular in Greece, they were "
"slower to catch on in Rome due to Senatorial resistance. Convinced that "
"the theater was an undignified pastime for a proper Roman, the Senate "
"decreed that all theaters had to be built as temporary structures and "
"were not allowed to have seating for the audience.  The first stone "
"theater was constructed by Pompey in 55 BC, who got around the "
"Senatorial proscription by placing a small shrine to Venus Victrix "
"above the seating area so that the theater could be considered a "
"temple.  \n"
"\n"
" With the dawn of the Imperial age, the prohibition against permanent "
"theaters was dropped. The \n"
"#492Previous Page#/#494Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 494: title
msgctxt "help/494/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/494/1"
msgid ""
"Emperors had little concern for maintaining the dignity of the average "
"Roman, much preferring to have him amused and quiet.  By the height of "
"the Empire, Rome had three major public theaters which could hold "
"around 50,000 spectators each, plus many smaller theaters. In a "
"turnaround of opinion, theaters became the entertainment of the "
"dignified Roman, who looked down his long Roman nose at the rowdier "
"pleasures to be had in the circus and arena.  \n"
" The better plays performed in the Roman theaters were those by the "
"Greek playwrights; Roman writers generally produced plays with a lot of "
"fighting and melodrama, the ancient world's equivalent of the action "
"movie.  Some of these plays featured the brutal practice of actually "
"slaying a live victim  (always a helpless slave, of course) when an "
"on-stage \n"
"#493Previous Page#/#495Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 495: title
msgctxt "help/495/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/495/1"
msgid ""
"death was called for. The formal theater used only male actors, but "
"both men and women performed in less formal presentations, and Rome had "
"several famous actresses with hordes of male admirers.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#494Previous Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""

