#. help page 523: title
msgctxt "help/523/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/523/1"
msgid ""
"Unlike the theater and the circus, which were designed and used for "
"specific activities, the Arena was an all-purpose sporting stadium "
"where a wide variety of events, usually violent and bloody, were staged "
"for the public's amusement.  The arena was used for gladiatorial "
"combats, wild animal fights, war games, and atheletic competitions. It "
"could even be filled with water and used to stage small-scale naval "
"battles.  \n"
"\n"
" The most famous arena in Rome is, of course, the Colosseum.  This huge "
"building was constructed at the end of the forum valley, between the "
"Palatine and Esquiline hills, on a site where Nero had put an \n"
"#522Previous Page#/#524 Next Page#\n"
"#520Help#/#521Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 524: title
msgctxt "help/524/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/524/1"
msgid ""
"artifical lake to go with his much-hated Golden Palace.  As a sign of "
"conciliation with the public, Nero's eventual successor Vespasian (who "
"took the Imperium after a series of bloody civil wars) drained the lake "
"and commissioned the building of the Colosseum for the explicit purpose "
"of providing free games for the public.  He died before it was "
"completed, however, and it was his son and sucessor Titus who "
"inaugerated the building in 80 AD.  \n"
"\n"
" The Colosseum, more properly known as the Flavian Amphitheater, had "
"three stories of seating plus an \"attic\" level.  It measured a third of "
"a mile around, and could seat 45,000, with room for another 5,000 "
"standing spectators.  It also featured a canvas awning, manned by "
"experienced sailors, which could be extended to give the spectators "
"some protection from \n"
"#523Previous Page#/#525 Next Page#\n"
"#520Help#/#521Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 525: title
msgctxt "help/525/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/525/1"
msgid ""
"sun and rain.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
