#. help page 580: title
msgctxt "help/580/0"
msgid "Grammaticus"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/580/1"
msgid ""
"The Grammaticus is a school for young children (older children attend "
"the Rhetor). Housing needs schools to develop -- a lack of schools acts "
"as a Limiting Factor on development.\n"
"\n"
"A Grammaticus and a Rhetor will serve a surrounding radius, so each "
"pocket of housing may need its own schools.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#581Tips# / #582History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 581: title
msgctxt "help/581/0"
msgid "Tips for Education"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/581/1"
msgid ""
"Your city won't be able to produce the best housing until you can build "
"a library.  Make sure you build a large enough population so that the "
"library becomes available.\n"
"\n"
"If you find areas housing fluctuating between two levels regularly, it "
"may be because your population is growing to a level that your "
"libraries can't cover well, which causes land value for some houses to "
"be limited.  When these houses shrink, it lowers the population, which "
"improves the coverage, which causes the houses to grow, which lowers "
"the coverage... and so on.  When this happens, build another library. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#580Help# / #582History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 582: title
msgctxt "help/582/0"
msgid "Education in Rome"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/582/1"
msgid ""
"Romans as a whole were a well-educated people, although their area of "
"knowledge was narrow and tightly constrained by what was considered "
"\"proper\" for a Roman citizen to study. Writing, debate, and philosophy "
"qualified; the more technical fields of knowledge such as science, "
"medicine, and the higher maths did not.\n"
"\n"
"Literacy was more common in ancient Rome than at any other time before "
"the nineteenth century.  All of the patrician class and many of the "
"upper middle-class plebeians had their children schooled.  Children "
"started their studies at age seven, usually being tutored by a teacher "
"or an educated Greek slave \n"
"#583Next Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 583: title
msgctxt "help/583/0"
msgid "Education in Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/583/1"
msgid ""
"in someone's home or in a public location, which might even be "
"outdoors. Boys and girls were both taught, although in separate "
"classrooms. Early studies primarily covered reading and writing in both "
"Latin and Greek. Discipline was harsh, and beatings for misbehavior "
"were common.  \n"
"\n"
"At age twelve or thirteen, boys went to the Grammaticus, a school where "
"literature, history, elocution and some elementary mathematics "
"(arithmetic and geometry) were taught. Girls might also go the "
"Grammaticus, though less frequently than their brothers, to be taught "
"poetry and music.  Few if any girls advanced to the next level of "
"school, the Rhetor, where students practiced the skills they would need "
"for life as an upper-class Roman: public speaking, debate, and law.  "
"After completing studies \n"
"#582Previous Page#/#584Next Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 584: title
msgctxt "help/584/0"
msgid "Education in Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/584/1"
msgid ""
"at the Rhetor, a particularly bright or rich student might travel to "
"Athens to study with the famous Greek teachers there. The first Roman "
"\"university\", known as the Athenaeum, was built by the Emperor Hadrian "
"in the second century AD.\n"
"\n"
" Rome also had many libraries, public and private, where scrolls, "
"parchment, and papyrus were stored in wall niches.  By the end of the "
"Empire, there were around forty public libraries and hundreds more "
"private ones in the city. They were built according to a standard floor "
"plan, featuring two rectangular rooms, one for Latin works and one for "
"Greek ones. These libraries functioned much as modern ones do, even "
"extending borrowing privileges to members.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#583Previous Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""

