#. help page 640: title
msgctxt "help/640/0"
msgid "Hospitals"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/640/1"
msgid ""
"Hospitals serve the entire city, and not just the areas near them. Your "
"city's Hospital Coverage is expressed as a percentage, and is displayed "
"in each building's Query panel. If your Hospital Cover dips below 100 "
"percent, housing throughout your city may become diseased. In this "
"case, you should build another hospital.\n"
"\n"
"Hospitals need road access. They also must be within areas that have "
"tax coverage by a Forum, so that Forum officials may administer the "
"hospital.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#641Tips# / #642History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 641: title
msgctxt "help/641/0"
msgid "Tips for Hospitals"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/641/1"
msgid ""
"If you find areas of housing fluctuating between two levels regularly, "
"it may be because your population is growing to a level that your "
"hospitals can't cover well, which causes land value for some houses to "
"be limited.  When these houses shrink, it lowers the population, which "
"improves the coverage, which causes the houses to grow, which lowers "
"the coverage... and so on.  When this happens, build another hospital. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#640Help# / #642History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 642: title
msgctxt "help/642/0"
msgid "Roman Health"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/642/1"
msgid ""
"Given the technological restrictions of the ancient world, the city of "
"Rome was a remarkably clean and healthful place to live.  The Romans "
"highly valued cleanliness, both personal and civic.  City cleanliness "
"was accomplished through Roman civil engineering, while personal "
"cleanliness was provided for by means of public bath houses.  \n"
"\n"
" Rome's site on the river Tiber is not a particularly good location in "
"which to build a city.  The hills are steep, and the valleys between "
"them are marshy and have a tendency to flood when the river is high.  "
"The marshes, with their bountiful malarial mosquito population, "
"presented the greatest threat to Roman \n"
"#643Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 643: title
msgctxt "help/643/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/643/1"
msgid ""
"health. The Romans, of course, didn't understand where malaria came "
"from, but they could see that swamps weren't healthy places to live.  "
"Accordingly, they built huge drains to channel water away from the land "
"(the Cloaca Maxima being the oldest example still in existence), and "
"created the Pool of the Tiber to control river flooding.   They also "
"improved their chances for health by importing fresh spring water via "
"aqueducts, rather than drinking from the polluted river.  In addition, "
"most of Rome had a well-developed sewer system,  (sometimes "
"underground, sometimes just as street channels to take away refuse) "
"except for the poorer quarters, where people simply threw refuse into "
"the streets.  There were also more than 150 well-drained public "
"lavatories. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#642Previous Page#/#644Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 644: title
msgctxt "help/644/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/644/1"
msgid ""
"Rome was a much healthier place to live under the Imperium than at any "
"time during the Middle Ages or Renaissance, and many of the other "
"cities in Italy and elsewhere in the Imperium also benefited from Roman "
"knowledge of sanitation.  Given the basic lack of knowledge about "
"disease causes, however, the threat of plague could never be eliminated "
"entirely, particularly in the crowded conditions of Rome, and the "
"mobility provided by the Roman road system made it easy for foreigners "
"to bring new diseases to Italy from far-off lands. Disease struck the "
"city several times in its history with the usual severity of ancient "
"plagues; in one outbreak during the reign of Marcus Aurelius, it was "
"estimated that 2,000 people died every day. \n"
"\n"
" Public hospitals were provided, but medical science, \n"
"#643Previous Page#/#645Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 645: title
msgctxt "help/645/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/645/1"
msgid ""
"like all other sciences under the Romans, except civil engineering and "
"animal husbandry, stagnated -- the health care available at the end of "
"the Imperium was not much better than that found at the start of the "
"Republic.  Proper Romans also did not consider a physician's work to be "
"dignified enough for a true Roman to undertake; ergo, nearly all the "
"doctors in the city were Greek, some of them slaves. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#644Previous Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""

