#. help page 655: title
msgctxt "help/655/0"
msgid "Help with Markets"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/655/1"
msgid ""
"Markets allow commerce to take place in your city. Markets send out "
"traders who use the city's road system to seek out potential customers. "
"In addition to the general business they do, markets will also sell the "
"goods produced by businesses in the city. Markets will have little "
"colored awnings that represent their trade. The busier the market, the "
"more awnings you will see around it, so its performance is apparent at "
"a glance.\n"
"\n"
"To succeed, markets need good road access, and a steady supply of goods "
"from city businesses. Although residents need access, they do not like "
"being too close to markets -- this tends to limit land values. \n"
"\n"
"#656Tips# / #657History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 656: title
msgctxt "help/656/0"
msgid "Tips for Markets"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/656/1"
msgid ""
"Markets will limit nearby land values, but not nearly as low as a "
"business or barracks.  If you are only trying to build \"middle-class\" "
"housing, don't worry about where you put your markets. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#655Help# / #657History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 657: title
msgctxt "help/657/0"
msgid "Industry in Rome"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/657/1"
msgid ""
"The city of Rome itself had little or no real industry.  Its wealth "
"sprang more from its central location in Italy (which made it a natural "
"center for trade), and from its position as the administrative capital "
"of the Empire than from any goods it could produce. The only real "
"industry in Rome centered around the necessity of providing food for "
"Rome's one million consumers. The rest of Rome's industry consisted of "
"thousands of small shopkeepers and artisans, usually operating out of "
"the first floor of some insula.  \n"
"\n"
"Rome had many specialized wholesale markets which sold only a single "
"commodity, and each had its own name. The holitorium sold vegetables, "
"the boarium \n"
"#658Next Page#\n"
"#655Help#/#656Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 658: title
msgctxt "help/658/0"
msgid "Industry in Rome (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/658/1"
msgid ""
"sold cattle, the suarium sold pigs, the vinarium sold wine, and so on. "
"There were also numerous general-purpose markets, usually permanent "
"buildings covered to keep the foodstuffs from spoiling quickly in the "
"direct sunlight. These markets could become quite grand -- Trajan's "
"Market featured three tiers of shops and a great covered market hall, "
"surely a close cousin to the modern-day shopping mall. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#657Previous Page#\n"
"#655Help#/#656Tips#"
msgstr ""

