#. help page 865: title
msgctxt "help/865/0"
msgid "Provincial Walls"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/865/1"
msgid ""
"Provincial Wall construction works in the same way as City Wall "
"construction on the City Level. You may enclose all of your Province's "
"exposed borders, or you may enclose only part of your Province.\n"
"\n"
"But be aware: if you separate economic sources (Farms, Quarries, etc.) "
"or towns from your city, their output will be diminished. All sources "
"within your Province want to be protected, so you should avoid dividing "
"your province.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#866Tips# / #867History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 866: title
msgctxt "help/866/0"
msgid "Tips for Provincial Walls"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/866/1"
msgid ""
"If you think that you can complete a province without using certain "
"distant meadows or rocky areas, then feel free to leave them outside of "
"your provincial walls.\n"
"\n"
"Use mountains as natural barriers to save on the cost of provincial "
"walls.  Build walls across valleys which enemies tend to approach from. "
" Also build gates in provincial walls, to that cohorts can get to "
"either side of the wall as needed.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#865Help# / #867History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 867: title
msgctxt "help/867/0"
msgid "Provincial Walls"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/867/1"
msgid ""
"The Romans often built long and impressive walls in newly-conquered "
"territories to help hold back the encroaching barbarian hordes. These "
"walls existed in many frontier provinces, but the most famous example "
"is Hadrian's Wall, built in 128 AD. This stone barrier, which bisects "
"England from Carlisle to Newcastle-on-Tyne, is seventy-six miles long, "
"twenty feet high, and eight feet thick. In front of the wall the Romans "
"dug a V-shaped ditch thirty feet wide at the top. Fourteen forts "
"holding a total of about a thousand soldiers were built into it at "
"regular intervals. This wall helped to keep the rampaging Picts and "
"Celts from overrunning Roman settlements to the south.  \n"
"\n"
"#868Next Page#\n"
"#865Help#/#866Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 868: title
msgctxt "help/868/0"
msgid "Roman Provincial Walls (cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/868/1"
msgid ""
"A second more modest wall was built even further north in Britain by "
"Antoninus Pius, Hadrian's successor. Less is known about this "
"structure, since it was made of blocks of turf instead of stone and "
"thus has not withstood the wear of the ages the way Hadrian's wall has. "
"Antoninus Pius' wall ran across Scotland, from the Firth of Forth to "
"the Clyde. It may have been more typical of Roman provinical walls than "
"Hadrian's creation; provincial walls in general were meant to last only "
"until the province had been thoroughly pacified and its borders "
"secured.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#867Previous Page#\n"
"#865Help#/#866Tips#"
msgstr ""

