#. help page 1000: title
msgctxt "help/1000/0"
msgid "Help with Ports"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1000/1"
msgid ""
"Ports provide trade access to lands overseas. When you build a port, it "
"may do business with the province indicated by the white arrow on its "
"side of your Province Map. A Port needs a Warehouse, but does not need "
"a Work Camp. Goods brought into the port will remain in its Warehouse "
"until it is either used by a City Level business, or sent to the "
"Emperor at his request.\n"
"Each ship you see in the ocean on your Province map carries a specific "
"cargo load. Ships come into a port, unload their cargo, and leave. You "
"may right-click on any ship to see what type of cargo it is carrying. "
"When you build a Shipyard adjacent to a Port, it increases the number "
"of ships plying your waters, and so will boost your trade. \n"
"\n"
"#1001Tips# / #1002History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1001: title
msgctxt "help/1001/0"
msgid "Tips for Ports"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1001/1"
msgid ""
"Before you build a port, look to see where ships first hit the coast of "
"your province.  You may want to eventually build ports on either side "
"of this point, to ensure you \"catch\" all the ships that come from a sea "
"lane. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1000Help# / #1002History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1002: title
msgctxt "help/1002/0"
msgid "Roman Ports"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1002/1"
msgid ""
"Since the Roman Empire surrounded and controlled the entirety of the "
"Mediterranean sea, it was natural that much of its trade should go by "
"ship. Roman ports were as well-engineered as everything else they "
"built. Each port had several wharves, divided up by type of trade.  "
"Near the wharves were the horrea, warehouses for goods. Some warehouses "
"were specialized for one type of good, but most held a diverse variety "
"of commodities. \n"
"\n"
" The city of Ostia, at the mouth of the Tiber, was Rome's port. It "
"featured more than three hundred horrea, the largest one 218 by 169 "
"yards in size. The port had to be refurbished and rebuilt several times \n"
"#1003Next Page#\n"
"#1000Help#/#1001Tips#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1003: title
msgctxt "help/1003/0"
msgid "History of Ports (Cont..)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1003/1"
msgid ""
"during its history, as the Tiber brought a large amount of silt down "
"from the Appenines which tended to clog the port and render it "
"unusable. The volume of dirt carried downstream to the river's mouth is "
"so large that today Ostia, once right on the sea, is now two miles from "
"the shore.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1002Previous Page#\n"
"#1000Help#/#1001Tips#"
msgstr ""

