#. help page 1201: title
msgctxt "help/1201/0"
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1201/1"
msgid ""
"\"In times long past lived the twin brothers Romulus and Remus. Born of "
"a union between Zeus and a Vestal Virgin, they were set adrift in a "
"basket on the river Tiber by their evil uncle. Their divine father "
"watched over the children, however, and sent a wolf and a woodpecker to "
"feed and care for them. Eventually, they were rescued by a shepherd, "
"and when they were grown, they became great warriors and leaders of "
"their people. \n"
"\n"
"\"One day the twins decided to build a great city on the site where "
"their basket washed ashore. But the brothers could not agree on whose "
"name the city should bear. In their anger, they fell to fighting, and "
"Remus was slain. Then Romulus went forth and founded the great city "
"that bears his name: Rome.\" \n"
"\n"
"#1200Previous Page# / #1202 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1202: title
msgctxt "help/1202/0"
msgid "Introduction (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1202/1"
msgid ""
"The #1980legend of Romulus and Remus# is the history of Rome as the "
"Romans themselves knew it, full of the drama of family murders, gods, "
"destinies, and omens. The difficulty for modern scholars lies, as "
"always, in gleaning solid historical fact from the gauzy stuff of myth "
"and legend.\n"
"\n"
"The histories written by the ancient Romans themselves give a lopsided "
"view of their own time, for Roman historians labored under an "
"impossible double burden. First, they had no objective historical "
"records to speak of -- their materials, particularly for the earliest "
"periods of Rome's history, were little more than some lists of public "
"officials plus the local folklore. Second, they were under enormous "
"pressure to make their histories acceptable to the political powers of "
"the time in which they wrote. Thus, the \n"
"#1201Previous Page# / #1203 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1203: title
msgctxt "help/1203/0"
msgid "Introduction (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1203/1"
msgid ""
"works of Roman authors such as Livy (writing around 25 BC), Tacitus "
"(writing around 100 AD), and Suetonius (writing around 120 AD) have to "
"be viewed more as historical novels, rather than anything more "
"objective.\n"
"\n"
"The separation of truth and falsehood in these accounts is a very "
"difficult task. Endless debates have taken place (and continue to "
"occur) among historians as to what parts of which work can be "
"considered accurate, and which are mistaken or outright fictional. "
"Ancient historians of other countries added to the confusion by "
"adjusting Rome's history to suit their own ideas. Archaeological "
"studies, which provide an unbiased (if skeletal) source of information "
"on the Roman world, have been hampered by the presence of modern cities "
"on top of \n"
"#1202Previous Page# / #1204 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1204: title
msgctxt "help/1204/0"
msgid "Introduction (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1204/1"
msgid ""
"many of the most interesting ancient sites. \n"
"\n"
"Historians are unable even to agree on #1990when the \"Roman Empire\" "
"began and ended.# Faced with these limitations, it's no wonder that the "
"modern view of Rome, Romans, and the Empire has undergone numerous "
"revisions on the way to today's accepted historical \"truth,\" and will "
"probably continue to change in the future as more information is "
"brought to light.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1203Previous Page# / #1800 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1205: title
msgctxt "help/1205/0"
msgid "Rome Before the Empire"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1205/1"
msgid ""
"By the time Rome first appeared in the histories of the ancient world "
"around 280 BC, it was already master of the entire Italian peninsula. "
"Information on the development of pre-Imperial Rome before this time is "
"scarce and fragmentary, but a few things are fairly certain. When Rome "
"was first settled, the Tiber river was the dividing line between the "
"Etrurian kingdom to the northwest and the Latin settlements to the "
"southwest. The river's source lay in the Apennine mountains, home of "
"the Sabine peoples to the northeast and the Samnites to the southeast. "
"At one point twelve miles upriver from the sea the Tiber splits around "
"a small island; this location made an ideal river crossing. Such a "
"crossing was a natural location for the meeting of the various tribes "
"and the \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1800Previous Page# / #1206 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1206: title
msgctxt "help/1206/0"
msgid "Rome Before the Empire (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1206/1"
msgid ""
"creation of a settlement of mixed population.\n"
"\n"
"Around the same time that Latin shepherds were building huts on the "
"Palatine hill, Sabine shepherds were doing the same thing over on the "
"Quirinal hill. One legend of early Rome says that the Latins discovered "
"at one point that they had a shortage of wives. To solve this problem, "
"they invited the Sabines to a festival. When the Sabines arrived, the "
"Latins promptly kidnapped their women and headed back to the Palatine "
"hill. The Sabines made preparations to reclaim their women, but before "
"the war could get underway they received word that the women were happy "
"with their new husbands and preferred to stay. This story is probably "
"not true, but it may well be a metaphor for a (relatively) peaceful "
"merging of the two tribes into one -- the Romans.\n"
"\n"
"#1205Previous Page# / #1207 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1207: title
msgctxt "help/1207/0"
msgid "Monarchy and Republic"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1207/1"
msgid ""
"Rome's first government was an Etruscan-style monarchy about which "
"little is known. Roman legends say that there were seven kings in "
"total, beginning with Romulus, then followed by Numa Pompilius, Tullus "
"Hostilius, Ancus Marcius, Lucius Tarquinius Priscus, Servius Tullius, "
"and Lucius Tarquinus Superbus. While Romulus (in his legendary form) "
"was fictional, the six other named kings probably existed, although the "
"various acts attributed to their names are questionable.\n"
"\n"
"The reign of the kings of Rome ended in 509 BC. According to official "
"Roman history, the tyrannical behavior of the king Lucius Tarquinus "
"Superbus provoked his overthrow by the Roman citizens, who swore that "
"never again would they be ruled by a king. Historians now believe that "
"the ending of the \n"
"#1206Previous Page# / #1208 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1208: title
msgctxt "help/1208/0"
msgid "Monarchy and Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1208/1"
msgid ""
"Roman monarchy was more of an accident. Around the time of Tarquinus "
"Superbus's ouster, Rome was at war with another local king, Porsenna of "
"Clusium. Porsenna attacked the city, forcing Tarquinus Superbus to "
"flee. The conqueror did not have the strength to hold the city, "
"however, and was chased out himself by the local populace. Instead of "
"inviting their departed ruler back, the kingless Romans decided to try "
"a different system of government instead. Thus the Roman Republic was "
"born.\n"
"\n"
"By the time of this dramatic shift, Roman society was already deeply "
"divided between two classes: the Patricians, who were the wealthy "
"land-owning class, and the rest of the city's residents, known as the "
"Plebs. The patricians formed into a law-making body called the Senate "
"(from the Latin word for \"senior\"), \n"
"#1207Previous Page# / #1209 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1209: title
msgctxt "help/1209/0"
msgid "Monarchy and Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1209/1"
msgid ""
"and elected two co-rulers, called consuls, to serve as executives for a "
"term of one year. Consuls were always elected from among the members of "
"the Senate.\n"
"\n"
"Rule by the Senate was an improvement over rule by a single man, but it "
"still left most of the city's population without any influence in legal "
"matters. The Senate began abusing its privileges almost immediately, "
"making laws that benefited their small minority at the expense of "
"everyone else in the city. Eventually the plebs became unhappy enough "
"to take action, and staged a city-wide walkout and strike. Since plebs "
"made up most of the army, the patricians had no choice but to give in "
"to their demands for a say in running the city. A new office was "
"created, called the Tribune of the People. The tribune was \n"
"#1208Previous Page# / #1210 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1210: title
msgctxt "help/1210/0"
msgid "Monarchy and Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1210/1"
msgid ""
"elected by the plebs, and had the power to veto any law passed by the "
"Senate. This still restricted the legal power of the plebs -- they "
"could not propose any laws, only veto those they disliked -- but it was "
"a major step forward regardless.\n"
"\n"
"The internal political struggles continued for another hundred years, "
"until the plebs finally won the right to hold any public office. A "
"number of new positions had been added by then, to help deal with the "
"administration of the growing city: praetors, in charge of civil "
"jurisdiction; quaestors, in charge of criminal matters; aediles, who "
"supervised public works and the markets; and censors, who kept track of "
"the city's population and supervised public morals.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1209Previous Page# / #1211 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1211: title
msgctxt "help/1211/0"
msgid "Rome Conquers Italy"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1211/1"
msgid ""
"During that same hundred years, Rome was making strides toward becoming "
"a real power in Italy. Shortly after the formation of the Republic, the "
"Romans organized the Latin League, a coalition of neighboring Latin "
"cities committed to mutual defense against outside enemies. In 396 BC "
"Rome defeated and destroyed her only major rival on the Tiber, the "
"Etruscan city of Veii. In a solemn ceremony regarded by Romans as one "
"of the most terrible punishments possible, the Roman priests performed "
"the evocatio on Veii -- a ritual designed to force the city's "
"protecting gods to leave Veii and take up residence in Rome instead.\n"
"\n"
"Six years later, Rome got her first taste of defeat at the hands of a "
"surprise Gallic invasion from the north. The Gauls stormed down the "
"Italian peninsula, \n"
"#1210Previous Page# / #1212 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1212: title
msgctxt "help/1212/0"
msgid "Rome Conquers Italy (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1212/1"
msgid ""
"wreaking havoc as they went, until they reached Rome, defeated her "
"garrison, and sacked and burned the city. Fortunately, the Romans were "
"able to scrape enough gold together to bribe the Gauls to go away and "
"leave them in peace, but the shocking event appears to have had a major "
"impact on Roman psychology. After the Gallic sack of the city, the "
"Romans became even more military, aggressive, and paranoid toward "
"neighboring nations than before. Throughout their long history, "
"however, the Romans maintained that they engaged only in \"just wars\" -- "
"wars that were forced upon them by outside aggression, and were "
"therefore favored by the gods. In order to have every war they desired "
"be considered \"just\", the Romans became experts at manipulating their "
"chosen targets into provoking war. Nearly any excuse would do, when the "
"Romans \n"
"#1211Previous Page# / #1213 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1213: title
msgctxt "help/1213/0"
msgid "Rome Conquers Italy (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1213/1"
msgid ""
"needed a reason.\n"
"\n"
"The Etruscan civilization to the north had already been weakened by the "
"growing strength of Rome and her allies; the Gallic invasion dealt "
"Etruria the final blow, and a new tribe, the Samnites, rose to "
"prominence in their place. The Samnites and the Romans clashed almost "
"immediately. The first war was decided in Rome's favor, but a new "
"problem arose in its wake. Rome's allies in the Latin League, who had "
"contributed men and resources to the war effort, felt they had not "
"gotten a fair share of the rewards of victory. They demanded to be "
"treated as equals. After a brief but bitter set of skirmishes known as "
"the Latin War, Rome gave in and granted the coveted right of Roman "
"citizenship to all residents of its allied cities. Rome then went back "
"to \n"
"#1212Previous Page# / #1214 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1214: title
msgctxt "help/1214/0"
msgid "Rome Conquers Italy (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1214/1"
msgid ""
"the problem of the Samnites, and over the next fifty years gradually "
"pushed them further and further north until Rome controlled Italy all "
"the way to the Po river valley.\n"
"\n"
"In 290 a Greek general named Pyrrhus answered a call for help from the "
"Greek cities on Italy's south shore, who were feeling understandably "
"nervous about the expansion of Roman control. Pyrrhus engaged the "
"Romans in Campania, the southern plain of Italy, and won two victories "
"against them -- but at so great a cost that the Greek general lamented, "
"\"Another such victory and I shall be undone,\" giving rise to the phrase "
"\"Pyrhhic victory.\" After a third battle, which he lost, Pyrrhus gave up "
"and went home to Greece. The Romans now controlled all of the Italian "
"peninsula.\n"
"\n"
"#1213Previous Page# / #1215 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1215: title
msgctxt "help/1215/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1215/1"
msgid ""
"THE PUNIC WARS\n"
"\n"
"Picture: A Carthaginian warship\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1214Previous Page# / #1216 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1216: title
msgctxt "help/1216/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1216/1"
msgid ""
"The supreme power in the Mediterranean in the early years of the third "
"century BC was the Phoenician city of Kar-Hadasht, now known as "
"Carthage. The Phoenicians, or \"Puns\", as the Romans called them, were "
"unparalleled shipbuilders and sailors, and controlled all of the sea "
"trade of the Mediterranean and a fair way beyond. With a straight-line "
"distance of less than four hundred miles between the two great cities, "
"both eager to expand their power, a clash was inevitable.\n"
"\n"
"The trouble began, as might have been predicted, on the island of "
"Sicily, strategically poised between the two powers. Control of the "
"island was divided between a group of Italian pirates, the Mamertines, "
"based in the city of Messana, and the residents of Syracuse, a rich and "
"independent Greek city with its \n"
"#1215Previous Page# / #1217 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1217: title
msgctxt "help/1217/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1217/1"
msgid ""
"own king. The Syracusans, tired of the Mamertine raids, attacked "
"Messana, which promptly yelled for help -- from both the Romans and the "
"Phoenicians at once. The Phoenicians, jealous of Syracuse's wealth, "
"were the first to move, sending a fleet and garrison of troops for "
"Messana. The Romans took longer to respond; Rome had never undertaken "
"an overseas military action before, and was poorly equipped for the "
"task, without even a single seaworthy fighting ship. But the Phoenician "
"occupation of Messana presented a distinct threat to southern Italy, "
"too newly won and dearly bought to be risked. The expedition was "
"approved, and Roman troops set sail for Messana. When they entered the "
"city, the Phoenicians immediately attacked, and the first Punic War was "
"underway.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1216Previous Page# / #1218 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1218: title
msgctxt "help/1218/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1218/1"
msgid ""
"The Romans rapidly built themselves a navy, using a beached "
"Carthaginian warship as their model. The war ranged from Sicily and the "
"southern coast of Italy to the shores of North Africa and the "
"Mediterranean islands of Sardinia and Corsica, which were in Phoenician "
"hands at the start of the war. Once the Romans had their navy built, "
"the two sides were evenly matched, and the conflict became a war of "
"attrition that dragged on for twenty-five years. Eventually, Carthage "
"gave up, more out of exhaustion than for any other reason. As part of "
"the war settlement, Rome gained control of Sicily, Sardinia, and "
"Corsica. Carthage also agreed to pay a large sum of gold to Rome as "
"reparations for war damages.\n"
"\n"
"The acquisition of the Mediterranean islands left \n"
"#1217Previous Page# / #1219 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1219: title
msgctxt "help/1219/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1219/1"
msgid ""
"Rome with a new problem. She now owned overseas territory -- but how to "
"govern it? A new sort of position was created, that of governor with "
"imperium (judicial power) over a certain area, called a provincia, or "
"province. This position was filled with praetors from the Roman Senate. "
"Sicily thus became Rome's first province, and Sardinia and Corsica "
"together her second.\n"
"\n"
"The peace with Carthage lasted less than fifty years. The Carthaginians "
"were down, but a long way from out. A daring Phoenician, Hamilcar Barca "
"(\"Lightning Hamilcar\"), seized the rich and populous Spanish peninsula, "
"and through a mixture of deft generalship and expert administration "
"raised a huge army of Spaniards, financed by gold from Spanish mines. "
"The purpose to which this army was to be put was \n"
"#1218Previous Page# / #1220 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1220: title
msgctxt "help/1220/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1220/1"
msgid ""
"obvious -- Hamilcar had a deep and burning hatred of Rome. "
"Unfortunately for Carthage, he died before he could make his feelings "
"known. Unfortunately for Rome, Hamilcar had an immensely able "
"successor: his son, Hannibal.\n"
"\n"
"In 221 BC Hannibal set out with his army on a long and dangerous march "
"east from Spain through Gaul and the Alps. He lost much of his army "
"crossing the mountains (his elephants suffered greatly in the cold), "
"but recruited replacements from the Gallic tribes north of the Po, who "
"had their own bone to pick with the Romans for their northward "
"expansion. After training his Gallic auxiliaries, Hannibal made a "
"beeline south through Italy, heading for Rome. The Romans made several "
"efforts to stop him, but were beaten three times running, most "
"disastrously at the \n"
"#1219Previous Page# / #1221 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1221: title
msgctxt "help/1221/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1221/1"
msgid ""
"Battle of Cannae in 216 BC.\n"
"\n"
"One of history's greatest mysteries is why, at this point, Hannibal "
"swung to the east and went past Rome into the south of Italy, instead "
"of forging on into the city. Had he marched straight for the city, he "
"might well have taken it. The most likely explanation is that Hannibal "
"wished to avoid a siege, which his force was too small and ill-equipped "
"to handle properly. By marching south, he may have hoped to shake the "
"resolve of Rome's allied cities and deprive Rome of support, but the "
"Latin allies remained steadfast. He may also have been waiting for "
"reserves to arrive across the sea from Carthage before heading for "
"Rome, but Roman control of the sea prevented that. His brother, "
"Hasdrubal, attempted to duplicate Hannibal's march across the Alps with "
"a reinforcing \n"
"#1220Previous Page# / #1222 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1222: title
msgctxt "help/1222/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1222/1"
msgid ""
"army, but was defeated and killed by the excellent Roman general Scipio "
"before he made it into Italy.\n"
"Hannibal spent nearly fourteen years in the south of Italy, waiting for "
"the right time to attack Rome and enduring endless harassment and "
"delaying tactics from the Roman army. The right time never came. In 203 "
"BC he was recalled to help defend Carthage, which had been losing "
"regularly to the Romans on all other fronts. He and Scipio met on the "
"field of Zama in Africa, and Hannibal was defeated, primarily thanks to "
"an untimely mutiny by a contingent of mercenaries. Although Hannibal "
"escaped to the East, Zama marked the end of Carthaginian hopes. In 201 "
"BC Carthage surrendered and accepted all of Rome's terms, which were "
"harsh. Carthage gave up all lands (including the Spanish prize) to "
"Rome, except for \n"
"#1221Previous Page# / #1223 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1223: title
msgctxt "help/1223/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1223/1"
msgid ""
"Carthage itself and its immediate surroundings; they turned over all of "
"their warships and elephants to the Romans; and they agreed not to "
"conduct any wars without permission from Rome.\n"
"\n"
"Although the power of Carthage was permanently broken, Rome wasn't "
"satisfied. During the second Punic war, Rome had contracted a rewarding "
"alliance with Masinissa, king of Numidia in North Africa. Numidia "
"bordered Carthage's few remaining territorial holdings, and Masinissa "
"used his favored position with Rome as a license to make forays into "
"Carthaginian lands. The Carthaginians, constrained by the treaty from "
"going to war with Masinissa, appealed to Rome for help -- which "
"repeatedly ruled in Masinissa's favor. Eventually, the Carthaginians "
"had no choice but to break the treaty and attack \n"
"#1222Previous Page# / #1224 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1224: title
msgctxt "help/1224/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1224/1"
msgid ""
"Masinissa. Rome, provided with the excuse it had been waiting for, "
"attacked Carthage. This time Carthage had no chance. The city was "
"razed, its citizens slaughtered, and its lands salted. The ceremony of "
"evocatio was performed, and the lands declared cursed. Yet, salt and "
"curses notwithstanding, Carthage's city site was favorable enough that "
"it would take only a little over a century for the Romans to decide to "
"found a new settlement on the bones of its old enemy.\n"
"\n"
"Greek historians, looking for some way to explain the enmity between "
"Carthage and Rome, created the myth of Dido and Aeneas to explain it, "
"but in truth legends weren't necessary. There was room for only one "
"power in the Mediterranean, and Rome was determined to be it. Carthage "
"was her sole rival. \n"
"#1223Previous Page# / #1225 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1225: title
msgctxt "help/1225/0"
msgid "The Punic Wars (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1225/1"
msgid ""
"Therefore, as Cato the Censor put it, \"Carthago delenda est\" -- "
"\"Carthage must be destroyed.\" \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1224Previous Page# / #1850 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1226: title
msgctxt "help/1226/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1226/1"
msgid ""
"THE DECLINE OF THE REPUBLIC\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Cornelius Sulla Felix\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1850Previous Page# / #1227 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1227: title
msgctxt "help/1227/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic "
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1227/1"
msgid ""
"Rome's success in the Punic Wars spelled a significant change in the "
"politics of the Mediterranean. The Romans, having defeated the major "
"power of the Mediterranean and reaped large rewards thereby, were "
"feeling their oats and looking for new challenges. They didn't have to "
"look far. They fought wars with the Illyrians, the Macedonians, the "
"Syrians, various Germanic tribes, and practically anyone else who "
"crossed their path.\n"
"\n"
"The booty from all these wars went entirely to a privileged few: the "
"Roman Senate. In addition to gaining land and riches for the Senators, "
"the wars brought a valuable new commodity: slaves by the tens of "
"thousands. Prior to this, slavery had been only a minor part of Roman "
"society. Originally, debtors could be sold into slavery as payment for "
"their debts, \n"
"#1226Previous Page# / #1228 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1228: title
msgctxt "help/1228/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1228/1"
msgid ""
"but in 326 BC the plebs won a major political battle and pushed through "
"a law saying no Roman citizen could own another Roman citizen. From "
"then until the end of the Punic wars, the only slaves in Rome were "
"those few prisoners of war who had not been ransomed by their home "
"countries. Roman expansion after the fall of Carthage changed all that, "
"as for the first time in history Rome had access to entire cities full "
"of people who could be enslaved and shipped back to Rome. At one time "
"during this period, the great slave market on the island of Delos moved "
"over 10,000 slaves a day.\n"
"\n"
"The Roman elite bought huge numbers of slaves and put them to work "
"farming land owned by the Senate. Between the new land gained through "
"warfare and old land bought up with the gold gained by the same \n"
"#1227Previous Page# / #1229 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1229: title
msgctxt "help/1229/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1229/1"
msgid ""
"means, most Senators owned huge estates. These estates needed cheap "
"labor, which slaves provided. As a side effect, the free smallholders "
"were increasingly pushed off their land and into poverty. They moved to "
"the city in search of work, but there was little to be had. A huge rift "
"between the privileged elite and the impoverished masses soon "
"developed, and serious trouble was not long in coming.\n"
"\n"
"Around 130 BC, a pair of liberal tribunes, the Gracchi brothers, "
"attempted to force through legislation that would give back land to "
"landless Roman citizens. Though some of their reforms were made into "
"law, the Gracchi themselves were murdered by the Senate for their "
"actions. Their land law was then enforced by giving the land of Italian "
"farmers (allies, but not citizens of Rome) to Romans to farm, which "
"enraged \n"
"#1228Previous Page# / #1230 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1230: title
msgctxt "help/1230/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1230/1"
msgid ""
"the Italians and set off the Social War. Rome's non-Latin Italian "
"allies seceded and demanded equal rights for all Italians. The Senate "
"grumbled, but as in the time of the Latin war, they had little choice "
"but to grant the demands. The ranks of those privileged to be called "
"Roman citizens, protected by Roman law, swelled once again.\n"
"\n"
"Meanwhile, the chaos brought about several important changes in Rome "
"itself. First, the Senate's habit of arranging the death of its "
"political enemies led the consul Gaius Marius to throw open membership "
"in the legions to all men, not just those rich enough to equip "
"themselves. In order to be able to afford their armor and arms, these "
"new soldiers usually went in debt to their commander, the debt to be "
"repaid out of loot gained during campaigns. The \n"
"#1229Previous Page# / #1231 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1231: title
msgctxt "help/1231/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1231/1"
msgid ""
"loyalty of these new legionnaires went first to their commander, and "
"only second to Rome. The full ramifications of this change would become "
"obvious in a very short time. Second, the ending of the war by Rome's "
"accession to the Italian demands irritated a proud and conservative "
"Roman general named Cornelius Sulla (nicknamed \"Felix\", meaning "
"\"lucky\".) Sulla was out of Rome at the time of the conclusion of the "
"Social War, but upon hearing what had happened he turned his army "
"around and had it march on the city itself -- the first time a Roman "
"general had done such a thing. It would not be the last.\n"
"\n"
"Sulla entered the city and had his troops kill hundreds of \"enemies of "
"the state.\" This action angered Gaius Marius, who was of liberal bent. "
"When Sulla left for \n"
"#1230Previous Page# / #1232 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1232: title
msgctxt "help/1232/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1232/1"
msgid ""
"a foreign war, Marius had his own troops march on Rome, seize the city, "
"and murder most of Sulla's conservative supporters. When Marius died "
"soon after, Sulla returned to Rome and reciprocated the favor, killing "
"not hundreds but thousands of citizens he suspected of harboring "
"liberal leanings. Sulla became Rome's first dictator, personally "
"reversing every popular reform of the past two centuries. Sulla did not "
"have Imperial aspirations, however; after making his political point, "
"he retired to his country house and died years afterward of natural "
"causes.\n"
"\n"
"For the moment, the Republic was still intact, though in form only. "
"Unrest continued in the wake of the social war. Frequent riots in the "
"city of Rome were compounded by several great slave uprisings. The "
"first revolt took place in Sicily in 136 BC and required \n"
"#1231Previous Page# / #1233 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1233: title
msgctxt "help/1233/0"
msgid "The Decline of the Republic (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1233/1"
msgid ""
"years of military action to put down. The second better-known revolt "
"began in 74 BC under the leadership of a former Roman soldier sent into "
"gladiatorial slavery for desertion, a man named Spartacus. Spartacus "
"and his huge army of followers (mostly freed slaves) terrorized "
"northern Italy for two years before finally being crushed by the Roman "
"general Marcus Licinius Crassus.\n"
"\n"
"The old Roman order was breaking down. Without significant political "
"reforms that the Senate proved unable or unwilling to make, the only "
"question was not if it would be utterly destroyed, but when.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1232Previous Page# / #1240 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

