#. help page 1241: title
msgctxt "help/1241/0"
msgid "Julius Caesar"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1241/1"
msgid ""
"Into this power vacuum social chaos had created stepped a young "
"politician from an undistinguished family named Gaius Julius Caesar -- "
"better known to history as just plain Caesar. Gifted with enormous "
"talents and the ambition to match, Caesar was a man whose name would "
"become synonymous with power in many modern languages: in German "
"kaiser, in Russian czar, and qaysar in Arabic.\n"
"\n"
"CAESAR'S EARLY CAREER\n"
"\n"
"A nephew by marriage of Gaius Marius, Caesar began his political career "
"at an early age. His leanings from the start were decidedly "
"anti-Republican; at age sixteen, he married into a politically liberal "
"family \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1240Previous Page# / #1242 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1242: title
msgctxt "help/1242/0"
msgid "Caesar's Early Career (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1242/1"
msgid ""
"and narrowly escaped execution in Sulla's conservative purges as a "
"result. At age twenty-two he entered public life as the Roman "
"equivalent of a lawyer. Before the decade was out he had been elected "
"quaestor (though he was still two years shy of the legal minimum age "
"for the post). His climb through the political ranks went swiftly. "
"After three years as quaestor he was elected aedile; two years later he "
"gained the religious position of pontifex maximus (through political "
"chicanery);the following year he was elected praetor. During 61 and 60 "
"BC he held the governorship of wealthy Spain, and used the position to "
"amass enough of a personal fortune to have himself elected consul in 59 "
"BC.\n"
"\n"
"By now the Senate was starting to become wary of Gaius Julius Caesar. "
"His ambitions to greater power \n"
"#1241Previous Page# / #1243 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1243: title
msgctxt "help/1243/0"
msgid "Caesar's Early Career (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1243/1"
msgid ""
"were far from secret, and he was amassing dangerous levels of support "
"both among the general populace and within the political ranks. His "
"personal charisma was enormous and his vitality inexhaustible. (His "
"sexual exploits in particular were the source of endless gossip.) His "
"election as consul should have entitled him to yet another lucrative "
"provincial governorship, but the Senate, not wishing to empower Caesar "
"any more than absolutely necessary, assigned him the position of "
"supervisor of Italy's forests and cattle trails -- not a post conducive "
"to lining a governor's pockets. They also arranged for the election of "
"an anti-Caesar co-consul. Their man proved no match for Caesar's "
"enthusiasm and connections, though -- people of the time spoke of the "
"\"Consulship of Caesar and Caesar.\"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1242Previous Page# / #1244 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1244: title
msgctxt "help/1244/0"
msgid "Caesar's Early Career (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1244/1"
msgid ""
"Caesar didn't let the Senate's tactics slow him down. Deprived of a "
"chance to use his energies making money, he instead used them to make "
"political alliances. His most successful move was to bring two of "
"Rome's most powerful men into his orbit, creating the unofficial \"First "
"Triumvirate.\" Caesar's partners were Gnaeus Pompeius, better known as "
"Pompey the Great, and Marcus Licinius Crassus. Pompey was one of Rome's "
"most respected generals, and Crassus, although also a general, was more "
"famous for his vast personal wealth. Pompey and Crassus were long-time "
"political enemies, but somehow Caesar managed to get the two of them "
"working together, with himself as the vital link.\n"
"\n"
"As consul, Caesar pushed through a land bill granting farms to Pompey's "
"veterans. He also proposed a bill to \n"
"#1243Previous Page# / #1245 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1245: title
msgctxt "help/1245/0"
msgid "Caesar's Early Career (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1245/1"
msgid ""
"curb the rampant misconduct of provincial governors. At the same time, "
"political friends of Caesar's arranged for him to be made governor of "
"Cisalpine Gaul and Illyricum, and added Transalpine Gaul a short time "
"later. This was what Caesar had been waiting for.\n"
"\n"
"A Roman politician couldn't expect to earn lasting power without "
"showing himself to be a competent military leader. Caesar had decided "
"on Gaul as his proving ground. Between 58 BC and 50 BC he roamed all "
"over that land, and wandered up to Britain as well, subjugating tribe "
"after Gallic tribe as he went. Although the Gallic cavalry men were "
"equal to, or even slightly better than, the Roman legionnaires in "
"fighting prowess, they were handicapped by the unwillingness of the "
"Gallic chieftains to cooperate. \n"
"#1244Previous Page# / #1246 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1246: title
msgctxt "help/1246/0"
msgid "Caesar's Early Career (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1246/1"
msgid ""
"Caesar's disciplined troops were able to pick them off one by one. In "
"52 BC the Gauls finally became desperate enough to unite under one "
"leader, Vercingetorix, but Caesar himself proved to be as skilled "
"militarily as he was politically. He besieged and defeated "
"Vercingetorix, and claimed all of Gaul for Rome.\n"
"\n"
"THE DIE IS CAST\n"
"\n"
"The conquest of Gaul gave Caesar gold, prestige, and an experienced and "
"formidable army fiercely loyal to his person. Meanwhile, back in Rome, "
"the Triumvirate was crumbling in Caesar's absence. Crassus was killed "
"in 53 BC while fighting against the Parthians in the East, and Pompey "
"began drifting politically more and more toward the Senate and \n"
"#1245Previous Page# / #1247 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1247: title
msgctxt "help/1247/0"
msgid "The Die Is Cast (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1247/1"
msgid ""
"away from Caesar. Pompey and the Senate started maneuvering to separate "
"Caesar from his soldiers, probably so they could have him arrested on "
"trumped-up charges and dispose of him quietly. Caesar wasn't fooled, "
"however, and deftly evaded the Senate's authority, with the help of his "
"own political agents in Rome.\n"
"\n"
"The matter came to a crux in January of 49 BC. Pompey and the Senate "
"finally managed to outmaneuver Caesar's allies, and ordered him to "
"leave his command and return to Rome as a private citizen. Caesar, "
"aware that this would leave him defenseless and probably dead, made a "
"fateful decision. He would return to Rome, yes -- but he would bring "
"his army with him. As he and his forces crossed the little river called "
"the Rubicon, Caesar supposedly commented \n"
"#1246Previous Page# / #1248 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1248: title
msgctxt "help/1248/0"
msgid "The Die Is Cast (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1248/1"
msgid ""
"\"The die is cast,\" meaning that he had just taken an action he could "
"never revoke. From that point, his future held either total power, or "
"death.\n"
"\n"
"Caesar's opponent in the civil war to determine Rome's future was his "
"former ally, Pompey. By the time Caesar's armies reached the gates of "
"Rome, Pompey had withdrawn to the East and the safety of his own "
"legions. Caesar went after him, and the two generals sparred back and "
"forth across the Mediterranean for two years. Pompey had the Senate's "
"official backing, but Caesar outclassed him both militarily and in "
"popular support. Eventually Caesar cornered Pompey in Egypt. After "
"Caesar had a discussion with Ptolemy, king of Egypt (and a dalliance "
"with the princess, Cleopatra), Ptolemy had his own men find and kill "
"Pompey and bring his head \n"
"#1247Previous Page# / #1249 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1249: title
msgctxt "help/1249/0"
msgid "The Die Is Cast (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1249/1"
msgid ""
"back to Caesar.\n"
"\n"
"After Pompey's death, no one in Rome seriously disputed that Caesar's "
"word had become law. The new dictator spent the next two years "
"traveling with his troops, putting down various pockets of resistance. "
"In 46 BC he reformed the Roman calendar (long in need of an overhaul) "
"and added a month to it, naming it July after himself. By 45 BC Caesar "
"was back in Rome, his Empire secure, and himself ready to assume "
"leadership as Rome's first Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"THE IDES OF MARCH\n"
"\n"
"Now, after coming so far, Caesar made his first serious error -- and it "
"would prove to be fatal. Although he was fully capable of being "
"ruthless toward his \n"
"#1248Previous Page# / #1250 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1250: title
msgctxt "help/1250/0"
msgid "The Ides of March (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1250/1"
msgid ""
"enemies (as the Gallic tribes could testify), he treated his Roman "
"opposition with mercy, refraining from having them executed. Perhaps he "
"became overconfident in his position and his overwhelming public "
"support or perhaps he was simply trying to promote himself as a much "
"better ruler than the corrupt and self-serving Senate. Certainly he "
"hoped to sway as much of the Senate to his side as possible, to lend "
"legitimacy to his rule. For whatever reason, Caesar left his enemies "
"alone, and was confident enough of their honor that he wandered the "
"streets of Rome without a bodyguard.\n"
"\n"
"His confidence was misplaced. The Senate was used to having its own "
"way, and had a long tradition of resorting to violence in order to "
"secure it. A conspiracy of some sixty Senators formed, led by \n"
"#1249Previous Page# / #1251 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1251: title
msgctxt "help/1251/0"
msgid "The Ides of March (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1251/1"
msgid ""
"Gaius Cassius Longinus and Marcus Junius Brutus, intending to do away "
"with Caesar. To gain support, they whispered that Caesar was looking to "
"set himself up as King of Rome (essentially true), and reminded Romans "
"that they had sworn off kings after the rule of Tarquinus Superbus, 450 "
"years ago. On March 15, 44 BC (the Ides of March), a crowd of Senators "
"surrounded Caesar in the Senate Hall and stabbed him more than twenty "
"times.\n"
"\n"
"The conspirators declared that they had freed Rome from the tyranny of "
"kings and restored the liberty of the Republic. The popular support "
"they expected, however, never materialized. The average Roman citizen "
"was disgusted with the Senate, and had been pleased by Caesar's plans "
"for the reformation and reorganization of the Empire. The rule of the "
"Senate\n"
"#1250Previous Page# / #1252 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1252: title
msgctxt "help/1252/0"
msgid "The Ides of March (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1252/1"
msgid ""
"had been broken beyond repair. Caesar's lieutenant, Marc Antony, made a "
"public speech praising Caesar and vilifying his murderers; Cassius and "
"Brutus fled to the East to escape the revenge of Caesar's supporters, "
"and the Empire descended into more than a decade of turmoil.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1251Previous Page# / #1260 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

