#. help page 1261: title
msgctxt "help/1261/0"
msgid "Heir to Caesar"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1261/1"
msgid ""
"After the death of Caesar, the Roman Republic was in a shambles. For "
"Rome's power to survive, a worthy successor to Caesar needed to be "
"found, and quickly. It seemed most likely that Caesar's right-hand man, "
"Marc Antony, would be the one to step into the Imperial shoes. Antony "
"himself fully expected that Caesar would name him heir, and had already "
"laid claim to Caesar's personal fortune when it was revealed that "
"Caesar's named successor was his eighteen year old grand-nephew and "
"adopted son, Gaius Octavius.\n"
"\n"
"The prospect of this untried youth claiming the power of Caesar must "
"have seemed ludicrous -- to everyone except Gaius Octavius, that is. "
"The young man was studying in Apollonia when word of Caesar's death and "
"his own inheritance reached him. \n"
"#1260Previous Page# / #1262 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1262: title
msgctxt "help/1262/0"
msgid "Heir to Caesar (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1262/1"
msgid ""
"His family and numerous other sensible people urged him to make no "
"attempt to claim what Caesar had left him, but Octavius paid no "
"attention. Shortly after his arrival in Rome, Octavius took the name of "
"his adopted father, calling himself Gaius Julius Caesar. According to "
"convention, he should have added \"Octavianus\" at the end, but Octavius "
"ignored the custom. To avoid considerable confusion, however, later "
"Roman historians began referring to him as Octavian. The history books "
"thus baptized the first true Roman Emperor with a name he himself never "
"actually used.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1261Previous Page# / #1263 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1263: title
msgctxt "help/1263/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1263/1"
msgid ""
"THE SECOND TRIUMVIRATE\n"
"Picture: Bust of Marc Antony\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1262Previous Page# / #1264 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1264: title
msgctxt "help/1264/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1264/1"
msgid ""
"Octavian's first action was to attempt to claim Caesar's estate from "
"Marc Antony, but Antony, who had already spent much of the money, "
"ignored him. Undaunted, Octavius sold some of his own property to raise "
"money, most of which he spent ingratiating himself with Caesar's "
"troops. In this he had an edge over his rival, for the personal loyalty "
"of Caesar's legions to their fallen commander required that they honor "
"Caesar's wishes as expressed in his will. Since Caesar had chosen "
"Octavius, not Marc Antony, Caesar's soldiers were inclined to prefer "
"the teenager as well, despite the fact that he had not yet proven "
"himself in combat. Octavian also found himself unexpectedly favored by "
"the Senate, who considered him less of a threat than the experienced "
"Antony. This esteemed body even went so far as to name Octavian a "
"senator and give him a vacant consulship, \n"
"#1263Previous Page# / #1265 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1265: title
msgctxt "help/1265/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1265/1"
msgid ""
"though he was far too young to legally hold either position. The Senate "
"no doubt planned to make a puppet ruler out of Octavian to appease the "
"masses. Octavian had other plans.\n"
"\n"
"Aware that he had no way of asserting his dominance over Marc Antony "
"just yet, Octavian proposed a novel solution probably inspired by "
"Caesar's alliance with Crassus and Pompey. He, Marc Antony, and Lepidus "
"(another Caesar supporter and chief priest of Rome) would rule the "
"Roman Empire jointly as the Second Triumvirate, starting in 43 BC. "
"Antony, who had experience and money but did not have Octavian's "
"legitimacy, agreed, and Lepidus, who otherwise would have had nothing, "
"was quite happy to join. Lepidus's participation in the Triumvirate was "
"no more than ceremonial; the real power rested with \n"
"#1264Previous Page# / #1266 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1266: title
msgctxt "help/1266/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1266/1"
msgid ""
"Antony and Octavian. The Senate, with little else in the way of useful "
"options, agreed to give the Triumvirate legal status for a period of "
"five years, \"for the reconstitution of the state.\" The first act of the "
"Triumvirs was to execute 300 senators and 2,000 citizens of lower rank. "
"No doubt both Antony and Octavian were eager to avoid Caesar's mistake "
"of leaving his enemies alive.\n"
"\n"
"In 42 the Senate voted to award Julius Caesar the status of a god of "
"the state, and gave the deified dictator the title \"Julius Divus.\" Why "
"exactly they did this is unclear. Perhaps they hoped to win the favor "
"of the Triumvirs and prevent further Senatorial executions. They also "
"may have anticipated how this action would add to Octavian's prestige "
"(at Antony's expense) by giving him the status of the son of a god. \n"
"#1265Previous Page# / #1267 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1267: title
msgctxt "help/1267/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1267/1"
msgid ""
"Octavian promptly had a fine temple built and dedicated to his deified "
"adoptive father.\n"
"\n"
"After dealing with their enemies in Rome, Octavian and Antony sailed "
"east with a fleet of warships to deal with Caesar's assassins. They "
"engaged the forces of Cassius and Brutus twice at Philippi, winning "
"each time; after the second loss, the two leading conspirators took "
"their own lives. That vital business dealt with, Octavian and Antony "
"returned to Rome and spent three years running the Empire and "
"attempting to strengthen their political positions. Octavian went off "
"to fight several small wars in order to gain experience in combat and "
"the prestige necessary to improve his status with the Legions.\n"
"\n"
"In 38 BC the Triumvirs gathered at Brundisium to \n"
"#1266Previous Page# / #1268 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1268: title
msgctxt "help/1268/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1268/1"
msgid ""
"renew their alliance. At this time, they divided the Roman lands among "
"themselves so that each could have a sphere of absolute authority. "
"Octavian took the West, Antony got the East, and Lepidus got a small "
"part of Africa to keep him happy. Italy was supposed to remain neutral, "
"but with Antony off enjoying the riches of Egypt and Lepidus "
"politically ineffectual, the heartland of the Empire was effectively in "
"Octavian's hands.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1267Previous Page# / #1269 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

