#. help page 1270: title
msgctxt "help/1270/0"
msgid "Imperator Caesar Augustus"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1270/1"
msgid ""
"While Antony managed his eastern lands (and consorted with Egypt's "
"Cleopatra, now Queen), Octavian campaigned for senatorial and public "
"favor in Rome. To give himself stronger ties to the aristocracy, he "
"married into one of Rome's elite families. To please the public, he "
"spent lavish amounts of money on public games, buildings, and works of "
"art. At time progressed, relations between Antony and Octavian became "
"strained; it was clear that neither man would be willing to settle for "
"half an Empire indefinitely.\n"
"\n"
"The year 33 BC passed without a treaty renewing the Triumvirate; "
"although Antony still claimed to act in the name of the alliance, "
"Octavian had dropped the pretense. By this point, he had twelve years "
"of political and military experience behind him, a solid \n"
"#1269Previous Page# / #1271 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1271: title
msgctxt "help/1271/0"
msgid "Imperator Caesar Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1271/1"
msgid ""
"network of political supporters from both upper and lower classes, and "
"firm control over Caesar's legions. It was time to claim his "
"inheritance. Octavian began to foment anger against Marc Antony in "
"Rome, claiming that Antony was conspiring with Queen Cleopatra to give "
"Roman territory over to Egypt. Antony didn't help his own case when he "
"divorced his Roman wife (who happened to be Octavian's sister) and "
"married Cleopatra instead. That gave Octavian the excuse he needed to "
"declare outright war on Marc Antony and Cleopatra.\n"
"\n"
"The two forces met that year at the battle of Actium. Octavian's side "
"of the fight was ably managed by his school chum Marcus Agrippa, and "
"most of Antony's fleet was crushed. Antony and Cleopatra fled back to "
"Egypt and committed suicide. Octavian marched to \n"
"#1270Previous Page# / #1272 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1272: title
msgctxt "help/1272/0"
msgid "Imperator Caesar Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1272/1"
msgid ""
"Egypt, executed Cleopatra's son Caesarion (fathered, so she claimed, by "
"Julius Caesar), and made Egypt a Roman province to be administered by "
"himself. Cleopatra's vast treasure went to pay off Octavian's veterans.\n"
"\n"
"From that point on, Octavian was supreme ruler of Rome and her Empire. "
"There were no ceremonies proclaiming his ascension to total power, no "
"major upheavals in Rome or elsewhere. It was simply a given that "
"Octavian was in charge, and no one seriously disputed it. The legal "
"basis for his rule was a continuous series of \"annual\" consulships from "
"31 BC through 23 BC. In 27 BC the plebs elected him \"Tribune for life\"; "
"by tradition, this became the official starting date of his reign. To "
"commemorate his new status, Octavian changed him name to \"Imperator "
"Caesar \n"
"#1271Previous Page# / #1273 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1273: title
msgctxt "help/1273/0"
msgid "Imperator Caesar Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1273/1"
msgid ""
"Augustus,\" meaning \"Caesar, the magnificent dictator,\" essentially. "
"From this, Octavian acquired his other historical name, \"Augustus.\" For "
"Octavian these were just names, however grand; to those who followed "
"him, they became titles of power.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1272Previous Page# / #1274 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1274: title
msgctxt "help/1274/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1274/1"
msgid ""
"THE EMPIRE OF AUGUSTUS\n"
"Picture: A cameo brooch of the Emperor Augustus\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1273Previous Page# / #1275 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1275: title
msgctxt "help/1275/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1275/1"
msgid ""
"\"I found Rome a city of brick, and left her a city of marble\" was "
"Augustus's claim toward the end of his reign. It was more true than "
"not; under Octavian's rule the city and the Empire found a stability "
"and prosperity it had not seen in centuries. Octavian's formal title "
"was \"princeps,\" meaning \"first citizen,\" and the style of government he "
"invented was known as the Principate. It lasted nearly two hundred "
"years with only minor changes.\n"
"\n"
"Octavian's contributions to the health and glory of Rome were many. He "
"cut down on spending by reducing the legions from 60 to 28, while "
"increasing the effectiveness of the 28 remaining legions by making them "
"a permanent standing force of professional soldiers. He created the "
"Praetorian Guard, an elite garrison for Rome itself, and rebuilt and \n"
"#1274Previous Page# / #1276 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1276: title
msgctxt "help/1276/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1276/1"
msgid ""
"modernized Rome's navy. In Rome itself, he pruned the deadwood from the "
"Senate, reducing it from over a thousand members to six hundred; gave "
"Rome its first fire department and first police department; created a "
"public grain dole for the city's poor; set up a military pension fund "
"for veterans; ordered a regular census to aid in city administration; "
"and sponsored artists, writers and historians, including Livy and "
"Virgil. He accrued honors and titles by the dozens, of which his "
"favorite was the accolade \"pater patriae,\" father of his country. The "
"eighth month of the Roman calendar was renamed after him.\n"
"\n"
"Octavian also promoted some long overdue social reorganization. During "
"the later years of the Republic, a significant middle class had grown "
"up in Rome and elsewhere. These men, known as \"equites,\" \n"
"#1275Previous Page# / #1277 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1277: title
msgctxt "help/1277/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1277/1"
msgid ""
"meaning knights, were worthy and successful men of plebeian birth. "
"Before Augustus, their class kept them out of the government, but "
"Augustus opened up many civil positions to this lower aristocracy, and "
"his administration benefited greatly from their energy. They formed the "
"core of the Imperial civil service, required to effectively manage a "
"great Empire. Instead of a two-way split between patricians and plebs "
"as at the start of the Republic, the Principate now recognized five "
"classes of men. The patricians were still on top, with the equites "
"right below them, followed by the ordinary plebs. These three classes "
"all held Roman citizenship. Below them were the non-citizens: freedmen, "
"which included former slaves and foreign immigrants, and the slaves at "
"the very bottom of the pile. A freedman was prohibited from gaining "
"citizenship, but his children were eligible for \n"
"#1276Previous Page# / #1278 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1278: title
msgctxt "help/1278/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1278/1"
msgid ""
"that status.\n"
"\n"
"The Empire beyond Rome expanded and prospered under Octavian. The "
"Princeps was a great road-builder, improving and extending the Empire's "
"highway network in all directions. This allowed his troops to move more "
"quickly to where they were needed. Although it seems a paradox, "
"Augustus's reign was renown for its peace while at the same time being "
"one of the more warlike periods of Rome's history. This contradiction "
"is a matter of viewpoint; while Augustus pursued many wars on Roman "
"frontiers in order to gain and hold solid defensive borders for Rome's "
"Empire, the citizens within those borders knew a peace and order that "
"had long been sorely lacking.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1277Previous Page# / #1279 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1279: title
msgctxt "help/1279/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1279/1"
msgid ""
"Augustus added several new provinces to Rome's territory and "
"reorganized many that already existed. He annexed Judaea; claimed "
"Northern Africa and created the provinces of Numidia and Mauretania "
"there; divided Gaul up into four provinces of Gallia Narbonensis, "
"Aquitania, Belgica, and Lugdunensis; and portioned Spain into Baetica, "
"Lusitania, and Tarraconensis. He pushed into Germany, but fierce "
"resistance from the Germanic tribes kept him from making any lasting "
"gains in that region. In particularly fractious border areas he "
"established client kingdoms rather than provinces. These lands were "
"ruled, administrated, and defended by the local nobility, who "
"understood that they maintained their independence only with Rome's "
"forbearance. This way, Rome could exact tribute from these lands and "
"control their external policy without having to \n"
"#1278Previous Page# / #1280 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1280: title
msgctxt "help/1280/0"
msgid "The Empire of Augustus (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1280/1"
msgid ""
"worry about administering or defending them.\n"
"\n"
"PROVINCIAL GOVERNMENT \n"
"\n"
"Augustus greatly expanded the number of provinces in the Roman Empire "
"and overhauled their system of administration. In the days of the "
"Republic, when provinces were few, each was governed by a praetor. When "
"more provinces were added, however, the total number of praetors had to "
"be increased, and the Senate wasn't in favor of this notion; since "
"praetor was the first step on the political ladder, the creation of "
"more praetors created more political competition and brought so-called "
"\"new men\" into the government. As a result, the post of proconsul was "
"created especially for provincial governors. Like\n"
"#1279Previous Page# / #1281 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1281: title
msgctxt "help/1281/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1281/1"
msgid ""
"Rome's consuls, proconsuls had ultimate authority within their sphere "
"of influence, but the prefix indicated that they were not \"real\" "
"consuls.\n"
"\n"
"Each time a province was created, the Senate would create a charter for "
"it. This document laid down the legal status of the communities in the "
"province, defined its boundaries, set the tax levels, and listed those "
"local customs and laws which the Romans agreed to respect. Charters "
"tended to be generous, to keep administration to a minimum. The Romans "
"truly did not care what customs the locals followed, provided they paid "
"tribute to Rome and obeyed the Senate when given an order. "
"Unfortunately, the provincial governor could, and often did, violate "
"the provincial charter when it suited him, without fear of censure or "
"reprisals from the Senate. The governor \n"
"#1280Previous Page# / #1282 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1282: title
msgctxt "help/1282/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1282/1"
msgid ""
"was also free to interpret the deliberately vague terms of the charter "
"in any way he liked. The only protection the locals had from the "
"governor was the governor's own sense of honor. This was a flimsy "
"bulwark which often failed under the governor's desire for personal "
"wealth and prestige.\n"
"\n"
"The governors were supposed to execute the wishes of the Republic, yet "
"the Senate had no means of enforcing this. Governors usually ruled "
"their provinces as petty kingdoms, sometimes even raiding provinces of "
"neighboring governors. This lack of control was both a symptom of and a "
"contributing factor in the fall of the Republic. Octavian rapidly put a "
"stop to the worst provincial excesses. He was wise enough to realize "
"that a peaceful province produced more tax revenue and required less "
"attention and \n"
"#1281Previous Page# / #1283 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1283: title
msgctxt "help/1283/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1283/1"
msgid ""
"expense than a restive one. The best way to encourage peace in the "
"provinces was to put loyal and able men in charge, and to allow the "
"provincials to govern themselves in all matters that had no effect on "
"Rome or Rome's interests.\n"
"\n"
"Octavian divided up the provinces into two types: Senatorial and "
"Imperial. The governors for Senatorial provinces were chosen by the "
"Senate, usually by lot. They served a single year and were not allowed "
"to have Roman troops under their command. These were generally the "
"quietest provinces. The governors for the Imperial provinces were "
"hand-picked by the Emperor and personally responsible to him. They "
"ruled as long as the Emperor let them, and were given troops to "
"command. The Emperor kept personal control over troublesome provinces, "
"those in \n"
"#1282Previous Page# / #1284 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1284: title
msgctxt "help/1284/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1284/1"
msgid ""
"strategic positions, or those otherwise exceptional -- Egypt was always "
"kept as an Imperial province, for example, because that way its riches "
"went straight into the Emperor's own coffers.\n"
"\n"
"Whether he was in charge of a Senatorial or Imperial province, a "
"governor under Octavian finally had a higher authority to answer to for "
"his actions. This helped limit excesses. Governors still lined their "
"own pockets and abused the locals, but if they did too much of either "
"they could wind up without their heads, and few would risk that. The "
"increase in provincial confidence and order, combined with Octavian's "
"road-building and suppression of corruption, banditry, and piracy, "
"stimulated trade and settlement. Provincial land was often parceled out "
"to retired veterans, increasing the number of \n"
"#1283Previous Page# / #1285 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1285: title
msgctxt "help/1285/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1285/1"
msgid ""
"loyal Romans in the provinces and aiding in the growth of civilization "
"and increase of standard of living in those areas. New urban centers "
"sprang up all over, and a feeling of prosperity and optimism spread "
"throughout the Empire.\n"
"\n"
"This was Augustus's greatest contribution to the Roman Empire: he made "
"his Principate, effective rule by a single man, a far better system of "
"government for the masses than did the Republic that had gone before. "
"By making required reforms and then living long enough to see them bear "
"fruit, he ensured that the Principate would continue on past his death. "
"His reign lasted fifty-six years; by the time he died, hardly anyone in "
"the Empire remembered the Republic, and those who did recall it "
"considered themselves well rid of it. The evils of an autocratic \n"
"#1284Previous Page# / #1286 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1286: title
msgctxt "help/1286/0"
msgid "Provincial Government (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1286/1"
msgid ""
"rule were barely in evidence. Few in Rome were bothered by the fact "
"that there were no constitutional safeguards against abuses of power by "
"the Princeps, or that an \"offense to the dignity of the Emperor\" was "
"now a crime punishable by death.\n"
"\n"
"Augustus was a great enough man to resist the temptation to become a "
"tyrant. Whenever the Princeps was likewise a great or even just a "
"decent man, the Empire thrived. Unfortunately, the Empire was not "
"always granted that blessing, as events would soon prove.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1285Previous Page# / #1290 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1287: title
msgctxt "help/1287/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1287/1"
msgid ""
"CHAPTER IV\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Engraving showing Tiberius sending the general Germanicus to "
"fight in the East.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
