msgctxt "help/1290/1"
msgid "@1287"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1291: title
msgctxt "help/1291/0"
msgid "The Julio-Claudians (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1291/1"
msgid ""
"The long reign of Augustus was responsible for allowing his reforms to "
"take firm root throughout the Empire, but it presented him with a "
"problem of a different sort: he outlived all of his first choices for a "
"successor. His only surviving child was a daughter, Julia. Augustus "
"married her off to his intended heir, his nephew Marcellus. Marcellus "
"died unexpectedly, however, before fathering any children, so Julia was "
"married off again, this time to Marcus Agrippa. This marriage was more "
"successful, producing five children, including two promising boys named "
"Gaius and Lucius. But both boys died before Augustus did, Lucius of an "
"illness and Gaius a few years later in battle.\n"
"\n"
"Left without a better choice, Augustus settled on his stepson Tiberius, "
"child of his second wife Livia by her \n"
"#1290Previous Page# / #1292 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1292: title
msgctxt "help/1292/0"
msgid "The Julio-Claudians (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1292/1"
msgid ""
"first husband. There were worse choices, to be sure, but Tiberius's "
"reign as Emperor would not be a happy one. He was undoubtedly the best "
"of a bad lot. Unfortunately, that is about the most one can say of him.\n"
"\n"
"A HARD ACT TO FOLLOW\n"
"\n"
"Tiberius was an intelligent man and an excellent soldier, but he was "
"temperamentally unsuited for the role Augustus forced on him. In order "
"to make him a potential heir, Augustus ordered him to divorce his first "
"wife (whom he genuinely loved) and enter into a disastrous union with "
"Augustus's twice-widowed daughter Julia. Julia, also unhappy about the "
"marriage, expressed that feeling with a long series of affairs that she "
"didn't bother to hide from her new \n"
"#1291Previous Page# / #1293 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1293: title
msgctxt "help/1293/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1293/1"
msgid ""
"husband. Under Roman law Tiberius would have been perfectly justified "
"in divorcing her, but Augustus was blind to his daughter's behavior, "
"and to denounce her to the Emperor would be a dangerous move. Tiberius "
"dealt with the situation by going into self-imposed exile on Rhodes, "
"leaving Julia behind in Rome to indulge herself.\n"
"\n"
"On Rhodes, the general who had shown remarkable care and concern for "
"his soldiers in the field changed into an angry, bitter, and withdrawn "
"man who seemed to care for no one and nothing beyond himself and his "
"own pleasures. Although he had gone into exile voluntarily, he could "
"not return to Rome until Augustus granted him permission, and Augustus "
"seemed disinclined to do so. Then a series of personal disasters befell "
"Augustus. First, his wife Livia secured \n"
"#1292Previous Page# / #1294 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1294: title
msgctxt "help/1294/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1294/1"
msgid ""
"proof of Julia's adultery and presented it to the Emperor; under Roman "
"law, Julia should have been executed, but instead Augustus banished her "
"forever to a small Mediterranean island. Then Julia's son Lucius died, "
"leaving only Gaius as a possible successor to the Imperial seat. "
"Augustus, seeing his list of heirs shortened, recalled Tiberius from "
"Rhodes.\n"
"\n"
"When Gaius died in combat, Tiberius was the closest thing to legitimate "
"successor left to Augustus. By then old and ill, Augustus named "
"Tiberius his adopted son and heir. He never liked Tiberius, however, "
"and Tiberius hated him. When Augustus finally passed on in 14 AD, "
"Tiberius tried to coerce the Senate into denying the deceased Emperor "
"the status of godhood. He failed, however, and Augustus ascended to sit \n"
"\n"
"#1293Previous Page# / #1295 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1295: title
msgctxt "help/1295/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1295/1"
msgid ""
"beside Julius Caesar in the pantheon of the state gods of Rome.\n"
"\n"
"No doubt Tiberius would have been much happier as a simple Roman "
"general, living with his beloved first wife, instead of being dragged "
"unwillingly to the Imperial seat. At first, however, he ruled well "
"enough; his new laws were sensible, fair, and forward-looking, and "
"throughout his reign he was careful about the Empire's economic "
"security. From the beginning, though, the number of treason "
"accusations, trials, and confiscations of property showed an ominous "
"increase that did not bode well for the future. Without any legal "
"protection against the whims of the Emperor, the notables of Rome could "
"only keep their heads low, cross their fingers, and hope to escape "
"notice.\n"
"\n"
"#1294Previous Page# / #1296 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1296: title
msgctxt "help/1296/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1296/1"
msgid ""
"It is unclear whether or not Tiberius was paranoid, or whether people "
"really were out to get him. By the time he succeeded Augustus, he had "
"become a sullen, bitter, and self-absorbed man, with little ability to "
"make himself liked, and less interest in doing so. Augustus had "
"appeased the Senate by asking their advice and treating them with "
"respect even as he took away the real power that had been theirs, but "
"Tiberius disdained the Senate and did not try to conceal the fact. The "
"feeling was mutual. Under such circumstances, paranoia on Tiberius's "
"part was understandable. It was also understandable, however, that a "
"Senate threatened with treason charges on little to no evidence might "
"begin to plot in earnest.\n"
"\n"
"In 23 AD Tiberius's only son and intended successor Drusus died under "
"suspicious circumstances, and \n"
"#1295Previous Page# / #1297 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1297: title
msgctxt "help/1297/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1297/1"
msgid ""
"Tiberius became even more embittered. In 26 AD he took up residence on "
"the island of Capri, which he made into his personal retreat. He left "
"the job of running the Empire to his trusted Praetorian Guard captain, "
"Sejanus. Tiberius's abandonment of Rome offended the Senate, and "
"relations between the Senate and the Emperor deteriorated even further. "
"On Capri, Tiberius's anger and paranoia appear to have developed into "
"full-blown madness; his private residence became the site of bizarre "
"and perverse acts of torture and depravity.\n"
"\n"
"Sejanus apparently held aspirations to take the Imperial seat for "
"himself. In 31 AD Tiberius received information showing that Sejanus "
"had had an affair with Drusus's wife, and the two of them had conspired "
"to poison Drusus. Tiberius sent a letter to \n"
"#1296Previous Page# / #1298 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1298: title
msgctxt "help/1298/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1298/1"
msgid ""
"the Senate that began by praising Sejanus, and ended by calling for his "
"immediate execution. The Senate complied. But this act of treachery "
"soured Tiberius even further on Rome. Without a son for an heir, "
"Tiberius decided to get his revenge on Rome through his successor.\n"
"\n"
"Tiberius's choice was his young grand-nephew, Gaius Germanicus. "
"Germanicus's father had been a famous general, and the boy himself had "
"grown up among the soldiers and was popular with them. His personality "
"flaws were significant, however, a fact which Tiberius seems to have "
"noted with glee: \"I am nursing a viper in Rome's bosom,\" he is reported "
"to have said. Time would prove him right. Gaius Germanicus became "
"better known by the nickname \n"
"\n"
"#1297Previous Page# / #1299 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1299: title
msgctxt "help/1299/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1299/1"
msgid ""
"given to him by the soldiers as a child: Caligula, meaning \"Little "
"Boot,\" after the tiny army boots he wore.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1298Previous Page# / #1300 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

