"\n"
"\n"
"#1299Previous Page# / #1301 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1301: title
msgctxt "help/1301/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1301/1"
msgid ""
"The events of Caligula's succession set the tone for the reign that was "
"to follow. In 37 AD, Tiberius became ill and fell into a coma. "
"Physicians were called to Capri, where they declared that the Emperor "
"would not live out the night. In Rome, Caligula's ascension was "
"announced, the legions hailed him, and the city proclaimed its new "
"leader. Then Tiberius awoke, sat up, and asked for something to eat. "
"Thinking fast, the captain of the Praetorian Guard picked up the "
"bedclothes and smothered Tiberius with them.\n"
"\n"
"Caligula's reign is a matter of some controversy among historians. "
"There's no doubt that it was bad, but exactly how bad it was and why "
"remain in doubt. Historians of ancient Rome had a virulent hatred of "
"Caligula, which he probably earned, but \n"
"#1300Previous Page# / #1302 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1302: title
msgctxt "help/1302/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1302/1"
msgid ""
"which led them to make up stories about his reign that weren't true, "
"such as one saying that he made his horse consul. They hardly needed to "
"have bothered, though -- Caligula's reign displayed enough insanity "
"without embellishment to convince anyone that the man should never have "
"been made Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"The succession of Caligula was at first the cause of rejoicing in Rome; "
"Tiberius had been disliked, and Caligula was popular -- for the moment. "
"The new Emperor immediately threw open the Imperial treasury that "
"Tiberius had carefully husbanded and indulged in lavish parties, games, "
"and other public entertainments. About seven months into his reign, "
"however, he was struck with a sudden and severe illness that almost "
"killed him. When he recovered, he \n"
"#1301Previous Page# / #1303 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1303: title
msgctxt "help/1303/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1303/1"
msgid ""
"had changed, and for the worse. He may simply have suffered brain "
"damage from his illness and become psychotic. Possibly he became "
"epileptic. Whatever the cause, Tiberius's viper became viciously "
"poisonous.\n"
"\n"
"Treason trials became increasingly frequent and arbitrary. Punishments "
"were unprecedented in their cruelty. Threats of an accusation of "
"treason were used to extort money from wealthy Romans to support "
"Caligula's wasteful spending habits. These became even more pronounced "
"and purposeless; at one point, for example, he decreed that a bridge of "
"boats should be constructed over the bay of Naples. Caligula slept with "
"his sisters and had one of them declared a goddess after her death. He "
"marched his armies on Gaul with little effect, and stirred up trouble \n"
"#1302Previous Page# / #1304 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1304: title
msgctxt "help/1304/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1304/1"
msgid ""
"elsewhere on Rome's borders just for his amusement.\n"
"\n"
"The degree to which Caligula was mad, instead of just vicious, is "
"debatable. As already noted, some of his reported wilder actions were "
"fabricated by his enemies. Other actions may have been intended as "
"\"sympathetic magic.\" For example, he marched his troops to the shore of "
"the English Channel, then ordered them to collect seashells and bring "
"them back to Rome, where he declared that he had conquered the sea. He "
"also had some of his men put on blond wigs and paraded them in Rome, "
"claiming he had conquered Gaul. This may have been insanity, or it may "
"have been intended as a ritual to bring about the conquest of Britain "
"and of Gaul as a concrete reality. The truth is lost in time.\n"
"\n"
"#1303Previous Page# / #1305 Next Page #\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1305: title
msgctxt "help/1305/0"
msgid "Tibererius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1305/1"
msgid ""
"For whatever reasons, Caligula's horrible reign gave Rome a taste of "
"what autocratic rule could be like under a bad ruler. In January of 41, "
"the Romans had had enough; a mob led by the Praetorian Guard and "
"several Senators assassinated Caligula, and then killed his wife and "
"children for good measure. The viper was crushed. Now what? \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1304Previous Page# / #1306 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1306: title
msgctxt "help/1306/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1306/1"
msgid ""
"THE ACCIDENTAL EMPEROR\n"
"Picture: Head of Claudius from a bronze statue\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1305Previous Page# / #1307 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1307: title
msgctxt "help/1307/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1307/1"
msgid ""
"Caligula's reign had been so disastrous that the Senate made a brief "
"attempt to restore the Republic after the mad Emperor's assassination. "
"(Naturally, he was not made a god.) The Praetorian Guard preferred the "
"Principate, however, and by that point their wishes counted more than "
"those of the Senate. But who should hold the position? Choosing someone "
"of Caligula's line was out of the question, yet the soldiers wanted an "
"Emperor with the heritage of Julius Caesar and Augustus to lead them. "
"Tradition says that the Praetorian Guard roamed through the Imperial "
"Palace, looking for someone suitable, and eventually found Caligula's "
"uncle Claudius cowering behind a curtain. Caligula had had many of his "
"close relatives executed on trumped-up treason charges, a fate Claudius "
"had avoided by pretending to be a halfwit. The soldiers dragged the "
"startled and \n"
"#1306Previous Page# / #1308 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1308: title
msgctxt "help/1308/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1308/1"
msgid ""
"protesting Claudius out of his hiding place and proclaimed him Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius's reign is somewhat difficult to analyze. Ancient historians "
"disliked him, and their accounts tend to be biased against the "
"accidental emperor. Other more objective historical evidence paints a "
"more positive picture of his rule. In any case, there is no question "
"that he was a vast improvement over his predecessor.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius was born into a family with many imperial connections and "
"famous names. His father, Nero Claudius Drusus, was brother to Tiberius "
"and a popular and successful general. Claudius himself was shy, "
"bookish, and physically unimpressive; the imperial family considered "
"him something of an \n"
"#1307Previous Page# / #1309 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1309: title
msgctxt "help/1309/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1309/1"
msgid ""
"embarrassment. Despite his later reputation as feeble-minded, he was in "
"truth an intelligent man, a student of Livy, and the author of several "
"dozen volumes of history on Rome and Etruria (as well as a few books on "
"dice games, his favorite pastime). His placement on the Imperial seat "
"was a surprise to everyone, including Claudius. As it turned out, the "
"soldiers could have made many worse choices than the quiet, deceptively "
"clever Claudius.\n"
"\n"
"The Senate at first were not particularly happy with the notion of "
"Emperor Claudius. There was an abortive attempt to bring back the "
"Republic, and several minor rebellions. A number of assassination "
"attempts were made on Claudius's life in his first year as Princeps. "
"But Claudius had the backing of the legions and was firm, even ruthless "
"sometimes, in \n"
"#1308Previous Page# / #1310 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1310: title
msgctxt "help/1310/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1310/1"
msgid ""
"eliminating resistance to his rule; fairly quickly the Senate settled "
"down and accepted him, though they never really liked him much. "
"Claudius had many friends in the freedman class, and made room for a "
"number of them in the civil service, an action the Senate found "
"distressingly low-bred.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius handled the external affairs of the Empire capably, if not "
"brilliantly. No soldier himself, he let others do his fighting for him. "
"Despite this, he was interested in expanding Rome's sphere of "
"influence, and made good progress in that direction: he added the North "
"African provinces of Caesariensis and Tingitana, created the province "
"of Lycia in Asia Minor, and annexed Thrace. From the point of view of "
"Western historians, his most noteworthy acquisition was the province "
"Britannia, where he \n"
"#1309Previous Page# / #1311 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1311: title
msgctxt "help/1311/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1311/1"
msgid ""
"created the colonies of Camulodonum (Colchester) and Londinium "
"(London). He did not get carried away in his expansionism, however, "
"sensibly avoiding embroiling Rome in major wars with the Germanic "
"tribes or the Parthians.\n"
"\n"
"Claudius's civil administration was as efficient and moderate as his "
"foreign policy. Although he showed occasional flashes of caprice when "
"dealing with individuals, overall he improved the justice system, "
"extended the rights of citizenship to new groups, and was tolerant of "
"non-Roman customs and religions. He repaired the city and constructed "
"new public buildings, rebuilt and extended roads throughout the empire, "
"reorganized Rome's grain supply, modernized the harbor at Ostia, and "
"founded numerous Roman colonies.\n"
"\n"
"#1310Previous Page# / #1312 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1312: title
msgctxt "help/1312/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1312/1"
msgid ""
"Although his public affairs were well-ordered, Claudius continued the "
"Julio-Claudian tradition of having a messy private life. He married a "
"total of four times. He had his third wife executed for adultery, and "
"then amended Roman marriage laws to allow him to wed his own niece, "
"Agrippina the Younger, great-granddaughter of Augustus. This was "
"probably Agrippina's idea, not the Emperor's; Claudius's fourth wife "
"was a woman of ruthless ambition. She soon convinced Claudius to pass "
"over Brittanicus, his own son from an earlier marriage, and name her "
"son Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus (son of her first husband Gnaeus "
"Domitius Ahenobarbus) as his heir. Once that was accomplished, "
"Agrippina promptly disposed of Claudius by feeding him poisonous "
"mushrooms. He died in 54 AD, after a thirteen year reign. \n"
"\n"
"#1311Previous Page# / #1313 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1313: title
msgctxt "help/1313/0"
msgid "The Accidental Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1313/1"
msgid ""
"Ancient Roman historians tended to dismiss Claudius as a weak-willed "
"man who listened too much to his wives and his lowbrow freedmen "
"advisors. Of course, Roman historians were all upper class citizens, so "
"their opinion of Claudius couldn't help but be colored by his lack of "
"respect for their upper class privileges. Later historians have viewed "
"Claudius in a much gentler and more favorable light. Although not one "
"of the Roman Empire's most brilliant leaders, he was the only Emperor "
"of the Julio-Claudian line to come anywhere close to Augustus in "
"ability. After his death, the Senate laid their seal of approval on his "
"reign by granting him godhood.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1312Previous Page# / #1314 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1314: title
msgctxt "help/1314/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1314/1"
msgid ""
"DAMNED IN MEMORY\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Bust of Nero\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1313Previous Page# / #1315 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1315: title
msgctxt "help/1315/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1315/1"
msgid ""
"The single biggest mistake Claudius made during his reign was allowing "
"Agrippina to choose his successor for him. Agrippina's son, his name "
"changed to Nero Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, was not yet "
"seventeen when his mother arranged his ascension to the Imperium. "
"Agrippina intended to rule through her son, but Nero's advisors Burrus "
"and Seneca brought the young Emperor under their control instead, "
"throwing Agrippina into a rage. In 55 AD Claudius's son Brittanicus was "
"poisoned; some historians believed Agrippina to be responsible, but "
"others say that Agrippina, furious over her son's rebellion, had begun "
"to conspire with Brittanicus to put him in Nero's place instead, "
"leading Nero to order Brittanicus killed. The following year, Agrippina "
"was forced into retirement to keep her from causing any more trouble.\n"
"\n"
"#1314Previous Page# / #1316 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1316: title
msgctxt "help/1316/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1316/1"
msgid ""
"For the first five years of his reign, Nero showed himself to be a "
"remarkably kind and generous Emperor. His advisors encouraged him to "
"indulge his own personal pleasures while they did the real business of "
"running the Empire, which kept Nero happy. He passed laws forbidding "
"gladiatorial killings, and instituted poetry competitions and athletic "
"games in their place. He also reduced taxes and gave slaves the right "
"to complain against unjust owners. His generosity was both public and "
"private; in addition to sending aid to cities victimized by natural "
"disasters, he also pardoned writers of satirical verses, who would have "
"swiftly been executed by any earlier Emperor. In sum, Nero was an "
"irresponsible and naive but essentially harmless young man.\n"
"\n"
"This began to change in AD 59, when Nero was \n"
"#1315Previous Page# / #1317 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1317: title
msgctxt "help/1317/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1317/1"
msgid ""
"twenty one. His mother Agrippina had gone insane with rage and "
"threatened Nero; in his first proven act of official murder, Nero had "
"her put to death. Shortly after that, he came under the influence of a "
"woman named Poppaea Sabina, the young wife of a Senator, who became his "
"mistress. Poppaea supplanted Burrus and Seneca as Nero's chief advisor, "
"and she was not nearly so well suited for the task. Eventually she "
"convinced Nero to have his wife Octavia (who also happened to be "
"Claudius's daughter) murdered so that Nero could marry her instead, "
"which he did in 65 AD.\n"
"\n"
"After that point, it became apparent to Nero that he could do exactly "
"as he wished, and no one could gainsay him. What he wished was to be an "
"actor and a musician, not an Emperor, for he had proved to \n"
"#1316Previous Page# / #1318 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1318: title
msgctxt "help/1318/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1318/1"
msgid ""
"have neither interest in nor talent for statesmanship. Nero began "
"acting on the stage, taking whatever roles he found interesting, and "
"also traveled all over the Empire participating in musical competitions "
"(which he always won, of course.) He also became involved in a number "
"of strange foreign cults. This behavior left the Senate and the morally "
"conservative Roman middle class aghast -- it was a serious affront to "
"the dignity and the decorum required of the ruler of a great Empire. "
"The army, too, was scandalized to see the descendent of their revered "
"Caesar behaving this way.\n"
"\n"
"In 64 AD a huge fire burned down much of Rome. Rumors whispered that "
"Nero himself had started it in order to be able to rebuild the city "
"more to his liking. The rumor wasn't true, for Nero was miles away at \n"
"#1317Previous Page# / #1319 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1319: title
msgctxt "help/1319/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1319/1"
msgid ""
"his villa during the fire (singing about the fall of Troy as he watched "
"the flames in the distance, according to legend). To deflect talk away "
"from himself, Nero blamed the fire on the new and unpopular Christian "
"religious sect, becoming the first Roman Emperor to persecute "
"Christians and thereby causing those unfortunates to regard him as the "
"Antichrist.\n"
"\n"
"When the fire died down, Nero ordered a huge (and hugely luxurious) new "
"home called the Golden Palace built for himself in the cleared area. "
"Had it been completed, it would have covered fully a third of Rome. To "
"pay for all this, Nero demanded higher and higher taxes from Rome and "
"the provinces, causing serious economic problems for the populace. "
"Combined with Nero's total lack of strong leadership, this oppression "
"led to severe unrest in parts of the \n"
"#1318Previous Page# / #1320 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1320: title
msgctxt "help/1320/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1320/1"
msgid ""
"Empire; Britain rose in revolt, as did Judaea and Gaul, and the "
"Parthians pushed into Roman lands in the east.\n"
"\n"
"By 65 AD, conspiracies to get rid of Nero had begun to form. The "
"Emperor was protected for a time by warnings from slaves of the "
"conspirators, who still remembered the rights he had given them. They "
"could not guard him forever, though. Unable to dispose of Nero by "
"subtler means, the Romans rose in open revolt in 68 AD. Legions in Gaul "
"and Spain proclaimed their commanders Emperor. Even the Praetorian "
"Guard repudiated Nero. The Senate, in an emergency meeting, declared "
"that Nero's life was forfeit, and decreed that he should be flogged to "
"death on the cross. Nero fled from Rome, then ended the Julio-Claudian "
"line of Emperors ignobly by \n"
"#1319Previous Page# / #1321 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1321: title
msgctxt "help/1321/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1321/1"
msgid ""
"cutting his own throat. The Senate, far from declaring Nero a god, "
"decreed him \"Damnatio Memoriae\" -- damned in memory -- and had all "
"mention of his reign stricken from the records. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1320Previous Page# / #1325 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1322: title
msgctxt "help/1322/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1322/1"
msgid ""
"CHAPTER V\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Bust of Vespasian\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
