#. help page 1326: title
msgctxt "help/1326/0"
msgid "The Flavian Emperors"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1326/1"
msgid ""
"Nero's death left the soldiers of the Empire without a descendant of "
"Caesar to acclaim as Emperor. Confusion and civil war were inevitable. "
"69 AD went down in history as \"The Year of Four Emperors,\" as the "
"Imperial seat changed hands with the seasons.\n"
"\n"
"The first contenders for the position were Julius Vindex, governor of "
"Gallia Lugdunensis and commander of its armies, and Sulpicius Galba, "
"likewise governor of Hispania Tarraconensis. Vindex' neighbor, governor "
"Verginius Rufus of Upper Germany, sent his armies across the Rhine and "
"crushed Vindex, whereupon Rufus's soldiers urged him to seize the "
"Principate. But Rufus resisted and instead gave his support to Galba. "
"The Senate, eager to stop the conflict, recognized Galba as Emperor. "
"Nero's activities had drained the treasury, however, and \n"
"#1325Previous Page# / #1327 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1327: title
msgctxt "help/1327/0"
msgid "The Flavian Emperors (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1327/1"
msgid ""
"Galba couldn't restore economic stability fast enough to suit the "
"populace; that, combined with his advanced age of 73, made him "
"unpopular. After only three months, he was assassinated by the "
"Praetorian Guard on the secret orders of Senator Marcus Salvius Otho.\n"
"\n"
"The Praetorians then acclaimed Otho as Emperor, but the provincial "
"armies of Upper and Lower Germany had other ideas, and proclaimed their "
"commander Aulus Vitellius Germanicus instead. Vitellius's army marched "
"on Rome and defeated Otho's troops. Otho committed suicide, and the "
"Senate acclaimed Vitellius -- not that they had a choice, since his "
"troops occupied the city. Out in the East, however, the other half of "
"the Roman Army had chosen still another \n"
"\n"
"#1326Previous Page# / #1328 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1328: title
msgctxt "help/1328/0"
msgid "The Flavian Emperors (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1328/1"
msgid ""
"Emperor: their commander, Titus Flavius Vespasianus, who had been "
"occupied in putting down a Jewish revolt in Judaea.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1327Previous Page# / #1329 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1329: title
msgctxt "help/1329/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1329/1"
msgid ""
"A NEW DYNASTY BEGINS\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Statue of Titus\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1328Previous Page# / #1330 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1330: title
msgctxt "help/1330/0"
msgid "A New Dynasty Begins"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1330/1"
msgid ""
"Instead of marching his armies on Rome, Vespasian decided to hit the "
"city in its most vulnerable spot: its stomach. Nearly all of Rome's "
"grain came from overseas, particularly Egypt's fertile Nile valley. "
"Vespasian accordingly headed south from Judaea and took Egypt, leaving "
"Rome to face the prospect of imminent starvation. Troops all over the "
"Empire immediately declared for Vespasian. Even Vitellius wanted to "
"step down in his favor, but his own troops wouldn't allow it. Vespasian "
"sat tight in Egypt and sent his right-hand man Antonius Primus with an "
"army to take Rome and dispose of Vitellius, which he did in December of "
"69 AD. Vespasian was declared Emperor in absentia, and returned to the "
"city in 70 AD.\n"
"\n"
"If Rome had been unlucky, Vespasian might have \n"
"#1329Previous Page# / #1331 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1331: title
msgctxt "help/1331/0"
msgid "A New Dynasty Begins (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1331/1"
msgid ""
"turned out to be a ruthless military despot. Instead, the city found "
"itself graced with a ruler possessed of the best Roman qualities: he "
"was honest, efficient, hardworking, and militarily gifted. By the time "
"his ten-year reign ended, he had earned himself the title \"Second "
"Founder of the Principate,\" by proving that Augustus's government could "
"succeed without a descendant of Augustus at its head. (In point of "
"fact, the Principate did significantly better without a descendant of "
"Augustus at its head.) Yet Vespasian did not attempt to disparage the "
"noble line he replaced; instead, he became the first Emperor to adopt "
"the name Caesar as a title of respect, instead of as a part of his own "
"name.\n"
"\n"
"Vespasian was the first Princeps who was not of old Roman stock. His "
"family came from the hinterlands \n"
"#1330Previous Page# / #1332 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1332: title
msgctxt "help/1332/0"
msgid "A New Dynasty Begins (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1332/1"
msgid ""
"of Italy, and his father was an eques (member of the equites class), "
"not a patrician. Vespasian, however, had earned himself admission to "
"the Senate on his own merits. The disorder in the Empire called for a "
"man of firmness, patience, and tact rather than brilliance, and "
"Vespasian proved to have all three.\n"
"\n"
"His first task was to restore Roman control over the provinces that had "
"been lost due to Nero's policies. He swiftly defeated the Gauls, and "
"his son Titus Vespasianus completed his father's work in Judaea by "
"destroying Jerusalem. The rebellious Celts and Picts in Britain were "
"pacified, temporarily at least. Having taken care of the short-term "
"emergencies, Vespasian solved the deeper problems that had caused them "
"by installing provincial governors who shared his own gifts of honesty "
"and efficiency. Although he had to \n"
"#1331Previous Page# / #1333 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1333: title
msgctxt "help/1333/0"
msgid "A New Dynasty Begins (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1333/1"
msgid ""
"raise taxes to pay off the huge debts run up by Nero, he used the money "
"wisely to restore the Empire to solvency, and to invest in public works "
"such as the Colosseum. He also established state salaries for teachers "
"of Roman and Greek oratory.\n"
"\n"
"Vespasian got along well with the Senate even though he granted them no "
"power. He treated that august body with respect and courtesy, though he "
"tolerated no nonsense from its more fractious members. He also helped "
"diversify its representation, promoting many provincial artistocrats "
"into Senate seats. That he would be deified after his rule was never in "
"doubt, although Vespasian himself appears to have been less than "
"thrilled with the honor; on his deathbed, he was quoted as saying, "
"\"Dammit, I can feel myself becoming a god.\" \n"
"\n"
"#1332Previous Page# / #1334 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1334: title
msgctxt "help/1334/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1334/1"
msgid ""
"THE SONS OF VESPASIAN\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Bust of Domitian\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1333Previous Page# / #1335 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1335: title
msgctxt "help/1335/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1335/1"
msgid ""
"One of Vespasian's most notable achievements was that he convinced the "
"Roman armies to accept a line other than that of Julius Caesar as their "
"rulers. Vespasian was determined to found a dynasty, saying \"Either my "
"sons will succeed me, or no one will.\" As it happened, both of them "
"did.\n"
"\n"
"Vespasian's eldest son and heir, Titus, was one of Rome's most beloved "
"and most unfortunate Emperors. His father carefully groomed him as his "
"heir, and had all but shared the Principate with him in the last few "
"years before his death. But Titus died of a fever only two years after "
"his ascension, during which time Vesuvius erupted, destroying the "
"cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum, and a huge fire swept through Rome. "
"If he had lived longer, he might have had time to make enemies; "
"instead, he died with the first \n"
"#1334Previous Page# / #1336 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1336: title
msgctxt "help/1336/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1336/1"
msgid ""
"bloom still on his reign, and was instantly deified.\n"
"Vespasian had never bothered to adequately prepare his second son, "
"Domitian, for the job of Emperor. In fact, he and Titus both appear to "
"have gone to lengths to keep Domitian from acquiring any power or "
"influence in Rome while they were alive. Possibly Vespasian simply "
"favored Titus so strongly that he wanted no competition for his eldest "
"son's rule, or perhaps he was aware that his youngest son was not "
"Emperor material. For whatever reason, Domitian's father had never "
"considered him a successor -- so naturally Domitian became fiercely "
"jealous of his own power when he gained the Imperial seat.\n"
"Domitian ruled as an autocrat from the very first. He \n"
"\n"
"#1335Previous Page# / #1337 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1337: title
msgctxt "help/1337/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1337/1"
msgid ""
"scorned the Senate, ignoring them most of the time and humiliating them "
"when he did deign to notice their existence. The legions, by contrast, "
"he pampered and showered with favors. Their support no doubt allowed "
"him to stay in power far longer than he would have otherwise.\n"
"\n"
" In 86 AD, five years into his reign, he decided not to await the "
"posthumous decision of the Senate and declared himself a living god. "
"Grumbling from the Senate and the citizens of Rome was met with a "
"flurry of treason trials and executions, which continued and grew more "
"common and baseless until Domitian held the entire Roman upper class in "
"a reign of terror. Publicly, Domitian decreed that Romans should be "
"held to a high standard of morality, with lapses punished severely. "
"This was \n"
"#1336Previous Page# / #1338 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1338: title
msgctxt "help/1338/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1338/1"
msgid ""
"little more than another excuse for Domitian to exercise his supreme "
"power, though; in his private life, he did as he pleased, even going so "
"far as to take one of his own nieces as a mistress.\n"
"\n"
"Yet Domitian's tyranny was mostly restricted to the city of Rome "
"itself. He selected competent governors for the provinces, who were too "
"scared of Domitian to risk misbehaving. Militarily, Domitian showed "
"little interest in acquiring new lands for Rome; his activity in this "
"sphere was restrained to putting down a rebellion in Africa, expanding "
"the province of Britain into Scotland, and struggling with recurring "
"problems in Dacia. At home, Domitian's sole positive accomplishment was "
"to promote a good deal of solid and worthwhile construction in Rome. He "
"had no financial talent, though, and caused economic \n"
"#1337Previous Page# / #1339 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1339: title
msgctxt "help/1339/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1339/1"
msgid ""
"problems by overspending on his building projects.\n"
"\n"
"The last few years of the Principate of Domitian were filled with "
"nonstop treason trials and executions that seriously depleted the "
"Senate body. Finally, the Praetorian Guard captain (with the help of "
"Domitian's wife) arranged to have the tyrant assassinated. The Senate "
"-- or what was left of it -- fell over itself in its haste to declare "
"Domitian's reign Damnatio Memoriae.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1338Previous Page# / #1345 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1340: title
msgctxt "help/1340/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1340/1"
msgid ""
"CHAPTER VI\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Statue of the Emperor Nerva\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
