#. help page 1346: title
msgctxt "help/1346/0"
msgid "The Antonine Emperors"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1346/1"
msgid ""
"Fortunately, the fall of the Flavian line did not cause the kind of "
"chaos the end of the Julio-Claudian line had. The removal of Domitian "
"appears to have been a well-coordinated act, for the Senate and the "
"Praetorian Guard elected a new Princeps before the tyrant's body was "
"cold. Their choice was the aged and conservative Senator Marcus "
"Cocceius Nerva. Nerva's first actions as Emperor were to suspend "
"Domitian's treason laws, recall all political exiles, and take an oath "
"not to kill any more Senators. His ascension to the throne marked the "
"beginning of what is usually called the \"Antonine dynasty\" of Emperors, "
"though it was not a true dynasty and was named after an Emperor who "
"ruled in the middle of the period, rather than at the beginning of it.\n"
"\n"
"Nerva accomplished two important things during his \n"
"#1345Previous Page# / #1347 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1347: title
msgctxt "help/1347/0"
msgid "The Antonines (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1347/1"
msgid ""
"brief stint as Emperor. First, he avoided being overthrown long enough "
"for things to settle down, thus preventing a civil war. Second, he "
"chose as his successor the best man he could find, instead of trying to "
"start a dynasty of his own, and thereby enabled the Golden Age of the "
"Roman Empire to come about.\n"
"\n"
"Unfortunately for historians, Rome's Golden Age is poorly documented. "
"Much of the information we have on the early Emperors comes from "
"Suetonius's \"Lives of the Caesars,\" a gossipy and fascinating history "
"that stops with Domitian's reign. Only fragmentary works of a similar "
"nature about the Golden Age still exist. It may be that few were "
"created to begin with; long periods of peace and prosperity don't make "
"for very interesting writing material.\n"
"#1346Previous Page# / #1348 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1348: title
msgctxt "help/1348/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1348/1"
msgid ""
"THE MODEL EMPEROR\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Detail of Trajan's Column\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1347Previous Page# / #1349 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1349: title
msgctxt "help/1349/0"
msgid "The Model Emperor"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1349/1"
msgid ""
"The only potential source of serious unrest in Nerva's Principate lay "
"with the legions, who didn't like the idea of being led by a Senator "
"with no military experience. Nerva's choice of successor quelled them "
"quickly, however. Marcus Ulpius Traianus, better known as Trajan, was a "
"well-known and highly respected general. Nerva adopted him as his own "
"son, the first time a Princeps had adopted an heir unrelated to him by "
"either blood or marriage. Three months later Nerva died of natural "
"causes. Though he served only two years, the Senate happily awarded him "
"godhood.\n"
"\n"
"Trajan was the first Princeps who not only wasn't Roman -- he wasn't "
"even Italian, having been born in into to a family of distinguished "
"Spanish aristocrats. He possessed virtues rare in an Emperor: modesty, \n"
"#1348Previous Page# / #1350 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1350: title
msgctxt "help/1350/0"
msgid "The Model Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1350/1"
msgid ""
"affability, and humility. Though he was clearly an autocrat, he had "
"none of Domitian's vices, and instead ruled as a benevolent and beloved "
"dictator, the ultimate Roman father figure. Trajan's personal authority "
"was never in doubt, yet he treated the Senate with courtesy and respect "
"and even managed to become good personal friends with many of its "
"members. He succeeded not only in becoming respected (a fairly easy "
"task for a man with ultimate power) but also in becoming liked, a much "
"more difficult job. Upon his succession, he repeated Nerva's oath never "
"to execute a Senator, which must have endeared him to the Senate.\n"
"\n"
"The new Emperor was well-liked in the provinces too. A provincial "
"himself, he was careful to keep the borders secure and the people "
"protected. He provided \n"
"#1349Previous Page# / #1351 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1351: title
msgctxt "help/1351/0"
msgid "The Model Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1351/1"
msgid ""
"money for provincial improvements, especially of provincial roads. And "
"he personally recalled and tried any governor he caught being abusive "
"to the locals. In external affairs, Trajan abandoned Augustus's "
"principles of limiting the Empire's size and created several new "
"provinces: Arabia Petraea, Armenia, and Assyria. He also conquered "
"troublesome Dacia, whose gold mines provided him with revenue that he "
"put to good use. Under Trajan, the Roman Empire reached its greatest "
"geographical extent.\n"
"\n"
"Trajan did a great deal of building and city improvement in Rome and "
"elsewhere, reorganized the Imperial finances, and established charities "
"for orphaned children. He regularly made large public donations and "
"threw lavish festivals, one of which lasted over one hundred days. In "
"114 AD, the Senate \n"
"#1350Previous Page# / #1352 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1352: title
msgctxt "help/1352/0"
msgid "The Model Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1352/1"
msgid ""
"granted Trajan the title of \"Optimus\" -- \"Best.\" It was one of the few "
"official honors he allowed them to bestow upon him. Although he had "
"publicly renounced the policy of Imperial deification, upon his death "
"he was made a god as a matter of course (which probably would have "
"embarrassed him, had he been around to complain).\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1351Previous Page# / #1353 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1353: title
msgctxt "help/1353/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1353/1"
msgid ""
"FRONTIER GUARDIAN\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Bust of Hadrian\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1352Previous Page# / #1354 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1354: title
msgctxt "help/1354/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1354/1"
msgid ""
"While preparing for a campaign in Mesopotamia, Trajan fell ill of a "
"fever. On his deathbed, he adopted Publius Aelius Hadrianus -- a "
"distant relative and a longtime companion -- as his son and named him "
"heir. Like his predecessor, Hadrian was a Spaniard and a general, but "
"unlike Trajan he returned to Augustus's policy of containing the growth "
"of the Empire within defensible boundaries. One of his first actions as "
"Princeps was to pull back from Trajan's conquests in the East, "
"abandoning Assyria and returning Armenia's status to that of client "
"kingdom instead of province. His retreat from Assyria caused grumbling "
"among some of the other Roman generals, but was undoubtedly a wise "
"move; the new province had not truly been conquered, merely overrun, "
"and was already gearing up for rebellion. In England and Germany he "
"built walls to help hold back barbarian \n"
"#1353Previous Page# / #1355 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1355: title
msgctxt "help/1355/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1355/1"
msgid ""
"encroachments.\n"
"\n"
"Hadrian was a versatile and liberal Emperor, equally capable in the "
"military, administrative, and political aspects of his position. He was "
"strongly influenced by Greek thought, and believed that \"The ruler "
"exists for the state, not the state for the ruler.\" His provincial "
"origins led him to view Italy and Rome as a part of a greater whole, "
"and he promoted many provincials into Senate seats. Hadrian's most "
"serious administrative change came when his removed the requirement "
"that equites must serve in the military before they could have a career "
"with the civil service; although this worked well for a while, it began "
"a separation between the military and civil arms of the government that "
"would have major consequences in later years.\n"
"\n"
"#1354Previous Page# / #1356 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1356: title
msgctxt "help/1356/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1356/1"
msgid ""
"Upon his ascension, Hadrian renewed the oath of Nerva and Trajan to "
"refrain from executing Senators, and showed respect to the Senate. Yet "
"the Senate never fully liked or trusted him. He lacked Trajan's "
"personal charisma; his foreign policy seemed weak and lacking in glory; "
"and he paid too much attention to the provinces and not enough to Rome "
"to suit them. And yet he reigned for over twenty years with no serious "
"unrest anywhere in the Empire.\n"
"\n"
"In 115 AD, Hadrian was struck with a debilitating progressive illness. "
"At that point his rule became rather tyrannical, and he began a frantic "
"search for a suitable successor. He had no children or close allies to "
"choose from; his personality had always been rather aloof, which was "
"part of the reason the Senate never warmed to him. After discarding "
"several possibilities, \n"
"#1355Previous Page# / #1357 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1357: title
msgctxt "help/1357/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1357/1"
msgid ""
"he settled on Titus Aurelius Antoninus, a Senator already over fifty "
"years old, and adopted him as son and heir. Apparently worried that his "
"first choice was nearing the end of his life, he made the Senator adopt "
"two boys of Hadrian's choosing to be heirs after Antoninus: sixteen "
"year old Marcus Aurelius Antoninus, and seven year old Lucius Verus. "
"Hadrian passed away soon after.\n"
"\n"
"TRANQUILLATAS AND CONCORDIA\n"
"\n"
"Hadrian needn't have worried about Titus Marcus Antoninus's health. The "
"new Princeps reigned for over twenty years, and according to some "
"sources became Rome's \"most beloved Emperor of all.\" The coins minted "
"in his day bore the slogans \"Tranquillitatas\" and \"Concordia\" -- "
"Tranquillity and \n"
"#1356Previous Page# / #1358 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1358: title
msgctxt "help/1358/0"
msgid "Tranquillatas and Concordia (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1358/1"
msgid ""
"Peace.\n"
"\n"
"Antoninus's first act was to pressure the Senate to deify Hadrian. The "
"Senate was reluctant -- Hadrian had not been all that popular in Rome, "
"despite his competence -- but Antoninus's impassioned speeches both "
"persuaded the Senate to make Hadrian a god of the state and also to "
"bestow the nickname \"Pius\" on Antoninus, for his devotion to his "
"adoptive father. Antoninus, like Hadrian and Trajan, was a provincial. "
"His family hailed from Gallia Narbonensis, and his wife was a Spaniard.\n"
"\n"
"Records from Antoninus's reign are scarce, but what little is available "
"is uniformly positive. The new Princeps managed to pull off a trick "
"many modern politicians would like to duplicate: he lowered taxes \n"
"#1357Previous Page# / #1359 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1359: title
msgctxt "help/1359/0"
msgid "Tranquillatas and Concordia (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1359/1"
msgid ""
"while at the same time increasing public spending. He reformed parts of "
"Rome's judicial code to promote impartiality and equity under the law. "
"He defended and maintained Rome's borders but started no wars. The "
"barbarian tribes made a few raids into Roman territory, which boded ill "
"for the future, yet presented Antoninus no serious problems.\n"
"\n"
"To secure the succession, he honored Hadrian's wishes and married his "
"daughter to Marcus Aurelius, one of the boys Hadrian had ordered him to "
"adopt, and named him heir. On his death in 161 AD, he was widely and "
"genuinely mourned, and promptly deified. As a further tribute, the "
"entire line of good emperors of Rome's Golden Age was given his name: "
"the Antonines.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1358Previous Page# / #1360 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1360: title
msgctxt "help/1360/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1360/1"
msgid ""
"THE PHILOSOPHER EMPEROR\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Equestrian statue of Marcus Aurelius\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1359Previous Page# / #1361 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1361: title
msgctxt "help/1361/0"
msgid "The Philosopher Emperor"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1361/1"
msgid ""
"Marcus Aurelius was a Princeps much like Antoninus before him: quiet, "
"peace-loving, and unwarlike. While he had Antoninus's personality, "
"however, he entirely lacked Antoninus's good fortune.\n"
"\n"
"Marcus Aurelius would probably have been much happier as a scholar and "
"philosopher than as an Emperor. Written works of his that have come "
"down through the centuries reveal him as a deeply thoughtful, noble, "
"and conscientious man, with a rather pessimistic outlook "
"(understandable, given the events of his reign.) He didn't like the job "
"Antoninus had chosen him for, but he considered it his duty to fulfill "
"the responsibilities of the position as best he could.\n"
"\n"
"Although Marcus Aurelius did a more than capable\n"
"#1360Previous Page# / #1362 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1362: title
msgctxt "help/1362/0"
msgid "The Philosopher Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1362/1"
msgid ""
"job of running the Empire, disinterest in being Princeps probably led "
"him to the occasional severe lapse in judgment. Right at the very "
"beginning, he honored Hadrian's wishes beyond the point Hadrian would "
"probably have wanted by making his adoptive brother Lucius Verus "
"co-emperor. Lucius was a foolish and self-indulgent sort, but "
"fortunately not ambitious; he was content to amuse himself in Rome "
"while his brother took care of the business of ruling. This division of "
"power set a bad precedent, however, and foreshadowed the division of "
"the Empire that would later ensue.\n"
"\n"
"Marcus had bigger problems than an irresponsible brother, however. "
"Border troubles broke out all over, and the Emperor who loved peace was "
"forced to spend more of his reign campaigning than any \n"
"#1361Previous Page# / #1363 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1363: title
msgctxt "help/1363/0"
msgid "The Philosopher Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1363/1"
msgid ""
"Emperor who preceded him. The Parthians invaded in the East; there was "
"unrest in Gaul, an uprising of Moors in Mauretania, and outright "
"rebellion in the Nile Delta. Worst of all, the Germanic tribes in the "
"Danube region united and overwhelmed the Roman legions there, then "
"rampaged through Noricum and Pannonia and all the way to Aquilaea at "
"the head of the Adriatic. The Emperor was forced to sell off Imperial "
"treasures to raise funds for defense, and drafted gladiators and slaves "
"into the army to fill manpower shortages. Eventually the Germans were "
"beaten back, but at great cost. To make matters worse, soldiers "
"returning from fighting the Parthians in the East brought a new strain "
"of plague (possibly smallpox) back with them, which ravaged Rome and "
"several other Italian cities. Some historians believe that the heavy "
"population losses caused by this \n"
"#1362Previous Page# / #1364 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1364: title
msgctxt "help/1364/0"
msgid "The Philosopher Emperor (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1364/1"
msgid ""
"plague contributed to Rome's decline.\n"
"\n"
"In 177 AD a false rumor spread that Marcus Aurelius had been killed "
"while campaigning. When this word got out, another general made a quick "
"and ill-advised attempt to name himself Emperor. Although the "
"usurpation failed, it spooked Marcus Aurelius into making a second "
"serious lapse of judgment: he named his sixteen year old son, Lucius "
"Aelius Aurelius Commodus, co-emperor. When he died for real three years "
"later (on campaign), he was deified and Commodus was proclaimed Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1363Previous Page# / #1365 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1365: title
msgctxt "help/1365/0"
msgid "."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1365/1"
msgid ""
"LAST OF THE ANTONINES\n"
"\n"
"Picture: Bust showing Commodus as Hercules\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1364Previous Page# / #1366 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1366: title
msgctxt "help/1366/0"
msgid "Last of the Antonines"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1366/1"
msgid ""
"Marcus Aurelius, wise in nearly all other matters, made a serious error "
"when he abandoned the practice of adopting a well-qualified "
"non-relative to succeed him and instead chose own son Commodus. "
"Commodus did not want to be Emperor, was not suited to the task, and "
"unlike his father had no strong sense of duty to make him even attempt "
"to govern well. He was further proof (as if any were needed after Nero) "
"that teenagers shouldn't be made Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"While Nero fancied himself a performer, Commodus's ambition was to be a "
"sports superstar. He sponsored athletic games in which he competed (and "
"always won). He even went so far as to fight as a gladiator in the "
"Arena, like a common slave. Though he fancied himself the living "
"embodiment of Hercules, he was in \n"
"#1365Previous Page# / #1367 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1367: title
msgctxt "help/1367/0"
msgid "Last of the Antonines (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1367/1"
msgid ""
"truth both cowardly and cruel. He scorned, humiliated, and ignored the "
"Senate, keeping them in line by the use of paid informants and treason "
"trials. The Praetorian Guard he handled through bribery.\n"
"\n"
"Commodus was lucky enough to have competent generals watching the "
"borders for him, since he himself paid no attention to Rome's security. "
"He created internal troubles by levying heavy taxes to pay for his own "
"sport and by treating individuals with great cruelty. Eventually, drunk "
"on his own power, he followed in the footsteps of the last Roman tyrant "
"and declared himself a living god. Perhaps he, like Domitian, realized "
"that his chances for posthumous deification were slim. Any worship he "
"was likely to get would have to be extorted out of \n"
"#1366Previous Page# / #1368 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1368: title
msgctxt "help/1368/0"
msgid "Last of the Antonines (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1368/1"
msgid ""
"the citizens while he lived.\n"
"\n"
"The reign of Commodus ended much the same way Domitian's had: in "
"betrayal by those closest to him. The Praetorian Guard captain and "
"Commodus's favorite mistress united and bribed Commodus's sparring "
"partner, who strangled the Emperor as they wrestled. The Senate wasted "
"no time declaring Commodus's reign Damnatio Memoriae.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1367Previous Page# / #1370 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

