msgctxt "help/1370/0"
msgid "@1369"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1370/1"
msgid "@1369"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1371: title
msgctxt "help/1371/0"
msgid "The Long Decline"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1371/1"
msgid ""
"The death of Commodus in 192 set off another year of many Emperors, as "
"various generals resorted to civil war in an attempt to claim Imperial "
"authority. Unfortunately, this time there was no Vespasian waiting at "
"the end of it. Instead, Septimus Severus claimed the Principate in 193. "
"Septimus believed that an Emperor's authority rested entirely with the "
"military; accordingly, he lavished gifts on the legions and paid no "
"attention whatsoever to the Senate or to the traditions of Roman "
"government.\n"
"\n"
"Septimus Severus founded an undistinguished dynasty, the Severan "
"Emperors, who proved totally incapable of restoring sensible order to "
"the Empire. Throughout their fifty-year reign, they increased the power "
"of the legions over the civilian government, a habit that led to a "
"predictable conclusion: in 235, \n"
"#1370Previous Page# / #1372 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1372: title
msgctxt "help/1372/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1372/1"
msgid ""
"Severus Alexander, last of the line, was murdered by his own troops "
"when he failed to make good on a promised bribe. For the next "
"half-century, the Principate was held by fifteen different \"Emperors,\" "
"each a serving a handful of years before being killed by some other "
"general with ambition. The only one of the lot who appeared to have any "
"aptitude for true statesmanship was the Emperor Aurelian, ruler from "
"270 to 275 AD, who brought a fragile peace to the Empire (and built a "
"formidable wall around Rome) before being murdered by some of his own "
"officers. Aurelian was the first Emperor to claim to rule by the "
"authority of God (in his case, the Unconquerable Sun); thus, his reign "
"is often considered the time when the Principate became the \"Dominate,\" "
"from Aurelian's title of \"Dominus et Deus.\" \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1371Previous Page# / #1373 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1373: title
msgctxt "help/1373/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1373/1"
msgid ""
"Order was briefly restored by the ascension of the Emperor Diocletian "
"in 284 AD, but it required a complete overhaul of the government to "
"accomplish. Diocletian created a structure called the Tetrarchy. On top "
"of the stack were two co-Emperors, the Augusti, with two junior \"vice "
"Emperors,\" called Caesars. Each of the four had well-defined spheres of "
"influence. Diocletian managed to make this unsteady arrangement hold "
"together for about twenty years. During that time he made another "
"innovation, brought on by a declining population and manpower shortage "
"in the Empire. Diocletian made it a law that sons had to take up the "
"professions of their fathers: sons of soldiers had to remain soldiers, "
"and sons of farmers had to remain farmers. In addition, farmers were "
"bound to the land they tilled and could not move elsewhere without the "
"Emperor's permission. \n"
"#1372Previous Page# / #1374 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1374: title
msgctxt "help/1374/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1374/1"
msgid ""
"In these rules can clearly be seen the start of the feudal system.\n"
"\n"
"Diocletian's Tetrarchy did not outlast his death, to no one's real "
"surprise, and twenty years of unrest followed. In AD 324, the Emperor "
"Constantine rose to power. Constantine is famous for making two "
"striking changes in the Roman Empire that would have far-reaching "
"effects. First, he commissioned the building of a \"New Rome\" on the "
"site of the ancient city of Byzantium. This city, christened "
"Constantinople, rapidly became the center of activity in the East. "
"Second, Constantine was the first Emperor to convert to Christianity, "
"and he made it the state religion of the Empire. Constantine was "
"searching for a strong moral force to help bring order back to the "
"increasingly chaotic empire, and considered several \n"
"#1373Previous Page# / #1375 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1375: title
msgctxt "help/1375/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1375/1"
msgid ""
"religions (particularly Mithraism) before settling on Christianity. "
"Although his search was spurred by practicalities, his conversion was "
"probably sincere, for Constantine was a deeply religious individual.\n"
"\n"
"The creation of Constantinople began the separation of the Eastern and "
"Western halves of the Empire. Much of the administration and business "
"that was formally conducted in Rome moved to the new city. In 364 AD "
"the Emperor Valentinian informally divided the Empire, taking the West "
"for himself and giving the East to his brother Valens. About thirty "
"years later, the Emperor Honorious made the division official, taking "
"the West for himself and giving the East to Emperor Arcadius. Honorious "
"also moved his capital from Rome to more easily-defended Ravenna. This "
"accelerated a decline in Rome's status that had \n"
"#1374Previous Page# / #1376 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1376: title
msgctxt "help/1376/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1376/1"
msgid ""
"begun during the Tetrarchy, when none of the Tetrarchs had chosen to "
"use Rome as their seat of power. By the start of the fifth century AD, "
"Rome had degenerated so far that Alaric, chief of the Germanic Visigoth "
"tribe, was able to invade and sack the city for three days without "
"meeting significant resistance. Alaric, a recent convert to "
"Christianity, plundered the pagan temples but left the churches alone.\n"
"\n"
"In 406 AD, Attila's Huns began sweeping into Europe from Asia, driving "
"many European tribes west and south. The provinces which bordered on "
"German lands began to experience wave after wave of invasions as "
"Germanic tribes, caught between a rock and a hard place, chose the "
"lesser of two bad choices and moved in on Rome. The Emperor recalled "
"his \n"
"#1375Previous Page# / #1377 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1377: title
msgctxt "help/1377/0"
msgid "The Long Decline (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1377/1"
msgid ""
"troops from the farther provinces to protect the more central areas, "
"abandoning Britain to the Picts and Celts, who were promptly invaded in "
"their turn by the northern Germanic Angles, Saxons, and Jutes. Spain "
"became independent, and North Africa fell to the Vandals. By 450 AD or "
"so, the Roman Empire was essentially dead, with numerous Germanic "
"chieftains squabbling over who would get first crack at the corpse. The "
"winner of that argument was Odoacer, leader of a German mercenary army, "
"who invaded Rome, deposed its last \"Emperor\" (the boy Romulus "
"Augustulus), and seated himself in the city as King of Italy. Though "
"the Eastern half of the Empire would survive as the Byzantine Empire, "
"with Constantinople its heart city, there was nothing left of the "
"Western Empire but a ragtag collection of barbarian kingdoms.\n"
"\n"
"#1376Previous Page# / #1378 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1378: title
msgctxt "help/1378/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1378/1"
msgid ""
"The question of the decline of the Western Empire is a complicated one, "
"with many possible answers. But whatever theory is proposed to explain "
"it must also explain why a similar decline did not take place in the "
"Eastern Empire, which endured for another thousand years. Edward "
"Gibbon, one of the first modern historians to write about Rome, "
"proposed that the growth of Christianity played a major part, but this "
"makes little sense when one considers that the Byzantine Empire was "
"manifestly Christian and survived perfectly well. The more likely "
"explanation is one that has more to do with economics than religion.\n"
"\n"
"The Pax Romana (Peace of Rome) the Empire exerted over much of the "
"Mediterranean at its height led to an increase in commerce and a "
"growing wealth for \n"
"#1377Previous Page# / #1379 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1379: title
msgctxt "help/1379/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1379/1"
msgid ""
"the people living under its shield. Prosperity, combined with the "
"breeding of more productive animal and plant strains led to increased "
"food production, while Roman sanitation reduced death from illness. "
"Overall, the population of the Roman Empire grew at a steady rate under "
"the Principate. Eventually, it seems that the population got too large "
"to be supported by farmers using primitive farming methods. "
"Over-farming also led to impoverished soil and reduced crop yields. To "
"a certain extent, the Romans became victims of their own success.\n"
"\n"
"With the land producing less food, tax revenues were reduced, leading "
"to a reduced defense ability for the Empire. That led to more barbarian "
"raids across the borders, which ruined farm lands and disrupted trade, "
"reducing revenue still further. Then \n"
"#1378Previous Page# / #1380 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1380: title
msgctxt "help/1380/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1380/1"
msgid ""
"overcrowding and food shortages combined to bring on widespread plague, "
"which reduced the available labor pool and resulted in even less "
"revenue. Soon the Empire was an Empire in name only. The cities still "
"remained responsive to Imperial authority, but the rural areas, where "
"ninety percent of the Empire's population lived, developed into "
"numerous mini-kingdoms, with large landowners maintaining small private "
"armies and granting protection to the people who farmed their lands. "
"The Emperors found it difficult or impossible to collect taxes from "
"these self-sufficient enclaves, who felt little loyalty to a government "
"unable to offer them any protection.\n"
"\n"
"At the same time that Rome's military capability was diminishing, that "
"of her barbarian enemies was growing. Rome began with a significant \n"
"\n"
"#1379Previous Page# / #1381 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1381: title
msgctxt "help/1381/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1381/1"
msgid ""
"technological edge over the tribes she conquered, but as time went on "
"Roman development and application of new technologies all but stopped. "
"The Romans were great engineers, but they had no interest in basic "
"research which might have led to new discoveries to let them maintain "
"their defensive edge. During the entire lifespan of the Empire, "
"virtually no new technology was introduced. The barbarian tribes they "
"fought learned from the Romans until their own military technology "
"equaled or surpassed that of the Empire.\n"
"\n"
"The nature of the Roman Army also changed significantly. Once it became "
"possible for a young man to have a successful career without entering "
"the legions, most Roman and Italian men didn't bother to serve a term "
"with the army. As a result, it became \n"
"#1380Previous Page# / #1382 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1382: title
msgctxt "help/1382/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1382/1"
msgid ""
"more and more provincial, with less loyalty toward Rome and Italy and "
"less willingness to fight for her. Eventually, the Roman Army was "
"composed almost entirely of hired barbarian troops. By then, Rome was "
"locked into a vicious downward spiral; the less she could defend "
"herself, the more damage was done by raids and invasions, which reduced "
"her ability to defend herself further yet. Finally, the Western Empire "
"simply disintegrated.\n"
"\n"
"Why didn't the Eastern Empire suffer a similar fate? First, the Eastern "
"Empire had always been richer than the Western Empire. Much of her "
"income came from trade with distant lands, instead of from agriculture, "
"and therefore Eastern lands did not suffer from over-farming. Second, "
"the Eastern Empire had borders which could be more easily defended with "
"fewer \n"
"#1381Previous Page# / #1383 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1383: title
msgctxt "help/1383/0"
msgid "Why Did Rome Fall? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1383/1"
msgid ""
"troops; the Sahara Desert to the southwest, the Arabian desert to the "
"southeast, and the Black Sea to the north all provided major defensive "
"barriers. Nearly all of the Eastern Empire's military trouble came from "
"the Persians to the East, and from no other direction. The Western "
"Empire, in contrast, was wide open along a northern border that could "
"never be completely defended. With sufficient money to preserve her "
"security, the Eastern Empire never entered into the downward spiral "
"that claimed the West.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1382Previous Page# / #1390 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

