#. help page 1391: title
msgctxt "help/1391/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans?"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1391/1"
msgid ""
"Modern historians have often have a seemingly irresistible urge to "
"classify the Roman Empire as either a \"good\" or a \"bad\" thing. Some "
"interpretations say that the creation of the Empire replaced rule by "
"the people (the Republic) with rule by a single man, and consider it a "
"triumph for tyranny. This is far too narrow a view to stand up under "
"close examination.\n"
"\n"
"For one thing, the Republic was hardly a model of freedom and "
"enlightened equality. Power was concentrated in the hands of a very few "
"members of society, and by the end of the Republic the controlling "
"class was totally closed to those not born in it and hostile to any "
"changes that threatened its supremacy. In truth, the Senate of the late "
"Roman Republic was more tyrannical and self-serving than \n"
"#1390Previous Page# / #1392 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1392: title
msgctxt "help/1392/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1392/1"
msgid ""
"the majority of Rome's Emperors under the Principate created by "
"Augustus. Proof of this can be seen in how little interest there was in "
"restoring the rule of the Republic after its fall. Autocratic "
"government by one man proved the lesser of two evils in that "
"circumstance, for the one man was less corrupt than the entire body he "
"replaced. Although the worst Roman Emperors were more destructive than "
"the Senate at its lowest point, in general they produced peace and "
"prosperity completely beyond the capability of Republican Rome.\n"
"\n"
"The society of the Romans has also come under close scrutiny in modern "
"times. The Romans were guilty of a number of practices widely regarded "
"as evil by today's standards: widespread slavery, blood sports, and the "
"conquest, exploitation, and subjugation of \n"
"#1391Previous Page# / #1393 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1393: title
msgctxt "help/1393/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1393/1"
msgid ""
"non-Roman peoples. Judging Roman society by today's morals is not a "
"particularly fair thing to do, however. Nothing the Romans did was "
"unique to their society at the time; slavery was universally practiced, "
"and the conquest and subjugation of other peoples was what was expected "
"of any powerful civilization. The Romans can't really be faulted for "
"simply doing a better and more efficient job of it than anyone else in "
"the ancient world. This doesn't excuse the brutality and cruelty which "
"the provincials often experienced, of course, but as a rule such "
"behavior was instigated by individuals (especially corrupt governors) "
"rather than by the Roman system as a whole.\n"
"\n"
"At the same time, the Romans avoided several moral pitfalls other "
"civilizations of the time embraced. \n"
"#1392Previous Page# / #1394 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1394: title
msgctxt "help/1394/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1394/1"
msgid ""
"Carthage, for example, regularly sacrificed children to the gods, while "
"the Celtic Druids sacrificed adults. Roman religion was, by contrast, "
"astonishingly open-minded, tolerant, and bloodless. The Druidic "
"religion was one of only two which the Romans proscribed: Druidism was "
"outlawed because it called for human sacrifice, and Christianity was "
"outlawed because members of the sect refused to pay even the minimal "
"required homage to the gods of the Roman state. The Jews were "
"occasionally persecuted, but more for their stubborn and violent "
"resistance to Roman rule than for their religious practices. Otherwise, "
"as long as respect was paid to the gods of the Roman state, the Romans "
"did not care what religion residents of the Empire practiced.\n"
"\n"
"They also had little interest in interfering with local \n"
"#1393Previous Page# / #1395 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1395: title
msgctxt "help/1395/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1395/1"
msgid ""
"customs in areas that did not affect Rome or Rome's interests. "
"Romanization was a process that occurred incidentally, as a side effect "
"of Roman conquest, rather than being something that was forced on the "
"peoples the Romans conquered. Many of the conquered peoples eventually "
"came to embrace Romanization (and its good roads, sanitation, and "
"security) with open arms. The provincial elite in particular were happy "
"to take advantage of the opportunities presented to them by "
"participation in the Roman system of government. Most provinces "
"retained a large degree of self-rule as part of the Empire; although "
"the governor could intervene at any time, most didn't see the need. The "
"remarkably small amount of internal unrest in the Empire during the "
"years of the Principate (except when provoked by tyrannical Emperors) "
"would seem to indicate that \n"
"#1394Previous Page# / #1396 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1396: title
msgctxt "help/1396/0"
msgid "Who Were the Romans? (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1396/1"
msgid ""
"the majority of provincials were satisfied with the Roman order.\n"
"\n"
"Regardless of one's opinion of the Roman Empire as a whole, it is "
"impossible to deny the degree to which the civilization of the Romans "
"has affected modern Western civilization. In our language, in our "
"political thought, in our architecture, the echoes of the Roman Empire "
"can still be heard. Will the influence of our own civilization be so "
"clearly felt two thousand years from now?\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
