#. help page 1981: title
msgctxt "help/1981/0"
msgid "The Legend of Rome"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1981/1"
msgid ""
"It begins with Aeneas, son of Aphrodite, hero of fallen Troy. When his "
"home was destroyed by the Greeks, Aenaes fled the ruins carrying the "
"lares and penates -- the gods of city and house. He traveled long and "
"far and had many heroic adventures as he searched for a new home. Dido, "
"queen of mighty Carthage, gave her love to Aeneas, but the restless "
"hero was driven to wander; on his departure, the stricken queen took "
"her own life. Thus began the long hatred between Carthage and the "
"descendants of Aeneas.\n"
"\n"
"Finally, Aeneas and his followers came to the land of Latium, ruled by "
"King Latinus. Latium was at war with her neighbors; Aeneas lent his "
"strength to King Latinus and slew the enemy king. In gratitude, Latinus "
"gave his daughter to Aeneas. Their children became the Latin people, "
"and the Trojan gods \n"
"#1980Previous Page# / #1982 Next Page#\n"
"#1202Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1982: title
msgctxt "help/1982/0"
msgid "The Legend of Rome (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1982/1"
msgid ""
"accepted the new land as their own, and blessed it. There in Latium, "
"Aeneas's first-born son founded the mighty city of Alba Longa.\n"
"\n"
"Thirteen generations later, Aeneas's descendant Numitor, king of Alba "
"Longa, was deposed and exiled by his wicked brother Amulius, who "
"claimed the throne. Amulius slew all the children of Numitor except for "
"his daughter, Rhea Silva, whom he sent to become a Vestal Virgin, never "
"to continue the line of Numitor. The god Mars saw what had been done, "
"and took Rhea Silva for his own, and she bore him twin sons. Amulius, "
"enraged, imprisoned Rhea Silva and set her children adrift in a basket "
"on the river Tiber.\n"
"\n"
"But the boys were favored by the gods, who sent a woodpecker to feed "
"them, and a she-wolf to suckle \n"
"#1981Previous Page# / #1983 Next Page#\n"
"#1202Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1983: title
msgctxt "help/1983/0"
msgid "The Legend of Rome (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1983/1"
msgid ""
"them. After a time, the children were rescued by a humble shepherd, who "
"took them in and raised them as his own. The twins, called Romulus and "
"Remus, grew strong and soon became the leaders of the shepherd folk. It "
"was there that their grandfather, aged Numitor, found them and taught "
"them their true destiny. Romulus and Remus rose and overthrew the cruel "
"Amulius, and restored Numitor to the throne of Alba Longa. They then "
"left the city of their grandfather in search of a place of their own.\n"
"\n"
"Returning to the site on the shore of the river where their basket had "
"come to rest, they declared they would build a great city on the spot. "
"But the twins could not agree on whose name the city should bear. "
"Unable to settle the argument, they laid the matter \n"
"#1982Previous Page# / #1984 Next Page#\n"
"#1202Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1984: title
msgctxt "help/1984/0"
msgid "The Legend of Rome (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1984/1"
msgid ""
"before the gods and waited for an omen. As they waited, Remus saw a "
"flight of six eagles in the sky. In the next moment, Romulus saw a "
"flight of twelve eagles. Remus, having seen the eagles first, declared "
"the city his, but Romulus, seeing that his twelve eagles were stronger "
"than the six of Remus, claimed the right to found the city. A bitter "
"fight arose, and Romulus slew Remus.\n"
"\n"
"Thus did the new city become Rome, city of Romulus, and each of "
"Romulus's twelve eagles gave one hundred years of life and greatness to "
"the city.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1983Previous Page#\n"
"#1202Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1985: title
msgctxt "help/1985/0"
msgid "The Age of the Roman Empire"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1985/1"
msgid ""
"The historian Livy gave the date of Rome's founding by Romulus as 753 "
"BC -- on the twenty first of April, to be exact. Unfortunately, the "
"legendary Romulus has about as much authenticity as the legendary King "
"Arthur, so this date is probably incorrect. Current archaeological data "
"seem to indicate that the earliest settlements in Rome (by Latin "
"shepherds) instead happened around 950 BC. On the other hand, "
"archaeology agrees with Livy on the location of the founding as the "
"Palatine hill. In addition, although the settlement of Rome's hills was "
"probably a gradual, organic process, the founding of any city in Italy "
"in that era was a highly ritualized political and religious event "
"performed by a single male priest-leader, so it is not impossible that "
"some kind of historical Romulus did exist.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1991Next Page#\n"
"#1204Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

