#. help page 1991: title
msgctxt "help/1991/0"
msgid "The Age of the Empire (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1991/1"
msgid ""
"The \"Fall of the Roman Empire\" is often dated to 476 AD, with the "
"deposing of the last Roman Emperor Romulus Augustulus by the Germanic "
"chieftain Odoacer. When combined with Livy's starting date, this gives "
"the Roman Empire a lifespan of 1229 years, which is nicely in agreement "
"with the 1200 years prophesied by Romulus's twelve eagles. As we've "
"already seen, though, the 753 BC date is highly questionable, and the "
"date of 476 AD for the \"Fall\" isn't much better. Although this date is "
"historically accurate, the Roman Empire can't be said to have been "
"destroyed in any single year. The Empire did not so much fall as "
"gradually crumble away, and this degeneration affected only the Western "
"half of the Empire. The eastern territories under Roman dominion "
"remained unified as the Byzantine Empire, and lasted another thousand "
"years.\n"
"\n"
"#1990Previous Page# / #1992 Next Page#\n"
"#1204Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1992: title
msgctxt "help/1992/0"
msgid "The Age of the Empire (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1992/1"
msgid ""
"Another important point to remember is that, however long it lasted, "
"the \"Roman Empire\" only became such after it was middle-aged. The "
"civilization of Rome began as an Etruscan monarchy, developed into a "
"Republic, and then finally metamorphosed into an Empire around 27 BC, "
"seven hundred years after the city's legendary founding and nine "
"hundred after its historical founding. If one accepts 476 AD as a "
"convenient end point for the Empire, its lifespan was just over five "
"hundred years. And out of that time span, the classic Roman Empire as "
"most people think of it existed for only the first two hundred years, "
"give or take a few decades.\n"
"\n"
"This period of time, from the rise of Gaius Julius Caesar (circa 46 BC) "
"to the death of Commodus (192 AD), shows the Empire at its height. "
"These years saw \n"
"#1991Previous Page# / #1993 Next Page#\n"
"#1204Return to Main History#"
msgstr ""

#. help page 1993: title
msgctxt "help/1993/0"
msgid "The Age of the Empire (Cont.)"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "help/1993/1"
msgid ""
" Rome's best Emperors, men such as Augustus, Vespasian, and Trajan. It "
"also saw some of Rome's worst -- the tyrants Caligula, Nero, and "
"Domitian.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
